/* Course scope: uploaded Chemistry Guide, first assessment 2025. Teaching prose and practice are original. */
const COURSE = [{"id":"S1.1","name":"Particulate nature of matter","group":"Structure 1","sections":[{"id":"S1.1.1","title":"Elements, compounds and mixtures","hl":false,"summary":"Matter is classified according to its chemical composition.","objectives":["An element cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical means.","A compound contains atoms of different elements chemically bonded in a fixed ratio.","A mixture contains more than one element or compound in a variable ratio without chemical bonding.","Homogeneous mixtures are uniform throughout; heterogeneous mixtures are non-uniform.","Separate mixtures through solvation, filtration, recrystallization, evaporation, distillation or chromatography."],"explain":["Matter is grouped by what particles it contains and whether those particles are chemically bonded. **An element** contains only one kind of atom, **a compound** contains different elements bonded in a fixed ratio, and **a mixture** contains substances that are physically combined rather than chemically joined.","This difference tells you how substances can be separated. A compound requires a chemical change to separate its elements; a mixture can be separated by exploiting differences in properties such as particle size, solubility or boiling point. A **homogeneous** mixture has uniform composition; a **heterogeneous** mixture does not."],"steps":["Ask whether more than one substance is present. If no, the sample is a pure substance.","If pure, ask whether it contains one element or chemically bonded different elements.","If mixed, identify a physical property that differs: filtration for insoluble solids, evaporation or recrystallization for a dissolved solid, distillation for different boiling points, or chromatography for different attractions."],"example":{"q":"A sample contains sand, dissolved salt and water. How could you obtain dry salt?","steps":["Filter the sample: insoluble sand stays on the filter paper.","The filtrate is salt solution. Evaporate some water to obtain crystals, or use careful crystallization to recover the solid.","Dry the separated crystals."],"answer":"Filtration followed by evaporation / recrystallization."},"mistake":"Do not call salt water a compound. Its composition can vary, and its components are not chemically bonded to one another.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"classification","title":"Elements, compounds and mixtures","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":39,"guidePage":33},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.1.2","title":"Kinetic molecular theory","hl":false,"summary":"A particulate model explains solids, liquids, gases and changes of state.","objectives":["Solid particles occupy relatively fixed positions; liquid particles move past each other; gas particles move freely.","Know melting, freezing, evaporation, boiling, condensation, sublimation and deposition.","Include the state symbols (s), (l), (g) and (aq) in equations.","During changes of state, distinguish energy changes from temperature changes."],"explain":["The **kinetic molecular theory** imagines matter as moving particles. In a solid, particles vibrate in relatively fixed positions; in a liquid, particles remain close but flow around one another; in a gas, particles are widely separated and move freely.","A change of state does **not** change the identity of the substance. Instead, the arrangement, spacing and movement of particles change. Melting, vaporization and sublimation require energy input to overcome attractions; freezing, condensation and deposition release energy as stronger attractions develop."],"steps":["Identify the initial and final physical states.","Name the process: solid→liquid melting; liquid→gas vaporization; gas→liquid condensation; gas→solid deposition.","Explain in particle terms how separation and movement change, not that the particles themselves grow or shrink."],"example":{"q":"Why does liquid water require heating to become water vapour?","steps":["Water molecules in a liquid are close enough for substantial attractions between them.","Energy is needed to overcome enough of those intermolecular attractions for molecules to separate.","The molecules become far more separated in the gas; they remain H₂O molecules."],"answer":"Vaporization requires energy to overcome intermolecular attractions."},"mistake":"Boiling does not break the O–H covalent bonds within water molecules.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"states","title":"Kinetic molecular theory","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":39,"guidePage":33},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.1.3","title":"Temperature and particle energy","hl":false,"summary":"Kelvin temperature is a measure of average particle kinetic energy.","objectives":["Kelvin (K) is the SI temperature unit.","A 1 K interval equals a 1 °C interval.","Convert using T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15.","Interpret temperature plateaus on heating and cooling curves as changes of state."],"explain":["Temperature is related to the **average kinetic energy** of particles. The kelvin scale begins at absolute zero; a temperature change of 1 K is the same size as a change of 1 °C. For calculations use **T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15**.","When a pure substance undergoes a change of state at constant pressure, it can absorb or release energy while its temperature stays constant. During a heating-curve plateau, the supplied energy primarily changes particle arrangement and intermolecular potential energy rather than increasing average kinetic energy."],"steps":["Convert °C to K by adding 273.15; subtract 273.15 for K to °C.","On a heating curve, sloping sections show temperature rising within one state.","Flat sections show a phase change; label the states and the relevant change."],"example":{"q":"Convert 25.0 °C to kelvin. What happens to temperature during melting of a pure substance at constant pressure?","steps":["Use T = t + 273.15.","T = 25.0 + 273.15 = 298.15 K.","Temperature stays constant at the melting point until the phase change is complete."],"answer":"298.15 K; a melting plateau occurs."},"mistake":"Never insert Celsius temperatures directly into an ideal-gas calculation.","formula":"T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15","visual":{"kind":"heating","title":"Temperature and particle energy","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":39,"guidePage":33},"group":"Structure 1"}]},{"id":"S1.2","name":"The nuclear atom","group":"Structure 1","sections":[{"id":"S1.2.1","title":"Nuclear structure and notation","hl":false,"summary":"A dense, positively charged nucleus contains protons and neutrons; electrons surround it.","objectives":["Atomic number Z = number of protons.","Mass number A = protons + neutrons.","Neutrons = A − Z.","For a neutral atom electrons = Z; for a positive ion subtract its charge, for a negative ion add charge magnitude.","Know charges and relative masses of protons, neutrons and electrons; the electron mass is negligible."],"explain":["The atom has a tiny, dense, positively charged **nucleus** that contains protons and neutrons. Negatively charged electrons occupy the surrounding region. Most of the atom’s mass is concentrated in the nucleus.","The **atomic number Z** counts protons and identifies the element; the **mass number A** counts protons plus neutrons. Ion formation changes the number of electrons, not the number of protons in the nucleus. The nuclear symbol is written ᴬ_ZX."],"steps":["Read Z to find the number of protons.","Calculate neutrons = A − Z.","A neutral atom has Z electrons. Subtract for a positive ion and add for a negative ion."],"example":{"q":"Find the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in ²⁷₁₃Al³⁺.","steps":["Protons = Z = 13.","Neutrons = A − Z = 27 − 13 = 14.","A 3+ ion has lost three electrons, so electrons = 13 − 3 = 10."],"answer":"13 protons, 14 neutrons and 10 electrons."},"mistake":"The charge of an ion changes electron count; it does not change the atomic number.","formula":"n(neutrons) = A − Z","visual":{"kind":"atom","title":"Nuclear structure and notation","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":40,"guidePage":34},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.2.2","title":"Isotopes and average atomic mass","hl":false,"summary":"Isotopes have the same proton number and different neutron numbers.","objectives":["Different isotopes can have different physical properties.","Calculate weighted relative atomic mass using isotopic abundances.","Percentage abundances must total 100%.","You do not need to memorize specific named isotopes."],"explain":["**Isotopes** are atoms of the same element with equal proton number but different neutron numbers. They occupy the same place in the periodic table because their identity is fixed by proton number. Their masses differ, which can affect physical properties.","The relative atomic mass shown in the periodic table is usually not a whole number because it is a **weighted average** over naturally occurring isotopes. A more abundant isotope contributes more to the average than a rare one."],"steps":["Convert isotope percentages to fractions of 1.","Multiply each isotope mass by its fractional abundance.","Add the contributions and check that the result lies between the smallest and largest isotope masses."],"example":{"q":"An element has isotopes of masses 35 (75%) and 37 (25%). Find its relative atomic mass.","steps":["Convert: 75% → 0.75 and 25% → 0.25.","Calculate (35 × 0.75) + (37 × 0.25).","Add 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.50."],"answer":"Aᵣ = 35.50 (no units)."},"mistake":"Do not simply average isotope masses unless they are equally abundant.","formula":"Aᵣ = Σ(isotope mass × fractional abundance)","visual":{"kind":"isotopes","title":"Isotopes and average atomic mass","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":40,"guidePage":34},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.2.3","title":"Mass spectra","hl":true,"summary":"Mass spectra reveal isotopic masses and relative abundances.","objectives":["Use mass-to-charge peaks to identify isotopes when the ion charge is known.","Use relative peak intensities for isotope abundance.","Compute weighted average atomic mass from isotope data.","Instrument operating details are not assessed."],"explain":["A **mass spectrum** shows peaks associated with ions of different mass-to-charge ratio (m/z). For singly charged atomic ions, their m/z values identify the isotopes. Peak height or area gives information about **relative abundance**.","You can therefore read a spectrum and calculate the weighted average atomic mass from the relative intensities. The syllabus requires interpretation, not memorization of the mass spectrometer’s hardware or operating stages."],"steps":["Identify the isotopic peaks from the horizontal m/z axis.","Read the peak intensities; divide by their sum to obtain fractional abundance.","Calculate Σ(isotopic mass × fraction)."],"example":{"q":"A spectrum has singly charged peaks at m/z 10 (20 units) and 11 (80 units). Estimate Aᵣ.","steps":["Total intensity = 20 + 80 = 100.","Fractions: 0.20 and 0.80.","Aᵣ = 10 × 0.20 + 11 × 0.80 = 10.8."],"answer":"Estimated relative atomic mass = 10.8."},"mistake":"The tallest peak is the most abundant isotope, not necessarily the isotope with the greatest mass.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"spectrum","title":"Mass spectra","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":40,"guidePage":34},"group":"Structure 1"}]},{"id":"S1.3","name":"Electron configurations","group":"Structure 1","sections":[{"id":"S1.3.1","title":"Emission spectra","hl":false,"summary":"Excited atoms emit photons when electrons move to lower-energy states.","objectives":["A line spectrum consists of discrete wavelengths, unlike a continuous spectrum.","Shorter wavelength corresponds to higher frequency and photon energy.","Explain the relationship between colour, wavelength, frequency and energy."],"explain":["When an atom absorbs energy, an electron can move into an **excited state**. When it drops to a lower energy level, a photon is emitted with an energy equal to the difference between those levels.","Light has wavelength λ and frequency f, related by **c = λf**. Photon energy is **E = hf**. Therefore shorter wavelength means higher frequency and greater photon energy. A **continuous spectrum** contains an unbroken range of wavelengths; a line spectrum has discrete wavelengths."],"steps":["Identify whether light is emitted (downward electron transition) or absorbed (upward transition).","Use c = λf to change wavelength into frequency.","Use E = hf to relate frequency to the energy difference."],"example":{"q":"Which photon has more energy: one of wavelength 400 nm or one of 700 nm?","steps":["For electromagnetic radiation in vacuum, frequency f = c/λ.","400 nm is the shorter wavelength, hence has the larger frequency.","Since E = hf, 400 nm corresponds to the higher-energy photon."],"answer":"The 400 nm photon has more energy."},"mistake":"A higher-energy photon has a shorter, not longer, wavelength.","formula":"E = hf ; c = λf","visual":{"kind":"photon","title":"Emission spectra","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":40,"guidePage":34},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.3.2","title":"Hydrogen line emission spectrum","hl":false,"summary":"Distinct spectral lines indicate quantized electron energies.","objectives":["Identify the transitions that terminate at n = 1, n = 2 and n = 3.","The lines become closer together at higher energy and converge.","Names of the different hydrogen spectral series are not assessed."],"explain":["Hydrogen produces distinct spectral lines because its electron may occupy only particular **quantized energies**. Each emission line represents a transition from a higher level n to a lower one. Transitions ending at n = 1, n = 2 and n = 3 form different sets of lines.","At higher energy the energy levels move closer together. This is why the spectral lines become increasingly close and eventually approach a **convergence limit**. A line spectrum is evidence for discrete levels rather than a continuous range of electron energies."],"steps":["Identify the lower level at which the transition ends.","Relate a greater energy gap to a higher-energy photon, higher frequency and shorter wavelength.","Explain closer spectral lines by the smaller separations between high-energy levels."],"example":{"q":"Why are the spectral lines from transitions to n = 2 not equally spaced?","steps":["Allowed electronic energies are not equally spaced.","Different transitions therefore release unequal photon-energy differences.","Those energies correspond to unevenly separated frequencies and wavelengths."],"answer":"Because the atomic energy levels are quantized and unequally spaced."},"mistake":"Do not draw equally spaced electron energy levels for the hydrogen atom.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"hydrogen","title":"Hydrogen line emission spectrum","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":41,"guidePage":35},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.3.3","title":"Main energy levels","hl":false,"summary":"Each principal shell has an integer n and a maximum electron capacity.","objectives":["Use 2n² for the maximum number of electrons in the nth main energy level."],"explain":["The **principal quantum number n** labels a main energy level. The IB syllabus gives the maximum number of electrons in that shell as **2n²**. Higher values of n correspond to shells with greater maximum capacity.","This shell-capacity rule is a useful counting model. However, for actual electron configurations you also need the **sublevel and orbital** model in the next lessons, because electrons occupy orbitals in a specific energy order."],"steps":["Identify the principal level n.","Square n and multiply by 2.","Give the maximum electron count, not necessarily the number an actual atom has in that level."],"example":{"q":"What is the maximum capacity of the n = 3 shell?","steps":["Use 2n².","Substitute n = 3: 2 × 3².","2 × 9 = 18 electrons."],"answer":"18 electrons."},"mistake":"The 2n² capacity is not a statement that every atom completely fills lower numbered shells before all higher sublevels.","formula":"maximum electrons = 2n²","visual":{"kind":"shells","title":"Main energy levels","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":41,"guidePage":35},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.3.4","title":"Sublevels and orbitals","hl":false,"summary":"Main levels are divided into s, p, d and f sublevels.","objectives":["Recognize spherical s orbitals and three differently oriented p orbitals.","An orbital is a region with high probability of finding an electron."],"explain":["An **orbital** is a region of space where an electron is likely to be found. Main energy levels contain sublevels labelled s, p, d and f. They have different orbital counts and energies.","An **s orbital** is approximately spherical. Each p sublevel has **three p orbitals**, oriented along different spatial axes; each one is approximately dumbbell-shaped. Every individual orbital can hold at most two electrons."],"steps":["Identify whether the orbital is s (spherical) or p (two lobes).","For p, imagine three mutually perpendicular orientations: pₓ, pᵧ and p_z.","Count capacity from orbital count × 2 electrons per orbital."],"example":{"q":"How many electrons can a p sublevel hold?","steps":["A p sublevel contains three orbitals.","Each orbital accommodates up to two electrons.","3 × 2 = 6 electrons."],"answer":"Six electrons."},"mistake":"A p sublevel has three orbitals, not three electrons.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"orbitals","title":"Sublevels and orbitals","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":41,"guidePage":35},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.3.5","title":"Aufbau, Hund and Pauli","hl":false,"summary":"Use orbital filling rules for atoms and ions with Z ≤ 36.","objectives":["Aufbau: fill lower-energy orbitals before higher-energy orbitals.","Hund: occupy equivalent-energy orbitals singly before pairing.","Pauli: an orbital accommodates at most two electrons with opposite spin.","Write full and noble-gas condensed configurations and arrow-in-box orbital diagrams.","Know the chromium and copper exceptions."],"explain":["An electron configuration specifies the occupied orbitals of an atom or ion. The **Aufbau principle** fills lower-energy available orbitals first. **Pauli exclusion** allows no more than two electrons in each orbital, and they must have opposite spins. **Hund’s rule** places single electrons in separate equal-energy orbitals before pairing.","Write configurations in full (for example 1s² 2s² 2p²) or with a noble-gas core. For the first 36 elements, remember the **chromium and copper exceptions**: Cr is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ and Cu is [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹, rather than [Ar] 3d⁴ 4s² and [Ar] 3d⁹ 4s². When forming transition-metal cations, remove electrons from the outermost n shell before electrons from d."],"steps":["Determine the number of electrons, adjusting for ion charge.","Fill orbitals in the energy sequence, applying Aufbau, Pauli and Hund.","Check the count; verify any known Cr/Cu exception and remove outer-shell electrons first for cations."],"example":{"q":"Write the electron configuration of Fe²⁺ (Z = 26).","steps":["Neutral Fe is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶.","The 2+ ion has lost two electrons from 4s before 3d.","The remaining configuration is [Ar] 3d⁶."],"answer":"[Ar] 3d⁶."},"mistake":"Do not remove electrons from 3d before 4s when making Fe²⁺.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"filling","title":"Aufbau, Hund and Pauli","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":41,"guidePage":35},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.3.6","title":"First ionization energy","hl":true,"summary":"Explain patterns across periods and down groups, including discontinuities.","objectives":["First ionization energy is the energy needed to remove the first electron from gaseous atoms (per mole).","Across a period increasing effective nuclear attraction generally raises IE; exceptions arise from orbital energies and pairing.","Down a group increasing radius and shielding generally lower IE.","Use the convergence limit frequency or wavelength to calculate ionization energy."],"explain":["**First ionization energy** is the energy needed to remove one electron from each gaseous atom in one mole of gaseous atoms. In a period it generally increases because nuclear charge increases and the atomic radius decreases; down a group it generally decreases because increased shielding and distance weaken attraction.","Exceptions or **discontinuities** appear when an electron is removed from a different-energy sublevel or when electron pairing raises repulsion. At the hydrogen spectral convergence limit the electron is removed completely, so the photon energy at that limit corresponds to ionization."],"steps":["Across a period, consider proton number, shielding and whether the removed electron changes sublevel or becomes paired.","Down a group, consider increasing principal shell, radius and shielding.","For convergence-limit calculations, convert λ to metres, compute E = hc/λ per photon, then multiply by Nₐ for molar energy."],"example":{"q":"The hydrogen convergence limit is at 91.2 nm. Estimate its molar ionization energy using h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹.","steps":["Convert wavelength: 91.2 nm = 91.2 × 10⁻⁹ m.","E per photon = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸)/(91.2 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.","Multiply by Nₐ: (2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ × 6.02 × 10²³) / 1000 ≈ 1310 kJ mol⁻¹."],"answer":"Approximately 1.31 × 10³ kJ mol⁻¹."},"mistake":"E = hf gives the energy of one photon. Multiply by Avogadro’s constant for molar ionization energy.","formula":"E(photon) = hf = hc/λ","visual":{"kind":"ionization","title":"First ionization energy","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":41,"guidePage":35},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.3.7","title":"Successive ionization energies","hl":true,"summary":"Large jumps reveal where core electrons begin to be removed.","objectives":["Identify a large jump in successive IE data.","The number of electrons removed before a large jump often indicates the number of valence electrons in a main-group atom.","Use the pattern to deduce group placement."],"explain":["**Successive ionization energies** are measured by removing electrons one at a time from increasingly positive gaseous ions. Each removal generally requires more energy because the remaining electrons experience a greater net attraction.","A very **large jump** indicates that the next electron would be removed from a lower, inner energy level rather than the outer valence level. For a main-group element, the number of electrons removed before this large jump helps identify its group."],"steps":["Compare successive IE values, especially their ratios, rather than only absolute sizes.","Locate the first extremely large jump.","Count how many electrons were removed before it: that identifies the number of outer electrons for a typical main-group element."],"example":{"q":"IE values rise moderately for removals 1, 2 and 3, then jump dramatically at IE₄. What does this suggest?","steps":["Three electrons are removed before the large jump.","The fourth electron comes from an inner shell.","The atom has three valence electrons and would generally belong to group 13."],"answer":"Three outer electrons, typical of group 13."},"mistake":"Do not assume every successive increase is a shell change; look for the unusually large jump.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"successive","title":"Successive ionization energies","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":42,"guidePage":36},"group":"Structure 1"}]},{"id":"S1.4","name":"The mole","group":"Structure 1","sections":[{"id":"S1.4.1","title":"The Avogadro constant","hl":false,"summary":"One mole contains a specified number of elementary entities.","objectives":["Use amount of substance n in mol.","Entities can be atoms, molecules, ions, electrons or other specified particles.","Use the Avogadro constant Nₐ provided by the IB data booklet."],"explain":["A **mole** is a counting unit, much like a dozen, but for microscopic entities. One mole contains **6.02214076 × 10²³** specified particles (the Avogadro constant, Nₐ). You must always say what is being counted: atoms, molecules, ions or electrons.","Moles make it possible to connect microscopic numbers of particles with measurable quantities in a laboratory. **N = nNₐ**, where N is the number of specified entities and n is the amount in moles."],"steps":["Identify the particle or elementary entity being counted.","Multiply moles by Nₐ to obtain number of entities.","Divide number of entities by Nₐ to convert back to moles."],"example":{"q":"How many molecules are in 0.250 mol of water?","steps":["Use N = nNₐ.","N = 0.250 × 6.02214076 × 10²³.","N ≈ 1.51 × 10²³ molecules."],"answer":"1.51 × 10²³ H₂O molecules."},"mistake":"One mole of H₂O molecules contains three moles of individual atoms.","formula":"number of particles = n × Nₐ","visual":{"kind":"mole","title":"The Avogadro constant","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":42,"guidePage":36},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.4.2","title":"Relative atomic and formula masses","hl":false,"summary":"Relative masses use the carbon-12 scale.","objectives":["Aᵣ and Mᵣ have no units.","Sum atomic masses to obtain formula mass.","Use data-booklet atomic mass values to two decimal places."],"explain":["**Relative atomic mass Aᵣ** compares the average mass of atoms of an element to 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. **Relative formula mass Mᵣ** is obtained by adding Aᵣ values according to a chemical formula.","Both Aᵣ and Mᵣ are **relative numbers with no units**. The related molar mass M is expressed in **g mol⁻¹**. For examination calculations, take values from the IB data booklet and follow the specified rounding."],"steps":["Read each element’s Aᵣ from the data booklet.","Multiply by the number of that atom in the chemical formula.","Add all contributions to obtain Mᵣ; attach g mol⁻¹ only when stating molar mass M."],"example":{"q":"Find the relative formula mass of Ca(OH)₂ using Ca = 40.08, O = 16.00, H = 1.01.","steps":["There is one Ca atom, two O atoms and two H atoms.","Mᵣ = 40.08 + 2(16.00 + 1.01).","Mᵣ = 74.10."],"answer":"Mᵣ = 74.10 (no units); M = 74.10 g mol⁻¹."},"mistake":"Brackets multiply every atom inside them, not just the first.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"mass","title":"Relative atomic and formula masses","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":42,"guidePage":36},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.4.3","title":"Mass and amount of substance","hl":false,"summary":"Convert between mass, molar mass and the amount in moles.","objectives":["Molar mass M has units g mol⁻¹.","Use n = m/M.","Be consistent with units."],"explain":["**Molar mass M** is the mass of one mole of a substance. It is the link between a sample’s mass measured in grams and its amount of substance measured in moles.","The relationship **n = m/M** can be rearranged to **m = nM** or **M = m/n**. After finding moles, you can also multiply by Nₐ to estimate the number of specified particles."],"steps":["Determine M from the chemical formula or data booklet.","Convert mass in grams to amount using n = m/M.","If needed, convert amount to particles using N = nNₐ."],"example":{"q":"How many moles are present in 9.00 g of H₂O (M ≈ 18.02 g mol⁻¹)?","steps":["Select n = m/M.","n = 9.00 g ÷ 18.02 g mol⁻¹.","n ≈ 0.499 mol."],"answer":"Approximately 0.499 mol."},"mistake":"Moles (mol) and molar mass (g mol⁻¹) are different quantities.","formula":"n = m / M","visual":{"kind":"conversion","title":"Mass and amount of substance","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":42,"guidePage":36},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.4.4","title":"Empirical and molecular formula","hl":false,"summary":"Empirical formulas show simplest ratios; molecular formulas show actual numbers.","objectives":["Convert percentage masses to assumed masses and then to moles.","Divide each mole value by the smallest value.","Multiply ratios when needed to get whole numbers.","Compare empirical formula mass with actual molar mass to determine molecular formula."],"explain":["The **empirical formula** shows the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound. The **molecular formula** shows the actual number of each atom in a discrete molecule. An empirical formula might be CH₂O, while a particular molecular formula could be C₆H₁₂O₆.","Percentage composition can be converted to an empirical formula because the relative masses of elements can be converted to relative **mole amounts**, and chemical formulas express ratios of atoms rather than ratios of masses."],"steps":["Assume 100 g of compound so each mass percentage becomes grams.","Divide each element’s mass by its molar mass to get moles.","Divide by the smallest mole amount, then multiply if needed to obtain whole numbers.","Compare actual molar mass with empirical formula mass to find the molecular multiplier."],"example":{"q":"A substance contains 40.0% C, 6.7% H and 53.3% O. Find its empirical formula.","steps":["Assume 100 g: C 40.0 g, H 6.7 g, O 53.3 g.","Convert to moles: C ≈ 3.33, H ≈ 6.63, O ≈ 3.33.","Divide by 3.33: C:H:O ≈ 1:2:1."],"answer":"Empirical formula CH₂O."},"mistake":"Do not write the mass-percentage ratio as the atom ratio; first convert masses into moles.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"empirical","title":"Empirical and molecular formula","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":42,"guidePage":36},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.4.5","title":"Concentration","hl":false,"summary":"Molar concentration links solution volume and amount of solute.","objectives":["Use n = CV with V in dm³ when C is mol dm⁻³.","Convert between mol dm⁻³ and g dm⁻³ using molar mass.","Use square brackets to denote molar concentration."],"explain":["**Concentration** tells you how much solute is present in a given volume of solution. Molar concentration C is measured in mol dm⁻³ and related to amount n and volume V by **n = CV**.","You may also be given mass concentration in g dm⁻³. To convert g dm⁻³ to mol dm⁻³, divide by molar mass. To use the equation n = CV, convert volumes from cm³ to dm³ by dividing by 1000."],"steps":["Check whether concentration is in mol dm⁻³ or g dm⁻³.","Convert volume to dm³ if the given volume is in cm³.","Use n = CV or rearrange; convert using molar mass when needed."],"example":{"q":"How many moles of solute are in 250 cm³ of a 0.200 mol dm⁻³ solution?","steps":["Convert 250 cm³ to 0.250 dm³.","Use n = CV.","n = 0.200 × 0.250 = 0.0500 mol."],"answer":"0.0500 mol."},"mistake":"Using 250 instead of 0.250 in n = CV is a common factor-of-1000 error.","formula":"n = CV","visual":{"kind":"solution","title":"Concentration","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":43,"guidePage":37},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.4.6","title":"Avogadro’s law and gas volume","hl":false,"summary":"Equal gas volumes at the same T and P contain equal numbers of molecules.","objectives":["Gas volumes are proportional to mole amounts at the same temperature and pressure.","Use balanced equation stoichiometric ratios to calculate gas volumes."],"explain":["At the **same temperature and pressure**, gas volume is proportional to the number of molecules. This is Avogadro’s law: equal gas volumes under identical conditions contain equal numbers of molecules, even when the gases are different.","Balanced gas-reaction equations give mole ratios. When temperature and pressure match, those ratios are also **volume ratios** for gaseous reactants and products. This lets you answer gas-volume questions without first converting into grams."],"steps":["Write and balance the chemical equation.","Read the mole ratio for the two gaseous species.","Use the same ratio for gas volumes if all relevant gases are compared at the same T and P."],"example":{"q":"For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g), what volume of NH₃ forms from 12 dm³ H₂ if N₂ is in excess?","steps":["The equation shows 3 mol H₂ produce 2 mol NH₃.","At the same conditions, volume ratio H₂:NH₃ = 3:2.","V(NH₃) = 12 × (2/3) = 8.0 dm³."],"answer":"8.0 dm³ NH₃."},"mistake":"Volume ratios only follow mole ratios directly when gases are compared at the same temperature and pressure.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"avogadro","title":"Avogadro’s law and gas volume","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":43,"guidePage":37},"group":"Structure 1"}]},{"id":"S1.5","name":"Ideal gases","group":"Structure 1","sections":[{"id":"S1.5.1","title":"Assumptions of ideal gases","hl":false,"summary":"Ideal-gas particles have negligible volume and no intermolecular forces.","objectives":["Particles move and collide elastically.","Collisions conserve total kinetic energy.","The ideal gas is a simplified model."],"explain":["An **ideal gas** is a simplified model of freely moving particles. Its particles are treated as point-like with negligible volume, there are no attractive or repulsive intermolecular forces and collisions are perfectly elastic.","These assumptions make gas behaviour mathematically predictable. Real particles do have size and attract one another, but the model works well when those effects are small compared with the volume and motion of the gas."],"steps":["State the three assumptions explicitly.","Explain elastic collisions as collisions without a net loss of kinetic energy.","Recognize that the model approximates real gases under suitable conditions."],"example":{"q":"Why does the ideal-gas model predict pressure on container walls?","steps":["Gas particles move continually and randomly.","Their collisions with walls transfer momentum.","The many collisions produce force per unit area: pressure."],"answer":"Pressure results from gas-particle collisions with the walls."},"mistake":"Ideal gas particles are not stationary; their continuous random motion is essential.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"gasmodel","title":"Assumptions of ideal gases","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":43,"guidePage":37},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.5.2","title":"Deviations from ideal behaviour","hl":false,"summary":"Real gases deviate most at low temperature and high pressure.","objectives":["At low temperature, attractions between molecules become more important.","At high pressure, finite particle volume becomes important.","The syllabus requires qualitative, not mathematical, explanation of these deviations."],"explain":["**Real gases** deviate from ideal predictions when model assumptions become inaccurate. At **high pressure**, particles are squeezed closer together so their own volumes matter. At **low temperature**, particles move more slowly and intermolecular attractions become relatively important.","The effect depends on the gas: larger or more strongly polarizable molecules and those with stronger intermolecular forces may depart more noticeably from ideal behaviour. The IB syllabus requires a qualitative explanation, not a mathematical correction equation."],"steps":["Identify whether temperature is low or pressure is high.","Match high pressure with finite particle volume.","Match low temperature with increased importance of intermolecular attractions."],"example":{"q":"A gas at low temperature and high pressure behaves non-ideally. Give two reasons.","steps":["At high pressure, finite molecular volumes are not negligible relative to container volume.","At low temperature, attractions between molecules are significant compared with thermal motion.","These violate the ideal-gas assumptions."],"answer":"Finite particle volume and intermolecular attractions."},"mistake":"Do not claim all gases behave ideally simply because they are above their boiling point.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"realgas","title":"Deviations from ideal behaviour","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":43,"guidePage":37},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.5.3","title":"Gas graphs and molar volume","hl":false,"summary":"Examine P, V and T relationships for a fixed amount of gas.","objectives":["Use Kelvin in gas relationships.","Identify inversely proportional pressure–volume behaviour under constant temperature.","Identify proportional volume–temperature behaviour under constant pressure.","STP molar volume is supplied in the IB data booklet."],"explain":["For a fixed amount of ideal gas, pressure, volume and absolute temperature are related. At constant temperature, volume decreases as pressure increases; at constant pressure, volume increases as kelvin temperature increases.","**Molar volume** is the volume occupied by one mole of gas at a specified temperature and pressure. You must use values provided in the IB data booklet for the stated conditions and interpret graph shapes rather than just memorizing named gas laws."],"steps":["Identify which variable is held constant.","For fixed T: P and V are inversely proportional; the P–V graph curves downward.","For fixed P: V and T(K) are directly proportional; the V–T graph is a straight line through the origin."],"example":{"q":"A gas occupies 4.0 dm³ at a certain pressure and temperature. What happens to volume if pressure doubles at fixed temperature?","steps":["For fixed n and T, PV is constant.","P₂ = 2P₁, so V₂ = V₁/2.","V₂ = 2.0 dm³."],"answer":"2.0 dm³."},"mistake":"Volume is proportional to kelvin temperature, not Celsius temperature.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"gasgraphs","title":"Gas graphs and molar volume","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":43,"guidePage":37},"group":"Structure 1"},{"id":"S1.5.4","title":"Ideal and combined gas equations","hl":false,"summary":"Use SI pressure and volume and the gas constant.","objectives":["Use PV = nRT with pressure in pascal and volume in m³.","Use P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂ when the amount of gas is constant.","Convert temperatures to kelvin."],"explain":["The ideal gas equation combines the relationships between pressure **P**, volume **V**, amount **n**, temperature **T**, and gas constant **R**: **PV = nRT**. If the amount of gas is fixed between two states, use **P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂**.","In SI calculations with R in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, use pressure in **Pa**, volume in **m³**, and temperature in **K**. Check unit conversions before substituting numbers; 1 dm³ = 1 × 10⁻³ m³."],"steps":["List the known quantities and the required unknown.","Convert all quantities into compatible SI units.","Choose PV = nRT or the combined gas law, rearrange before substituting and check the result."],"example":{"q":"Calculate P for 0.200 mol gas at 300 K in 0.00500 m³. Use R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.","steps":["Rearrange PV = nRT to P = nRT/V.","Substitute: P = (0.200 × 8.31 × 300)/0.00500.","P = 99,720 Pa = 9.97 × 10⁴ Pa to 3 significant figures."],"answer":"9.97 × 10⁴ Pa."},"mistake":"If volume is supplied in dm³, convert to m³ before using R = 8.31.","formula":"PV = nRT ; P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂","visual":{"kind":"piston","title":"Ideal and combined gas equations","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":44,"guidePage":38},"group":"Structure 1"}]},{"id":"S2.1","name":"The ionic model","group":"Structure 2","sections":[{"id":"S2.1.1","title":"Formation of ions","hl":false,"summary":"Atoms gain or lose electrons to form cations or anions.","objectives":["Metals commonly lose electrons and form positive ions.","Non-metals commonly gain electrons and form negative ions.","Predict charges from electron configuration.","Include variable charges of transition element ions."],"explain":["An atom becomes an **ion** when it gains or loses electrons. Metals commonly lose their outer electrons to form positively charged **cations**; non-metals commonly gain electrons to form negatively charged **anions**.","Electron configuration is the reasoning behind many common ion charges. An element often forms an ion that reaches a stable outer-shell configuration. Some transition elements form ions with more than one charge because their electron arrangements can lose different numbers of electrons."],"steps":["Write the neutral atom’s outer electron arrangement.","Ask how many electrons are lost or gained to obtain an energetically favourable configuration.","Use a positive sign for electron loss and negative for electron gain."],"example":{"q":"Explain formation of Mg²⁺ from a neutral Mg atom.","steps":["A magnesium atom has two outer 3s electrons.","It loses two electrons.","The resulting Mg²⁺ ion has 12 protons but only 10 electrons."],"answer":"Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻."},"mistake":"A 2+ ion has lost two electrons, not gained two protons.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"ionformation","title":"Formation of ions","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":44,"guidePage":38},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.1.2","title":"Ionic bonds and formulas","hl":false,"summary":"An ionic bond is electrostatic attraction between opposite ions.","objectives":["Balance charges to form neutral empirical formulas.","For binary ionic names, write the cation then the anion ending in -ide.","Memorize ammonium NH₄⁺, hydroxide OH⁻, nitrate NO₃⁻, hydrogencarbonate HCO₃⁻, carbonate CO₃²⁻, sulfate SO₄²⁻ and phosphate PO₄³⁻."],"explain":["An **ionic bond** is the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions. It is not simply the act of electron transfer: transfer forms ions, while attraction holds the ions together in the compound.","In an ionic formula the total positive charge must balance the total negative charge. For binary compounds, write the cation name first and the anion with an **-ide** ending. For compounds containing polyatomic ions, retain the correct ion formula and use brackets when more than one is required."],"steps":["Write each ion symbol and its charge.","Find the smallest whole-number combination that gives zero net charge.","Write the empirical formula without charges; then name the ions in order."],"example":{"q":"Find the formula for aluminium sulfate from Al³⁺ and SO₄²⁻.","steps":["A common total charge is 6: two Al³⁺ ions give +6.","Three SO₄²⁻ ions give −6.","Use brackets for repeated polyatomic ions."],"answer":"Al₂(SO₄)₃."},"mistake":"Never alter subscripts inside a polyatomic ion to force charge balance.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"ionicbond","title":"Ionic bonds and formulas","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":44,"guidePage":38},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.1.3","title":"Giant ionic lattices and physical properties","hl":false,"summary":"Ionic solids form three-dimensional lattices.","objectives":["Explain high melting temperatures and low volatility using strong ionic attractions.","Explain why solids do not conduct but molten and aqueous forms can.","Discuss solubility as a competition between lattice interactions and solvent interactions.","Discuss lattice enthalpy as a measure of bond strength affected by ionic radii and charges."],"explain":["Ionic compounds form a **three-dimensional repeating lattice** of alternating cations and anions. Strong electrostatic attractions act throughout the lattice, explaining many ionic solids’ relatively high melting temperatures and low volatility.","An ionic solid usually does not conduct electricity because its ions are locked in lattice positions. When molten, or dissolved in water if sufficiently soluble, its **mobile ions** can carry charge. Lattice enthalpy reflects bonding strength and is influenced by **ion charge and ionic radius**."],"steps":["Start with the lattice model rather than separate “molecules”.","Connect high melting point to the energy needed to overcome strong electrostatic attractions.","Connect conductivity to whether charged particles can move.","For lattice strength, compare charges and distances between ions."],"example":{"q":"Why can molten NaCl conduct electricity when solid NaCl cannot?","steps":["Both contain positively and negatively charged ions.","In the solid, ions cannot move through the rigid lattice.","In the melt, ions are mobile and carry electrical charge."],"answer":"Only molten NaCl has sufficiently mobile charge-carrying ions."},"mistake":"Solid ionic compounds have ions, but not freely moving ions.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"lattice","title":"Giant ionic lattices and physical properties","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":44,"guidePage":38},"group":"Structure 2"}]},{"id":"S2.2","name":"The covalent model","group":"Structure 2","sections":[{"id":"S2.2.1","title":"Covalent bonds and Lewis formulas","hl":false,"summary":"A shared electron pair attracts two positive nuclei.","objectives":["Draw Lewis structures showing bonding pairs and lone pairs.","Apply the octet rule where applicable, including permitted incomplete octets.","Use dots, crosses or dashes to show electrons.","Work with ions as well as neutral molecules and with organic and inorganic examples."],"explain":["A **covalent bond** is the electrostatic attraction between a **shared pair of electrons** and the two positively charged nuclei. Electron sharing often allows atoms to approach an outer-shell configuration of eight electrons—the **octet rule**—though the rule has important exceptions.","**Lewis structures** show all valence electrons, including bonding and non-bonding (lone) pairs. A line represents a shared pair; dots or crosses can show lone pairs. For ions, enclose the structure in brackets and show its charge. The initial syllabus covers structures with up to four electron pairs around each atom."],"steps":["Add up valence electrons; add electrons for negative charge, subtract for positive charge.","Choose a plausible central atom and connect atoms with single bonds.","Complete outer-atom octets, place remaining electrons on the centre, then consider multiple bonds if needed.","Check the total electron count and ion charge."],"example":{"q":"Draw the Lewis structure of H₂O.","steps":["O contributes 6 valence electrons and two H contribute 2: total 8.","Two O–H single bonds use 4 electrons.","The remaining 4 electrons are two lone pairs on O."],"answer":"H–O–H with two lone pairs on the oxygen atom."},"mistake":"Hydrogen follows a duet (two electrons), not an octet.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"lewis","title":"Covalent bonds and Lewis formulas","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":45,"guidePage":39},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.2","title":"Single, double and triple bonds","hl":false,"summary":"One, two and three shared pairs create single, double and triple bonds.","objectives":["Within comparable bonds, increased bond order generally shortens and strengthens the bond.","Explain differences in bond length and strength using the number of shared pairs."],"explain":["One **shared pair** of electrons gives a single bond, two pairs a double bond, and three pairs a triple bond. The number of shared electron pairs is the **bond order** for these simple bonds.","As bond order increases between the same two elements, bonding generally becomes **shorter and stronger** because greater shared electron density holds the nuclei together more tightly. This helps explain different molecule lengths, bond energies and reactivities."],"steps":["Count the shared pairs or lines between the same pair of atoms.","Classify the bond as single, double or triple.","Compare bond length and strength for similar atoms: triple generally shortest/strongest; single generally longest/weakest."],"example":{"q":"Compare N–N, N=N and N≡N.","steps":["They have one, two and three shared electron pairs respectively.","The higher bond order generally gives a shorter bond.","N≡N is generally shorter and stronger than N=N, which is shorter and stronger than N–N."],"answer":"Bond strength generally rises and bond length falls with bond order."},"mistake":"This comparison works best between bonds involving the same pair of elements; do not compare different elements using bond order alone.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"bondorder","title":"Single, double and triple bonds","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":45,"guidePage":39},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.3","title":"Coordinate bonding","hl":false,"summary":"A donor atom supplies both electrons in the shared bonding pair.","objectives":["Recognize lone-pair donation as the origin of a coordinate bond.","At HL, include examples with transition element complexes."],"explain":["A **coordination (dative covalent) bond** forms when **both electrons** in a shared pair initially come from one atom. Once formed, it is a covalent bond; the description concerns the origin of the electron pair.","One atom needs an available lone pair, while the other has space to accept that pair. HL chemistry includes coordination bonding in **transition element complexes**, where ligands donate electron pairs to a central metal ion."],"steps":["Identify an atom or ion with a lone pair (electron-pair donor).","Identify an electron-deficient atom or ion that can accept the pair.","Draw a single arrow from the donor to the acceptor when showing bond formation."],"example":{"q":"Explain how NH₄⁺ can form from NH₃ and H⁺.","steps":["The nitrogen in NH₃ has one lone pair.","H⁺ can accept this pair.","Nitrogen donates both electrons to make the fourth N–H bond."],"answer":"NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺ via lone-pair donation."},"mistake":"The coordination bond is not a different strength or permanent “special type” after formation; it is distinguished by electron-pair origin.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"coordinate","title":"Coordinate bonding","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":45,"guidePage":39},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.4","title":"VSEPR: shape and electron domains","hl":false,"summary":"Electron domains repel and spread out around a central atom.","objectives":["Count each single, double or triple bond as one electron domain.","Count each lone pair as one domain.","Distinguish electron-domain geometry from molecular shape.","For two domains: linear; three: trigonal planar; four: tetrahedral electron-domain arrangement.","Lone pairs and multiple bonds can modify bond angles."],"explain":["**VSEPR** predicts geometry from repulsions between regions of electron density around a central atom. Count each single, double or triple bond as **one electron domain**, and count each lone pair as one domain.","Domains arrange to minimize repulsion. With two, three or four domains, ideal electron-domain geometries are **linear (180°)**, **trigonal planar (120°)** and **tetrahedral (109.5°)**. Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs and often compress the observed bond angles; molecular geometry describes atom positions only."],"steps":["Draw a Lewis structure and identify the central atom.","Count bonding domains and lone pairs around it.","Determine electron-domain geometry, then omit lone-pair positions when naming molecular geometry.","Adjust expected bond angles qualitatively for lone pairs and multiple bonds."],"example":{"q":"Predict the electron-domain and molecular geometries of NH₃.","steps":["The N atom has three N–H bonds and one lone pair: four domains.","Four domains give tetrahedral electron-domain geometry.","Ignoring the lone pair when naming atom arrangement gives trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry."],"answer":"Tetrahedral electron arrangement; trigonal pyramidal molecule, angle about 107°."},"mistake":"A double bond is ONE domain for VSEPR even though it contains two shared electron pairs.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"vsepr","title":"VSEPR: shape and electron domains","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":45,"guidePage":39},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.5","title":"Electronegativity and bond polarity","hl":false,"summary":"Difference in electronegativity creates partial charges.","objectives":["Higher electronegativity attracts bonding electrons more strongly.","Deduce a bond dipole from relative electronegativity.","Represent bond dipoles using δ− and δ+ or a vector."],"explain":["**Electronegativity** describes the ability of a bonded atom to attract the shared electrons. If two bonded atoms differ in electronegativity, the electron pair is pulled unevenly, producing a **polar covalent bond**.","The more electronegative end has partial negative charge (δ−), while the other end has partial positive charge (δ+). A bond dipole can be drawn as an arrow toward the partially negative end. Electronegativity values are provided in the IB data booklet."],"steps":["Look up both atoms’ electronegativity values.","Identify the atom with the higher value as the electron-rich end (δ−).","Draw δ+ and δ− or a vector pointing toward the more electronegative atom."],"example":{"q":"Which end of an H–Cl bond is δ−?","steps":["Chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen.","The bonding electron density is attracted more strongly toward Cl.","Label H δ+ and Cl δ−."],"answer":"Cl is δ−; H is δ+."},"mistake":"Partial charges in a polar covalent bond are not the same as full ionic charges.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"bondpolarity","title":"Electronegativity and bond polarity","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":46,"guidePage":40},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.6","title":"Molecular polarity","hl":false,"summary":"Polarity depends on both bond dipoles and the three-dimensional geometry.","objectives":["Symmetrically arranged equal bond dipoles may cancel.","Asymmetrical bond arrangements can give a non-zero molecular dipole.","You must explain examples with cancellation and without cancellation."],"explain":["A molecule’s overall polarity is the **vector sum of its bond dipoles**. Even a molecule containing polar bonds can be non-polar if its geometry is symmetrical and the bond dipoles cancel.","For example, CO₂ has two polar C=O bonds but is linear, so their dipoles cancel. H₂O has polar O–H bonds and a bent molecular shape, so the dipoles do not cancel and the molecule has a net dipole."],"steps":["Identify each polar bond and the direction of its dipole.","Determine the three-dimensional molecular shape using VSEPR.","Combine dipole arrows as vectors; if they cancel, the overall molecule has no permanent dipole."],"example":{"q":"Compare molecular polarity of CO₂ and H₂O.","steps":["CO₂: O=C=O is linear with opposing equal dipoles.","Its dipoles cancel, so CO₂ is non-polar overall.","H₂O is bent; its O–H dipoles reinforce instead of cancelling."],"answer":"CO₂ is non-polar overall; H₂O is polar."},"mistake":"Polar bonds do not automatically mean a polar molecule. Always inspect molecular geometry.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"moleculepolarity","title":"Molecular polarity","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":46,"guidePage":40},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.7","title":"Covalent networks","hl":false,"summary":"Carbon and silicon exhibit extended covalent networks.","objectives":["Compare diamond, graphite, graphene, fullerenes, silicon and silicon dioxide.","Relate different arrangements of bonding to conductivity, hardness and other properties.","Explain how allotropes of the same element can have different chemical and physical properties."],"explain":["**Giant covalent networks** contain atoms joined by covalent bonds across an extended structure. Silicon and silicon dioxide form network solids. The carbon allotropes **diamond, graphite, graphene and fullerenes** have different bonding patterns and therefore different properties.","Diamond forms a strong three-dimensional network with four bonds per carbon, making it very hard and normally electrically insulating; graphite consists of stacked hexagonal layers with mobile delocalized electrons within sheets, so it conducts and its layers can slide. Graphene is a single sheet with good in-plane electrical conductivity. **Fullerenes** are discrete carbon cages with weaker forces between molecules than giant covalent networks.","**Silicon** also forms a three-dimensional covalent network and behaves as a semiconductor. **Silicon dioxide (SiO₂)** has a giant network of Si and O atoms, with each silicon surrounded by four oxygen atoms and oxygen atoms linking silicon centres; strong covalent bonding helps explain its high melting temperature. These different connectivities—not simply the element names—control material properties."],"steps":["Identify whether the structure is giant 3D, layered, single-sheet or discrete molecular.","Explain strength or melting behaviour using the bonds that must be overcome.","For electrical conductivity, identify whether delocalized electrons or other mobile charge carriers are present."],"example":{"q":"Why does graphite conduct electricity but diamond normally does not?","steps":["Each carbon in graphite bonds to three others within a sheet.","It has delocalized electrons that can move across layers.","Diamond has four covalent bonds per carbon and lacks comparable mobile electrons."],"answer":"Graphite has mobile delocalized electrons; diamond does not."},"mistake":"Do not say graphite conducts because “layers slide”; sliding explains softness, not electrical conductivity.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"allotropes","title":"Covalent networks","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":46,"guidePage":40},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.8","title":"Intermolecular forces","hl":false,"summary":"The strength and type of intermolecular attractions depend on size and polarity.","objectives":["London dispersion forces operate between all molecules.","Permanent dipole–dipole forces operate between polar molecules.","Dipole-induced dipole forces act when a permanent dipole polarizes another particle.","Hydrogen bonds involve H bonded to an electronegative atom interacting with an electronegative atom nearby.","“Van der Waals” includes London dispersion, dipole–dipole and dipole-induced dipole in this syllabus."],"explain":["**Intermolecular forces** are attractions between molecules, distinct from covalent bonds within molecules. **London dispersion** forces arise from fluctuating electron distributions and are present in all molecules. **Dipole–dipole** attractions occur between permanent dipoles; a **dipole-induced dipole** occurs when one molecule polarizes another.","**Hydrogen bonding** is a stronger, directionally important interaction involving H bonded to an electronegative atom and an attractive interaction with a nearby electronegative atom. In typical IB examples, look for H bonded to N, O or F with suitable neighbouring electron density. The term **van der Waals forces** includes dispersion, dipole-induced dipole and dipole–dipole interactions in this syllabus."],"steps":["Determine whether a molecule has electrons: all molecular substances show London dispersion.","Check for a permanent dipole: polar molecules can display dipole–dipole interactions.","Check for H–N, H–O or H–F and a suitable neighbouring electronegative atom: hydrogen bonding may occur.","If a polar molecule encounters a non-polar one, consider dipole-induced dipole interactions."],"example":{"q":"Compare the main intermolecular forces in CH₄, HCl and H₂O.","steps":["CH₄ is non-polar, so its intermolecular interactions are dominated by London dispersion.","HCl is polar, so it exhibits dispersion and permanent dipole–dipole interactions.","H₂O exhibits dispersion, permanent dipole interactions and hydrogen bonding."],"answer":"CH₄: dispersion; HCl: dispersion + dipole–dipole; H₂O: dispersion + hydrogen bonding."},"mistake":"The presence of hydrogen does not guarantee hydrogen bonding: CH₄ and HCl are not normally described as hydrogen-bonding substances.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"imfs","title":"Intermolecular forces","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":46,"guidePage":40},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.9","title":"Properties of covalent substances","hl":false,"summary":"For comparable molar masses: dispersion < dipole–dipole < hydrogen bonding (general trend).","objectives":["Use intermolecular force strength to compare volatility.","Discuss electrical conductivity from mobile charged particles or delocalized electrons.","Predict solubility by considering attractive interactions with the solvent.","Avoid comparing intermolecular force strengths without controlling molecular size."],"explain":["For molecules of **comparable molar mass**, the IB guide gives the general order **London dispersion < dipole–dipole < hydrogen bonding**. The stronger the intermolecular forces, the more energy generally needed to separate molecules during boiling.","This helps explain **volatility** (a high-volatility substance evaporates easily and generally has a lower boiling temperature), solubility and electrical conductivity. Covalent molecular substances often do not conduct because they lack mobile ions and delocalized charge carriers. Solubility depends on how well new solute–solvent interactions compensate for the attractions broken."],"steps":["Check that the molecules compared have similar molar masses; large size can strengthen dispersion forces considerably.","Identify the strongest intermolecular interaction each can form.","Relate stronger forces to a higher boiling temperature / lower volatility, and discuss compatibility with solvent interactions for solubility."],"example":{"q":"Why does ethanol boil at a higher temperature than a similar-mass molecule exhibiting only dispersion forces?","steps":["Ethanol has an O–H bond and can hydrogen bond between molecules.","A similar-sized non-polar molecule is dominated by weaker dispersion forces.","Separating ethanol molecules requires more energy, so boiling occurs at a higher temperature."],"answer":"Additional hydrogen bonding leads to stronger intermolecular attractions."},"mistake":"At boiling, intermolecular attractions are overcome; the covalent bonds inside molecules usually remain intact.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"boiling","title":"Properties of covalent substances","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":46,"guidePage":40},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.10","title":"Chromatography and Rf","hl":false,"summary":"Mixtures separate because components interact differently with mobile and stationary phases.","objectives":["Components more strongly attracted to the stationary phase generally move less.","Calculate retardation factor from distances travelled by spot and solvent front.","Interpret relative Rf values in a given chromatographic system.","Locating agents and detailed operation of GC/HPLC are not assessed."],"explain":["**Chromatography** separates substances according to their different attractions to a **stationary phase** and a moving **mobile phase**. A component that interacts strongly with the stationary phase usually moves less far, while a component that prefers the mobile phase travels further.","The **retardation factor Rf** compares the distance moved by a spot with the distance moved by the solvent front, both from the start line: **Rf = distance of spot ÷ distance of solvent front**. Under ordinary conditions values lie between 0 and 1. Comparisons only make sense under comparable experimental conditions."],"steps":["Draw a baseline and mark where the mixture was placed.","Measure vertically from the baseline to the centre of the separated spot.","Measure from the same baseline to the solvent front.","Divide spot distance by solvent-front distance; interpret lower Rf as relatively greater stationary-phase attraction."],"example":{"q":"A pigment spot moves 3.0 cm; solvent front moves 7.5 cm. Calculate Rf.","steps":["Rf = distance travelled by spot ÷ distance travelled by solvent front.","Rf = 3.0 / 7.5.","Rf = 0.40 (no units)."],"answer":"Rf = 0.40."},"mistake":"Measure both distances from the original baseline, not from the bottom of the paper.","formula":"Rf = (distance moved by spot) / (distance moved by solvent front)","visual":{"kind":"chromatography","title":"Chromatography and Rf","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":47,"guidePage":41},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.11","title":"Resonance and delocalization","hl":true,"summary":"More than one Lewis structure can place a double bond in different positions.","objectives":["Draw valid resonance contributors by shifting electron pairs, not atom positions.","Understand that the real bonding is delocalized over the relevant atoms."],"explain":["Some molecules or ions cannot be adequately represented by one Lewis drawing with a double bond in a single fixed location. Multiple valid drawings that keep the same atom positions but change electron placement are called **resonance structures**.","The real structure is a **resonance hybrid**: certain electrons are **delocalized** rather than jumping back and forth between two fixed arrangements. Resonance helps explain why several bonds can have the same intermediate length and why some species are relatively stabilized."],"steps":["Draw a valid Lewis structure and identify a region where a double bond or lone pair can be redistributed.","Move only electrons, not nuclei, to obtain another valid contributor.","Use a resonance double arrow and describe the real structure as delocalized."],"example":{"q":"Why can two equivalent N–O bonds in NO₂⁻ be represented by different Lewis drawings?","steps":["One Lewis drawing places N=O on the first oxygen.","Another places it on the second oxygen, while atom positions stay the same.","In the actual ion, bonding electron density is delocalized over both N–O positions."],"answer":"Two resonance contributors describe one delocalized ion."},"mistake":"Resonance structures do not represent separate rapidly interconverting molecules.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"resonance","title":"Resonance and delocalization","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":47,"guidePage":41},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.12","title":"Evidence for benzene resonance","hl":true,"summary":"Benzene (C₆H₆) has a delocalized ring of π electrons.","objectives":["Use physical bond-length evidence to discuss its structure.","Use chemical evidence of benzene’s relative stability to support delocalization."],"explain":["**Benzene (C₆H₆)** is a flat six-carbon ring in which each carbon has a remaining p orbital. Those p orbitals overlap around the ring, creating a **delocalized π electron system** above and below the ring plane.","If benzene had permanently alternating single and double bonds, the C–C bond lengths should differ. **Observed equal C–C bond lengths**, intermediate between normal single and double bonds, support resonance. Benzene also tends to undergo reactions that preserve the ring’s delocalization, rather than behaving exactly like an ordinary alkene."],"steps":["Draw benzene as six linked carbon atoms with one H per carbon.","Represent its resonance contributors as alternating double bonds in two positions.","Use equal bond-length evidence to justify the delocalized hexagon / circle model."],"example":{"q":"Give one physical and one chemical observation supporting delocalization in benzene.","steps":["Physical: the six C–C bonds have equal lengths.","Chemical: benzene is less prone to simple addition reactions than an isolated alkene under similar conditions.","Both observations are consistent with an unusually stable delocalized π system."],"answer":"Equal ring bond lengths and characteristic relative resistance to addition."},"mistake":"The alternating-bond hexagon is a resonance contributor; real benzene does not contain three permanently localized ordinary double bonds.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"benzene","title":"Evidence for benzene resonance","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":47,"guidePage":41},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.13","title":"Expanded octets and five/six domains","hl":true,"summary":"Some atoms form structures with more than eight electrons around a central atom.","objectives":["Draw Lewis structures with five or six electron domains on the central atom.","Predict trigonal bipyramidal and octahedral electron-domain arrangements.","Determine molecular shapes by accounting for lone pairs."],"explain":["The simple octet rule does not describe every valid Lewis representation. Some central atoms in the syllabus can be depicted with **expanded octets**, containing more than eight bonding electrons around them.","At HL you must represent species with **five** and **six** electron domains and apply VSEPR. Five domains generally give a **trigonal bipyramidal** electron arrangement; six give an **octahedral** arrangement. Lone pairs change molecular geometry while the electron-domain geometry remains set by the total number of domains."],"steps":["Count total electron domains, including lone pairs and each multiple bond as one.","Draw five-domain geometry as trigonal bipyramidal or six-domain as octahedral.","Exclude lone-pair positions when naming the molecular geometry."],"example":{"q":"Predict the molecular geometry of PCl₅ and SF₆.","steps":["PCl₅ has five bonding domains and no lone pairs on phosphorus.","Its electron and molecular geometry are trigonal bipyramidal.","SF₆ has six bonding domains and no lone pairs on sulfur; it is octahedral."],"answer":"PCl₅: trigonal bipyramidal; SF₆: octahedral."},"mistake":"Do not use “tetrahedral” for a five- or six-domain centre.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"expanded","title":"Expanded octets and five/six domains","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":47,"guidePage":41},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.14","title":"Formal charge","hl":true,"summary":"Formal charge compares an atom’s valence electrons with its allocated electrons in a Lewis structure.","objectives":["Calculate formal charge on each atom of a proposed structure.","Prefer plausible structures using the distribution and magnitude of formal charges.","Formal charge is not the same as oxidation number."],"explain":["**Formal charge** is a bookkeeping method that assigns bonding electrons equally between bonded atoms, regardless of electronegativity. It helps compare competing valid Lewis structures. For an atom: **FC = valence electrons − non-bonding electrons − ½(bonding electrons)**.","The sum of the formal charges must equal the species’ overall charge. Structures with smaller formal-charge magnitudes and an appropriate placement of negative charge are often preferred, although this is a guide rather than an absolute law."],"steps":["Find the neutral atom’s valence electron count.","Count its lone-pair electrons and total bonding electrons.","Subtract non-bonding electrons and half the bonding electrons.","Check that formal charges sum to the overall species charge."],"example":{"q":"Calculate the formal charge on N in NH₄⁺.","steps":["Nitrogen has five valence electrons.","In NH₄⁺ nitrogen has no lone pairs and four single bonds (8 bonding electrons).","FC = 5 − 0 − ½(8) = +1."],"answer":"Formal charge on N = +1."},"mistake":"Formal charge differs from oxidation state: formal charge splits bonding electrons equally.","formula":"FC = valence e⁻ − nonbonding e⁻ − ½(bonding e⁻)","visual":{"kind":"formalcharge","title":"Formal charge","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":47,"guidePage":41},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.15","title":"Sigma and pi bonds","hl":true,"summary":"A sigma bond forms along the bond axis; a pi bond forms from sideways p-orbital overlap.","objectives":["A single bond contains one σ bond.","A double bond contains one σ and one π bond.","A triple bond contains one σ and two π bonds.","Count σ and π bonds in organic and inorganic examples."],"explain":["A **sigma (σ) bond** forms by head-on orbital overlap with electron density concentrated along the internuclear axis. A **pi (π) bond** forms when parallel p orbitals overlap laterally, with electron density on either side of that axis.","A single bond consists of one σ bond; a double bond consists of **one σ plus one π**, and a triple bond consists of **one σ plus two π** bonds. You must count both types in organic and inorganic molecules and ions."],"steps":["Count every connected pair of atoms as one sigma bond.","For each double bond add one pi bond.","For each triple bond add two pi bonds."],"example":{"q":"How many σ and π bonds are present in ethene, C₂H₄?","steps":["Four C–H single bonds give four σ bonds.","The C=C double bond contributes one σ and one π.","Total σ = 5, π = 1."],"answer":"Five σ bonds and one π bond."},"mistake":"A double bond is not two σ bonds. The second component is π.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"sigmapi","title":"Sigma and pi bonds","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":47,"guidePage":41},"group":"Structure 2"},{"id":"S2.2.16","title":"Hybridization","hl":true,"summary":"Mixing atomic orbitals produces hybrid orbitals for bonding.","objectives":["Know sp, sp² and sp³ hybridization.","sp has two electron domains (linear); sp² three (trigonal planar); sp³ four (tetrahedral electron-domain geometry).","Deduce hybridization from Lewis structures and domain count; predict geometry from hybridization."],"explain":["**Hybridization** is a model in which atomic orbitals combine mathematically to form new equivalent hybrid orbitals suited to a particular bonding geometry. For the IB syllabus, you need only **sp, sp² and sp³**.","The number of electron domains around an atom provides a practical route to hybridization: **2 domains → sp → linear**, **3 → sp² → trigonal planar**, **4 → sp³ → tetrahedral electron-domain geometry**. Unhybridized p orbitals can form π bonds in double and triple bonds."],"steps":["Draw the Lewis structure and count electron domains around the atom.","Use two domains for sp, three for sp², four for sp³.","Relate the hybridization to geometry and available unhybridized p orbitals."],"example":{"q":"Compare carbon hybridization in methane, ethene and ethyne.","steps":["CH₄ carbon has four domains → sp³ (tetrahedral).","C₂H₄ each carbon has three domains → sp² (trigonal planar).","C₂H₂ each carbon has two domains → sp (linear)."],"answer":"CH₄: sp³; C₂H₄: sp²; C₂H₂: sp."},"mistake":"Count an entire double or triple bond as one electron domain when finding hybridization.","formula":"","visual":{"kind":"hybrid","title":"Hybridization","labels":[]},"legacy":true,"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":48,"guidePage":42},"group":"Structure 2"}]},{"id":"S2.3","name":"The metallic model","group":"Structure 2","sections":[{"id":"S2.3.1","title":"Metallic bonding and metal properties","hl":false,"summary":"Mobile electrons attract a lattice of metal cations.","explain":["In a metal, outer electrons are delocalized across an extended structure rather than confined to one bond. Electrostatic attraction between these electrons and positive metal ions holds the solid together.","Mobile electrons transport charge and energy, explaining electrical and thermal conductivity. Metallic attraction is non-directional, so layers can move while attraction to the electron cloud remains; this explains malleability."],"steps":["Identify cations and delocalized electrons","For conductivity, identify mobile electrons","For malleability, explain maintained attraction as layers move"],"example":{"q":"Why can a metal be hammered into shape without shattering like an ionic crystal?","steps":["Metal ions can change relative positions under force","Electrons remain delocalized across the structure","Electrostatic attraction is maintained rather than creating strongly repelling like-charge planes"],"answer":"Non-directional metallic bonding permits deformation."},"mistake":"Do not say positive metal ions conduct electricity by moving through a solid wire.","visual":{"kind":"lattice","title":"Metallic bonding and metal properties","labels":["Cation lattice","Delocalized electrons","Mobile charge","Non-directional attraction"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":48,"guidePage":42},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S2.3.2","title":"What strengthens metallic bonding?","hl":false,"summary":"Greater cation charge and smaller radius usually strengthen metallic attraction.","explain":["Metallic bonding depends on how strongly the cations attract the delocalized electrons. A greater positive charge and a smaller ionic radius increase this attraction, provided comparable structures are being considered.","Across the period-3 metals sodium, magnesium and aluminium, increasing numbers of contributed electrons and higher cation charge generally strengthen metallic bonding. More energy is required to overcome the stronger attractions, giving higher melting points in this simple comparison."],"steps":["Compare cation charges","Compare radii and electron density","Relate attraction strength to melting point"],"example":{"q":"Explain the general increase in melting point from Na to Mg to Al.","steps":["Na contributes one outer electron, Mg two and Al three in the simple model","Cation charge increases while size generally decreases","The denser electron cloud and stronger attraction require more energy to overcome"],"answer":"Metallic bonding generally strengthens from Na to Al."},"mistake":"Do not describe a metal as separate molecules joined by intermolecular forces.","visual":{"kind":"trend","title":"What strengthens metallic bonding?","labels":["Higher cation charge","Smaller radius","Stronger attraction","Higher melting point"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":48,"guidePage":42},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S2.3.3","title":"Transition-metal bonding","hl":true,"summary":"Delocalized d electrons contribute to transition-metal properties.","explain":["Transition elements can involve d electrons as well as outer s electrons in metallic bonding. Their delocalized electron population can produce strong attractions to the positive ion framework.","This helps explain their typically high melting temperatures and good electrical conductivity. Melting trends across the d block are less simple than the s-block model because electron arrangements and solid structures vary."],"steps":["Identify available delocalized d and s electrons","Connect stronger attraction to melting temperature","Connect mobile electrons to conductivity"],"example":{"q":"Why is iron generally a high-melting electrical conductor?","steps":["Its solid contains metallic bonding with delocalized electrons","Strong attraction throughout the lattice requires substantial energy to overcome","Mobile delocalized electrons carry charge"],"answer":"Strong metallic bonding and mobile electrons explain the properties."},"mistake":"Avoid claiming every transition element has a higher melting point than every main-group metal.","visual":{"kind":"lattice","title":"Transition-metal bonding","labels":["s electrons + d electrons","Delocalization","Strong metallic lattice","Electrical conduction"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":48,"guidePage":42},"group":"Structure"}]},{"id":"S2.4","name":"From models to materials","group":"Structure 2","sections":[{"id":"S2.4.1","title":"The bonding continuum","hl":false,"summary":"Ionic, covalent and metallic descriptions are useful limiting models.","explain":["Real bonding does not always fit into three completely separate boxes. Some substances combine unequal electron sharing, directional covalent character and more widespread electron delocalization. A bonding triangle represents this continuum.","Use the dominant bonding contribution to explain observed behaviour, while recognizing mixed character. A material near an ionic region may have high melting temperature and conduct when molten; a material with metallic character is more likely to conduct as a solid."],"steps":["Identify observed properties","Choose the most useful bonding model","State where mixed character limits a simple classification"],"example":{"q":"Why is a bonding continuum more useful than insisting every compound is perfectly ionic or covalent?","steps":["Electron sharing varies continuously with the atoms involved","Many real bonds have mixed character","A continuum helps connect this mixture to measured physical properties"],"answer":"Bonding models are limits, not perfectly separated categories."},"mistake":"A triangle position is a model of bonding character, not a claim that a sample contains three separate phases.","visual":{"kind":"triangle","title":"The bonding continuum","labels":["Ionic limit","Covalent limit","Metallic limit","Mixed bonding character"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":48,"guidePage":42},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S2.4.2","title":"Using electronegativity in a bonding triangle","hl":false,"summary":"Electronegativity difference and average locate binary substances in a bonding model.","explain":["For a binary compound, calculate the electronegativity difference Δχ = |χA − χB| and the average χaverage = (χA + χB)/2. Use these values with the axes and regions of the supplied bonding triangle.","A large difference usually suggests substantial ionic character. A small difference combined with a high average suggests shared electrons in covalent bonding; a small difference and low average is associated with metallic character. These are tendencies, not universal rules."],"steps":["Look up both electronegativities","Calculate difference and average","Plot using the supplied axes and infer properties"],"example":{"q":"For atoms with χ = 0.9 and 3.0, calculate the coordinates used with a bonding triangle.","steps":["Difference = ","3.0 − 0.9"," = 2.1","Average = (3.0 + 0.9)/2 = 1.95","Use the supplied triangle; the relatively large difference suggests ionic character"],"answer":"Δχ = 2.1 and χaverage = 1.95."},"mistake":"Do not calculate percentage ionic character; the syllabus does not require it.","visual":{"kind":"triangle","title":"Using electronegativity in a bonding triangle","labels":["χA and χB","Difference: 2.1","Average: 1.95","Plot on supplied triangle"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":49,"guidePage":43},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S2.4.3","title":"Alloys and enhanced properties","hl":false,"summary":"Alloys are mixtures with modified metallic structures and useful properties.","explain":["An alloy contains a metal mixed with one or more other elements. Differently sized atoms can disrupt the regular arrangement of a pure metal, making it harder for layers or defects to move. This often increases hardness.","Metallic bonding remains non-directional, allowing mixtures without needing a fixed molecular ratio. Alloy composition can be selected for strength, corrosion resistance or other properties; examples include brass, bronze and stainless steel, but specific recipes need not be memorized."],"steps":["Identify the base metal and mixed atoms","Explain disruption of a regular lattice","Relate restricted sliding to hardness"],"example":{"q":"Why can an alloy be harder than a pure metal?","steps":["Added atoms can differ in size","They distort the regular metal arrangement","Movement of layers becomes more difficult while metallic attraction remains"],"answer":"Lattice distortion can increase hardness."},"mistake":"Alloys are mixtures, not necessarily compounds with fixed formulas.","visual":{"kind":"lattice","title":"Alloys and enhanced properties","labels":["Pure regular lattice","Different-sized atoms","Distorted arrangement","Harder to deform"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":49,"guidePage":43},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S2.4.4","title":"Polymer structure and plastic properties","hl":false,"summary":"Polymers are macromolecules built from repeated monomer units.","explain":["A polymer is a very large molecule constructed from many repeated subunits. Long covalently bonded chains may become entangled, and attractions between chains help determine flexibility, strength and softening behaviour.","Many plastics are lightweight, mouldable and poor electrical conductors because they lack mobile charge carriers. Natural polymers and synthetic polymers share the repeated-unit idea, although their functional groups and chain structures differ considerably."],"steps":["Identify the repeated structural pattern","Distinguish covalent chain bonds from attractions between chains","Connect chain arrangement to a stated property"],"example":{"q":"Why can a common polymer plastic be a useful electrical insulator?","steps":["Its electrons are largely localized in covalent bonds","It lacks mobile ions in the solid","There are no abundant mobile charge carriers to transport current"],"answer":"A lack of mobile charge carriers explains insulation."},"mistake":"Do not say all polymers are identical or all are biodegradable.","visual":{"kind":"chain","title":"Polymer structure and plastic properties","labels":["Monomer units","Long covalent chains","Interchain attractions","Material properties"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":49,"guidePage":43},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S2.4.5","title":"Addition polymerization","hl":false,"summary":"Alkene double bonds open to connect monomers without eliminating a small molecule.","explain":["In addition polymerization, the carbon–carbon double bond in each alkene monomer becomes a carbon–carbon single bond as new bonds form to neighbouring units. The polymer backbone contains the two original alkene carbons.","Draw one repeat unit in brackets with continuation bonds passing through both sides and n outside. All monomer atoms remain in the polymer, so the ideal addition process has 100% atom economy. Side groups stay attached to their original carbon."],"steps":["Identify the C=C bond","Change it to a C–C backbone and retain side groups","Add continuation bonds, brackets and n"],"example":{"q":"Write the repeat unit formed from propene, CH2=CHCH3.","steps":["The two double-bond carbons become the backbone","Retain CH3 attached to the second backbone carbon","Represent one repeat unit with open continuation bonds"],"answer":"[–CH2–CH(CH3)–]n."},"mistake":"Do not include the original C=C bond inside a simple alkene addition-polymer repeat unit.","visual":{"kind":"chain","title":"Addition polymerization","labels":["CH2=CH–R","Open double bond","–CH2–CH(R)–","n repeat units"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":49,"guidePage":43},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S2.4.6","title":"Condensation polymers","hl":true,"summary":"Functional groups join monomers while releasing a small molecule.","explain":["Condensation polymerization joins monomers with suitable functional groups at two sites. A carboxylic acid plus alcohol forms an ester link, while a carboxylic acid plus amine forms an amide link; water is commonly released.","Polyesters contain –COO– links and polyamides contain –CONH– links. Identify the small molecule removed at each link and preserve the rest of each monomer in the repeat unit. Hydrolysis reverses these link-forming reactions and can break the chain."],"steps":["Identify two reactive groups on each monomer","Remove the atoms forming water at each link","Join through ester or amide links and draw continuation bonds"],"example":{"q":"What link forms between a dicarboxylic acid and a diamine?","steps":["A –COOH group reacts with a –NH2 group","OH from acid and H from amine form water","The remaining fragments join as –CO–NH–"],"answer":"A polyamide link, –CONH–, with water eliminated."},"mistake":"Do not confuse an amide link with an amine group: an amide contains a carbonyl beside N.","visual":{"kind":"chain","title":"Condensation polymers","labels":["Dicarboxylic acid","Diamine","–CO–NH– link","Water released"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":49,"guidePage":43},"group":"Structure"}]},{"id":"S3.1","name":"The periodic table: Classification of elements","group":"Structure 3","sections":[{"id":"S3.1.1","title":"Periods, groups and blocks","hl":false,"summary":"The periodic table arranges elements by atomic number and electronic structure.","explain":["Rows are periods and columns are groups, numbered 1–18. The s, p, d and f blocks reflect the types of sublevel being filled across those regions. Metals mostly occupy the left and centre, while non-metals are concentrated on the upper right.","Metalloids occupy parts of the boundary region between typical metals and non-metals and show intermediate properties. Use the data-booklet periodic table to locate elements instead of trying to infer every property from a single category."],"steps":["Locate the element by atomic number","Read its row and column","Identify block and broad metal/non-metal region"],"example":{"q":"Where is chlorine (Z = 17) located?","steps":["Find atomic number 17","It is in the third row and group 17","Its differentiating electron enters a p sublevel"],"answer":"Period 3, group 17, p block, non-metal."},"mistake":"Group and period are not interchangeable.","visual":{"kind":"periodic","title":"Periods, groups and blocks","labels":["Rows: periods","Columns: groups","s p d f blocks","Metal–non-metal regions"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":50,"guidePage":44},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.2","title":"Position and electron configuration","hl":false,"summary":"Period and group information connects to occupied shells and valence electrons.","explain":["The period usually identifies the highest occupied principal energy level in an atom. For main-group atoms, group membership is related to the outer s and p electron count, explaining similar chemical behaviour within a group.","Use configurations up to Z = 36 to connect electronic structure with table position. Recognize group 1 alkali metals, group 17 halogens, group 18 noble gases and the d-block transition region. Electron configuration gives a stronger explanation than simply learning group labels."],"steps":["Write the configuration","Identify the highest occupied n","Count outer-shell electrons for main-group placement"],"example":{"q":"Deduce the position of an atom with configuration [Ne]3s2 3p4.","steps":["Highest occupied principal shell is n = 3","The outer shell has 2 + 4 = 6 electrons","A p-block atom with six valence electrons is in group 16"],"answer":"Period 3, group 16: sulfur."},"mistake":"For d-block atoms, group placement cannot be determined by treating outer s electrons alone like a main-group rule.","visual":{"kind":"periodic","title":"Position and electron configuration","labels":["Configuration","Highest n → period","Outer electrons → group","Block from sublevel"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":50,"guidePage":44},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.3","title":"Periodic trends","hl":false,"summary":"Radius and electronic attraction explain recurring property patterns.","explain":["Across a period, increasing nuclear charge with limited additional shielding generally reduces atomic radius and increases ionization energy and electronegativity. Down a group, added electron shells increase size and shielding, generally reducing attraction for outer electrons.","Cations are smaller than their parent atoms because electron loss can remove a shell and increases attraction per remaining electron. Anions are larger because additional electron repulsion expands the electron distribution. Electron affinity concerns the energy change when an electron is added to a gaseous atom and has exceptions; specify the sign convention used."],"steps":["Identify nuclear charge, shell count and shielding","Use these to compare attraction to outer electrons","Apply the reasoning to the requested property and note relevant exceptions"],"example":{"q":"Why is Na+ much smaller than Na?","steps":["Neutral Na has an electron in the third shell","Na+ has lost this outer electron and has only occupied first and second shells","The remaining electrons are attracted by the same nucleus"],"answer":"Loss of the outer occupied shell produces a much smaller cation."},"mistake":"Never explain a trend only with “it wants a full shell”; discuss electrostatic attraction.","visual":{"kind":"trend","title":"Periodic trends","labels":["Across: stronger attraction","Down: more shells","Cation smaller","Anion larger"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":50,"guidePage":44},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.4","title":"Group 1 and group 17 reactivity","hl":false,"summary":"Metal electron loss and halogen electron gain change systematically down groups.","explain":["Group 1 metals react with water to form a metal hydroxide and hydrogen. Down the group, outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded, so electron loss becomes easier and reactions become more vigorous.","Halogens act as oxidizing agents by accepting electrons. Down group 17, increased radius and shielding make electron gain less favourable, so oxidizing strength decreases. A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halogen from its halide solution."],"steps":["For group 1, write metal + water → hydroxide + hydrogen","For halogens, compare positions in group 17","Predict displacement only when the free halogen is a stronger oxidant"],"example":{"q":"Predict whether chlorine reacts with bromide ions and write the ionic equation.","steps":["Chlorine is above bromine in group 17","Chlorine is the stronger oxidant and accepts electrons from Br−","Balance atoms and charge"],"answer":"Cl2 + 2Br− → 2Cl− + Br2."},"mistake":"Do not apply the group-1 reactivity trend to halogens; their electron-transfer roles differ.","visual":{"kind":"trend","title":"Group 1 and group 17 reactivity","labels":["Group 1: lose e−","Reactivity increases down","Group 17: gain e−","Oxidizing power decreases down"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":50,"guidePage":44},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.5","title":"Oxide acidity and environmental effects","hl":false,"summary":"Oxides range from basic through amphoteric to acidic across a period.","explain":["Typical metal oxides are basic and react with acids; some react with water to form hydroxides. Many non-metal oxides are acidic and react with water to form acids or with bases to form salts. Amphoteric oxides react with both acids and bases.","Examples include Na2O + H2O → 2NaOH, CaO + H2O → Ca(OH)2, CO2 + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 and SO2 + H2O ⇌ H2SO3. Atmospheric sulfur and nitrogen oxides contribute to acid rain; dissolved CO2 shifts ocean carbonate equilibria toward greater acidity."],"steps":["Identify whether the oxide is predominantly metal or non-metal","Classify as basic, amphoteric or acidic using evidence","Write a balanced reaction with water where appropriate"],"example":{"q":"Write the reaction of sodium oxide with water and classify the product.","steps":["Sodium oxide is a basic metal oxide","It forms aqueous sodium hydroxide","Balance Na using coefficient 2 for NaOH"],"answer":"Na2O + H2O → 2NaOH; alkaline product."},"mistake":"Not every metal oxide reacts rapidly with water, even when it is classified as basic.","visual":{"kind":"trend","title":"Oxide acidity and environmental effects","labels":["Metal oxides: basic","Boundary oxides: amphoteric","Non-metal oxides: acidic","CO2 dissolution"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":50,"guidePage":44},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.6","title":"Oxidation states","hl":false,"summary":"Oxidation state is a formal electron-allocation number, not necessarily an actual charge.","explain":["Assign oxidation states as if each bond were ionic, with bonding electrons allocated to the more electronegative atom. An uncombined element has oxidation state zero because no net electron allocation to a different element occurs.","The sum of oxidation states equals the total species charge. Oxygen is usually −2 but is −1 in peroxides; hydrogen is usually +1 but is −1 in metal hydrides. Use known assignments to solve for unknown values and recognize nitrate/nitrite and sulfate/sulfite names."],"steps":["Apply familiar element rules and exceptions","Set the sum equal to the overall charge","Solve for the unknown oxidation state"],"example":{"q":"Find sulfur’s oxidation state in SO4^2−.","steps":["Assign each oxygen −2","Let sulfur be x: x + 4(−2) = −2","Solve x − 8 = −2"],"answer":"Sulfur is +6."},"mistake":"Write oxidation state as +6, not 6+; oxidation state is not the same notation as ion charge.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Oxidation states","labels":["Known O states","Sum = ion charge","x − 8 = −2","S = +6"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":50,"guidePage":44},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.7","title":"Ionization-energy discontinuities","hl":true,"summary":"Sublevels and electron pairing explain small decreases in an overall increasing trend.","explain":["Across a period, the overall increase in first ionization energy is interrupted when the removed electron occupies a higher-energy p sublevel instead of an s sublevel, or when it becomes paired in an already occupied orbital.","For example, the electron removed from aluminium is in 3p, at higher energy than magnesium’s removed 3s electron. Sulfur has a paired 3p electron, experiencing repulsion; phosphorus has singly occupied p orbitals. Explain the energy of the electron removed rather than invoking unexplained special stability."],"steps":["Compare removed-electron sublevels","Check whether it is paired","Explain how higher orbital energy or repulsion lowers removal energy"],"example":{"q":"Why is IE1 of Al lower than that of Mg despite greater nuclear charge?","steps":["Mg loses a 3s electron","Al loses a 3p electron","The 3p electron has higher energy and is easier to remove"],"answer":"A change from s to higher-energy p removal creates the discontinuity."},"mistake":"The guide explicitly prefers electron-energy explanations to slogans about “special stability” of half-filled shells.","visual":{"kind":"trend","title":"Ionization-energy discontinuities","labels":["General increase","3s → 3p change","Pairing repulsion","Small discontinuities"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":51,"guidePage":45},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.8","title":"Characteristic transition-element properties","hl":true,"summary":"Incomplete d sublevels support distinctive chemical and physical behaviour.","explain":["Transition elements characteristically form ions with incomplete d sublevels. Their properties include variable oxidation states, coloured compounds, complex-ion formation, catalytic activity, magnetic behaviour and often high melting points.","Do not assume every compound of every d-block element shows every property. An ion with d0 or d10 configuration may not show colour from d–d transitions. Catalysis and complex formation arise from accessible electron arrangements and interaction with other species."],"steps":["Identify incomplete d occupation","Name characteristic properties relevant to the question","Relate a property to electronic structure without overgeneralizing"],"example":{"q":"Why might a d10 ion fail to show the usual transition-ion colour?","steps":["d–d absorption needs an occupied lower d orbital and an accessible higher d orbital","A filled d10 arrangement lacks the usual available d–d transition","No ordinary visible d–d absorption is expected"],"answer":"A filled d sublevel does not support the usual d–d colour mechanism."},"mistake":"“All d-block compounds are coloured” is false.","visual":{"kind":"orbitals","title":"Characteristic transition-element properties","labels":["Incomplete d sublevel","Variable oxidation","Complex ions","Catalysis and colour"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":51,"guidePage":45},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.9","title":"Transition-metal ions and oxidation states","hl":true,"summary":"Similar s/d energies permit more than one stable oxidation state.","explain":["The energies associated with removing successive valence electrons in transition elements can be close enough that different numbers are lost in different compounds. The accessible s and d electrons therefore allow variable oxidation states.","When writing configurations for first-row transition ions, remove 4s electrons before 3d electrons. A neutral atom’s Aufbau filling order is not the same as the electron-removal order from its ions."],"steps":["Write neutral-atom configuration","Remove 4s electrons first","Remove additional electrons from 3d and recount"],"example":{"q":"Give electron configurations of Fe2+ and Fe3+ from Fe = [Ar]3d6 4s2.","steps":["Remove two 4s electrons for Fe2+","This leaves [Ar]3d6","Remove one further 3d electron for Fe3+"],"answer":"Fe2+: [Ar]3d6; Fe3+: [Ar]3d5."},"mistake":"Do not remove 3d electrons before 4s electrons in these cation configurations.","visual":{"kind":"orbitals","title":"Transition-metal ions and oxidation states","labels":["Neutral 3d6 4s2","Lose 4s electrons","Fe2+: 3d6","Fe3+: 3d5"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":51,"guidePage":45},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.1.10","title":"Colour in transition complexes","hl":true,"summary":"Visible-light absorption can promote an electron between split d orbitals.","explain":["Ligands change the energies of the metal’s d orbitals, splitting them into sets. If the energy difference matches a visible photon, absorption can promote an electron between these levels. The detailed splitting patterns for different coordination geometries are not required.","The observed colour is complementary to the absorbed colour. Use the supplied colour wheel to identify the absorbed wavelength range, then f = c/λ to calculate frequency. Remember that nanometres must become metres in SI calculations."],"steps":["Read the observed colour","Use the colour wheel for the complementary absorbed colour","Convert wavelength and calculate frequency if required"],"example":{"q":"A complex absorbs 600 nm light. Estimate the absorbed frequency using c = 3.00 × 10^8 m s−1.","steps":["Convert 600 nm to 6.00 × 10−7 m","Use f = c/λ","f = 3.00 × 10^8 / 6.00 × 10−7"],"answer":"f = 5.00 × 10^14 Hz."},"mistake":"The colour seen is generally not the colour absorbed.","visual":{"kind":"orbitals","title":"Colour in transition complexes","labels":["Ligand field","Split d levels","Absorbed photon","Complementary observed colour"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":51,"guidePage":45},"group":"Structure"}]},{"id":"S3.2","name":"Functional groups: Classification of organic compounds","group":"Structure 3","sections":[{"id":"S3.2.1","title":"Representations of organic molecules","hl":false,"summary":"Different formulas emphasize different information about a molecule.","explain":["A molecular formula counts atoms, whereas a full structural formula shows bonds and a condensed formula groups atoms along the chain. Skeletal drawings omit carbon labels and most hydrogens attached to carbon: each unlabelled line end or vertex represents carbon.","When reading a skeletal formula, add enough hydrogen to give each neutral carbon four bonds. Show heteroatoms such as O, N and halogens explicitly. Three-dimensional models reveal spatial arrangement that flat drawings can hide."],"steps":["Count carbons at vertices and ends","Identify all explicit heteroatoms and multiple bonds","Complete normal carbon valence and count hydrogens"],"example":{"q":"Convert CH3CH2CH2OH to a molecular formula.","steps":["There are three carbon atoms","Hydrogens total 3 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 8","There is one oxygen atom"],"answer":"C3H8O."},"mistake":"Hydrogens on O or N may be explicitly shown and must be counted separately.","visual":{"kind":"molecule","title":"Representations of organic molecules","labels":["Molecular: atom counts","Condensed: grouped atoms","Skeletal: corners = C","3D: spatial positions"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":52,"guidePage":46},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.2","title":"Recognizing functional groups","hl":false,"summary":"Functional groups determine characteristic patterns of reactivity.","explain":["Look for distinctive atom groupings: halogeno C–X; hydroxyl –OH; carbonyl C=O; carboxyl –COOH; alkoxy –OR; amino –NH2; amido –CONH–; ester –COO–; and phenyl C6H5–. A carbonyl alone does not identify every compound class.","Saturated hydrocarbons contain only C–C single bonds; unsaturated hydrocarbons contain C=C or C≡C. Functional groups influence polarity, intermolecular forces and the types of reactions a molecule can undergo."],"steps":["Find heteroatoms and multiple bonds","Identify the whole local group, not one bond only","Classify the compound using its group"],"example":{"q":"Identify the functional group in CH3COOCH2CH3.","steps":["Locate the carbonyl C=O","The carbonyl carbon is bonded to an oxygen linked to another carbon group","The combined –COO– arrangement identifies an ester"],"answer":"The molecule contains an ester functional group."},"mistake":"An ester’s –COO– is not a carboxylic acid unless the single-bonded O is attached to H.","visual":{"kind":"molecule","title":"Recognizing functional groups","labels":["Hydroxyl –OH","Carboxyl –COOH","Ester –COO–","Amide –CONH–"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":52,"guidePage":46},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.3","title":"Homologous series and general formulas","hl":false,"summary":"Members share a functional pattern and differ successively by CH2.","explain":["A homologous series is a family with a common functional group pattern and similar chemical reactions. Successive members normally differ by a CH2 unit and have predictable general formulas. For open-chain single-functional hydrocarbons, alkanes are CnH2n+2, monoalkenes CnH2n and monoalkynes CnH2n−2.","Recognize alkanes, alkenes, alkynes, halogenoalkanes, alcohols, aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids, ethers, amines, amides and esters from their structures. Formula alone may not be sufficient because different series can share a molecular formula."],"steps":["Identify the functional group","Check the carbon skeleton and saturation","Use a suitable general formula with its assumptions"],"example":{"q":"Which series includes CH3CH2CHO?","steps":["The terminal carbon has C=O and is bonded to H","This is a terminal –CHO group","Compounds with this group are aldehydes"],"answer":"Aldehyde homologous series."},"mistake":"CnH2n is not uniquely an alkene formula: cycloalkanes can have it too.","visual":{"kind":"chain","title":"Homologous series and general formulas","labels":["Same functional group","Similar reactions","Successive +CH2","Predictable physical trend"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":52,"guidePage":46},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.4","title":"Physical-property trends in organic families","hl":false,"summary":"Chain length and branching influence intermolecular attractions.","explain":["As chain length increases within a homologous series, electron count and polarizability generally rise, increasing London dispersion forces. This usually increases boiling temperature because more energy is needed to separate molecules.","Branching often reduces the contact area between similarly sized molecules, lowering dispersion interactions and boiling temperature relative to a less-branched isomer. Melting patterns can be less regular because crystal packing also matters; functional groups add further interactions such as hydrogen bonding."],"steps":["Compare functional groups first","Compare chain length or molar mass","For isomers, consider branching and packing"],"example":{"q":"Why does hexane usually boil at a higher temperature than butane?","steps":["Both are non-polar alkanes dominated by dispersion forces","Hexane has more electrons and a larger polarizable electron cloud","Its stronger attractions require more energy to overcome"],"answer":"Stronger London dispersion in the larger molecules."},"mistake":"Do not explain boiling by breaking C–C bonds; molecules remain chemically intact.","visual":{"kind":"trend","title":"Physical-property trends in organic families","labels":["Longer chain","More polarizable electrons","Stronger dispersion","Higher boiling temperature"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":52,"guidePage":46},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.5","title":"IUPAC naming up to six-carbon parents","hl":false,"summary":"Systematic names encode the parent chain, substituents and functional group.","explain":["Choose a parent chain containing the principal functional group and any relevant multiple bond. Number to give the relevant group or unsaturation a low locant, then identify substituents and use the appropriate suffix. This syllabus requires saturated or mono-unsaturated compounds with up to six carbons in the parent chain and one type of specified group.","Examples include halogeno prefixes, –ol for alcohols, –al for aldehydes, –one for ketones and –oic acid for carboxylic acids. Repeated substituents use di-, tri-, tetra-, penta- or hexa-. Make sure names distinguish position and branching."],"steps":["Choose parent chain","Number for the principal group","Locate and name branches and substituents"],"example":{"q":"Name CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3.","steps":["The longest chain contains four carbons: butan-","Number from the end giving OH carbon 2 rather than 3","Use the alcohol suffix –ol with its locant"],"answer":"Butan-2-ol."},"mistake":"Do not choose a longest chain that excludes the principal functional group.","visual":{"kind":"molecule","title":"IUPAC naming up to six-carbon parents","labels":["Parent chain","Number positions","Substituent prefixes","Functional suffix"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":52,"guidePage":46},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.6","title":"Structural isomers and carbon classification","hl":false,"summary":"Structural isomers share a molecular formula but differ in connectivity.","explain":["Structural isomers can differ in branching, position of a functional group or the functional group itself. They contain the same numbers of each atom but connect those atoms differently, which can change both physical and chemical properties.","For alcohols and halogenoalkanes, primary/secondary/tertiary describes the number of carbon groups attached to the carbon carrying OH or halogen. For amines, it describes how many carbon groups are attached directly to nitrogen—these are related ideas but different counting locations."],"steps":["Check equal molecular formulas","Compare connectivity rather than orientation","Identify chain, position or functional-group isomerism"],"example":{"q":"Are ethanol CH3CH2OH and methoxymethane CH3OCH3 structural isomers?","steps":["Both have molecular formula C2H6O","Ethanol contains an alcohol group while methoxymethane is an ether","Their atom connectivities differ"],"answer":"Yes: functional-group structural isomers."},"mistake":"Rotating a single bond does not create a new structural isomer.","visual":{"kind":"molecule","title":"Structural isomers and carbon classification","labels":["Same molecular formula","Different connectivity","Chain or position","Functional-group change"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":53,"guidePage":47},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.7","title":"Stereoisomers, cis–trans and chirality","hl":true,"summary":"The same connectivity can produce distinct arrangements in space.","explain":["Stereoisomers have the same atom connectivity and bond multiplicities but different spatial arrangements. Restricted rotation about C=C allows cis–trans isomerism when each alkene carbon has two different substituents; rings can also restrict arrangement, including C3 and C4 cycloalkanes.","A chiral tetrahedral carbon has four different attached groups and can give non-superimposable mirror-image enantiomers. Enantiomers rotate plane-polarized light oppositely and can behave differently in chiral environments. A racemic mixture contains equal amounts and has no net optical rotation. Use wedge–dash drawings; E/Z naming is not assessed."],"steps":["Check for restricted rotation and appropriate substituents","For chirality, look for four different groups on a tetrahedral carbon","Test whether mirror images superimpose"],"example":{"q":"Why is carbon 2 in butan-2-ol chiral?","steps":["Carbon 2 is tetrahedral","It is attached to H, OH, CH3 and CH2CH3","Those four groups are all different"],"answer":"It is a chiral centre and can form two enantiomers."},"mistake":"A carbon attached to two identical groups is not chiral by the four-different-groups criterion.","visual":{"kind":"molecule","title":"Stereoisomers, cis–trans and chirality","labels":["Same connectivity","Different spatial arrangement","Four different groups","Mirror-image pair"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":53,"guidePage":47},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.8","title":"Organic mass spectra and fragmentation","hl":true,"summary":"Fragment ions reveal structural features of organic molecules.","explain":["The molecular ion forms when an electron is removed from a molecule without initial fragmentation. Its m/z can help identify molecular mass, provided the ion’s charge is known. Fragmentation then breaks bonds to produce smaller positive ions and neutral fragments.","Interpret prominent fragment masses using the data booklet and compare proposed structures against several peaks. A high-intensity peak represents an abundant ion, often relatively stable; the base peak is not automatically the molecular ion."],"steps":["Locate a possible molecular-ion peak","Use fragment m/z values to suggest groups","Check all evidence against candidate structures"],"example":{"q":"A molecular ion is at m/z 46 and a prominent fragment at m/z 31. What information can this provide?","steps":["For a singly charged molecular ion, relative molecular mass is about 46","A 31 fragment may be consistent with CH2OH+ using supplied fragment data","The evidence supports an alcohol candidate but is not sufficient alone to prove one structure"],"answer":"Molecular mass and a possible oxygen-containing fragment narrow the candidates."},"mistake":"Do not identify a whole molecule from one fragment peak alone.","visual":{"kind":"spectrum","title":"Organic mass spectra and fragmentation","labels":["Molecular ion M+","Bond cleavage","Fragment ions","Combine peaks"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":53,"guidePage":47},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.9","title":"IR spectroscopy and bond identification","hl":true,"summary":"IR absorption probes bond vibrations and functional groups.","explain":["Infrared radiation can be absorbed when its energy matches a molecular vibration that changes dipole moment. Different bonds and bonding environments absorb in characteristic wavenumber ranges, so the functional-group region of a spectrum helps identify groups.","Use the data booklet rather than relying on guessed exact numbers. A broad O–H absorption and a strong C=O absorption provide different clues. Greenhouse gases absorb outgoing terrestrial infrared in suitable vibrational modes, helping explain their influence on energy transfer through the atmosphere."],"steps":["Inspect broad versus sharp absorptions","Compare characteristic ranges with the data booklet","Combine presence and absence of key bands"],"example":{"q":"An IR spectrum shows a strong carbonyl band and a very broad acid O–H absorption. Which group is likely?","steps":["A strong C=O band identifies a carbonyl-containing structure","A broad O–H band in the carboxylic-acid region suggests acidic OH","Together these indicate –COOH"],"answer":"A carboxylic acid group."},"mistake":"Do not assume every bond absorbs all IR; the vibration must involve a dipole change.","visual":{"kind":"spectrum","title":"IR spectroscopy and bond identification","labels":["IR radiation","Bond vibration","Dipole change","Functional-group signature"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":53,"guidePage":47},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.10","title":"Proton NMR: signals, shifts and integration","hl":true,"summary":"1H NMR distinguishes hydrogen chemical environments.","explain":["Hydrogen nuclei in different electronic environments respond at different chemical shifts in a magnetic field. Chemically equivalent hydrogens give the same signal, so the number of distinct signals reveals the number of hydrogen environments.","The integration ratio indicates relative numbers of hydrogens in each environment. Chemical shift values suggest neighbouring atoms or groups; use the data booklet and combine shifts with integrals instead of interpreting one feature in isolation."],"steps":["Count chemically different H environments","Read relative integrations","Use chemical shifts to identify likely surroundings"],"example":{"q":"Why does CH3OCH3 have one main proton environment?","steps":["The two methyl groups are related by molecular symmetry","All six methyl hydrogens are chemically equivalent in the usual spectrum","Equivalent hydrogens contribute to the same signal"],"answer":"One signal integrating for six hydrogens."},"mistake":"Signal count is the number of chemical environments, not the number of hydrogen atoms.","visual":{"kind":"spectrum","title":"Proton NMR: signals, shifts and integration","labels":["Different environments","Different chemical shifts","Signal count","Integration ratio"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":53,"guidePage":47},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.11","title":"Proton NMR splitting","hl":true,"summary":"Neighbouring hydrogens split signals into characteristic multiplets.","explain":["In simple first-order cases, a proton set with n equivalent neighbouring hydrogens on an adjacent carbon gives n + 1 peaks: singlet, doublet, triplet or quartet for zero, one, two or three such neighbours. Equivalent protons do not split one another.","Splitting gives local connectivity information that complements integration. Exchangeable O–H or N–H signals may behave differently and often do not follow the simple rule reliably; interpret the provided data rather than forcing every peak into it."],"steps":["Identify equivalent proton sets","Count adjacent equivalent H neighbours","Apply n + 1 and compare with integration"],"example":{"q":"Predict the splitting of the two carbon-bound proton sets in CH3CH2Br.","steps":["CH3 hydrogens have two equivalent adjacent CH2 hydrogens","They give a triplet","CH2 hydrogens have three adjacent CH3 hydrogens and give a quartet"],"answer":"CH3 triplet and CH2 quartet, integration 3:2."},"mistake":"A triplet is one signal split into three peaks, not three independent chemical environments.","visual":{"kind":"spectrum","title":"Proton NMR splitting","labels":["0 neighbours: singlet","1: doublet","2: triplet","3: quartet"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":54,"guidePage":48},"group":"Structure"},{"id":"S3.2.12","title":"Combining analytical evidence","hl":true,"summary":"A secure structure must satisfy all available analytical data.","explain":["Molecular formula, mass spectrum, IR and proton NMR each answer a different question. Molecular mass and formula constrain composition; IR suggests functional groups; NMR shows hydrogen environments, counts and neighbours; fragmentation adds structural clues.","Generate plausible candidate structures and reject those contradicting any strong observation. A good answer explains why one structure fits the complete pattern, not merely why it could produce a single peak."],"steps":["Establish composition and molecular mass","Identify likely functional groups from IR","Use NMR signals, integration and splitting to connect groups","Check fragments and all remaining data"],"example":{"q":"A C2H6O molecule has an O–H IR band and carbon-bound NMR signals with a 3:2 integration and triplet/quartet pattern. Identify it.","steps":["C2H6O allows ethanol or an ether","O–H absorption favours an alcohol","A CH3CH2 unit explains the triplet/quartet with integration 3:2"],"answer":"Ethanol, CH3CH2OH."},"mistake":"A candidate that matches IR but contradicts NMR is not a valid final identification.","visual":{"kind":"spectrum","title":"Combining analytical evidence","labels":["Formula + mass","IR functional group","NMR connectivity","One consistent structure"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":54,"guidePage":48},"group":"Structure"}]},{"id":"R1.1","name":"Measuring enthalpy changes","group":"Reactivity 1","sections":[{"id":"R1.1.1","title":"System, surroundings, heat and temperature","hl":false,"summary":"Energy transfers between system and surroundings while total energy is conserved.","explain":["The system is the part of the universe being studied, such as the chemicals reacting in a calorimeter. Everything else is the surroundings. Energy leaving one enters the other, so a temperature change does not mean energy has been created or destroyed.","Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference; temperature measures average particle kinetic energy. Two samples can have the same temperature but different total thermal energies because they contain different amounts of matter."],"steps":["Define the system boundary","Identify the measured surroundings","Describe transfer and conserve total energy"],"example":{"q":"A reaction warms the water around it. Where did the water’s energy come from?","steps":["Define the reacting chemicals as the system","Water is part of the surroundings","Energy transferred from the system to the water"],"answer":"The system lost energy while the surroundings gained it."},"mistake":"Heat and temperature are different physical quantities.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"System, surroundings, heat and temperature","labels":["Reacting system","Energy transfer","Surrounding water","Total energy conserved"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":54,"guidePage":48},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.1.2","title":"Exothermic and endothermic reactions","hl":false,"summary":"The sign of enthalpy depends on the direction of heat transfer.","explain":["An exothermic reaction transfers heat from the reacting system to the surroundings; the surroundings often warm and the reaction enthalpy change is negative. An endothermic reaction absorbs heat from the surroundings, often cooling them, and has positive enthalpy change.","Always state whose temperature is measured. A thermometer in solution measures part of the surroundings relative to the chemical reaction; its warming indicates energy release by the system, not a positive reaction enthalpy."],"steps":["Identify whether surroundings warm or cool","Infer direction of heat transfer","Assign negative ΔH for exothermic and positive ΔH for endothermic"],"example":{"q":"A reaction mixture cools from 22°C to 17°C. Classify the reaction.","steps":["The measured surroundings lose thermal energy","The reaction system absorbs that energy","Energy absorption corresponds to positive enthalpy change"],"answer":"Endothermic, ΔH > 0."},"mistake":"A temperature decrease does not mean the reaction enthalpy change is negative.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"Exothermic and endothermic reactions","labels":["Exothermic: heat out","Surroundings warm","Endothermic: heat in","Surroundings cool"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":54,"guidePage":48},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.1.3","title":"Energy profiles and relative stability","hl":false,"summary":"Product and reactant energy levels determine the sign of ΔH.","explain":["An energy profile plots potential energy on the vertical axis against reaction coordinate on the horizontal axis. Exothermic reactions end with products below reactants; endothermic reactions end with products above reactants.","A lower-energy chemical arrangement is more stable relative to a higher-energy one under this simple comparison. The peak represents an energy barrier, but the overall enthalpy difference is between product and reactant levels, not between reactants and the peak."],"steps":["Label axes correctly","Place reactant and product energy levels","Draw ΔH from reactants to products and assign sign"],"example":{"q":"Products lie 75 kJ mol−1 below reactants. What is ΔH and which side is lower in energy?","steps":["Calculate final minus initial energy","The product level is 75 lower","Therefore ΔH is negative"],"answer":"ΔH = −75 kJ mol−1; products are lower in energy."},"mistake":"Reaction coordinate is not time and the peak height is not the overall enthalpy change.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"Energy profiles and relative stability","labels":["Reactants","Energy barrier","Products","ΔH = final − initial"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":54,"guidePage":48},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.1.4","title":"Calorimetry calculations","hl":false,"summary":"Temperature change of surroundings can reveal reaction enthalpy.","explain":["A calorimeter estimates heat absorbed by a known mass using Q = mcΔT, where c is specific heat capacity. At constant pressure, reaction enthalpy per mole is estimated from ΔH = −Q/n if Q measures heat gained by the surroundings.","Use mass in grams with c in J g−1 K−1, then convert joules to kilojoules for ΔH. Heat loss, calorimeter absorption and incomplete combustion can make measured changes smaller in magnitude than theoretical predictions."],"steps":["Compute ΔT = final − initial","Calculate Q = mcΔT for the measured material","Divide by reacted moles, reverse sign and convert J to kJ"],"example":{"q":"50.0 g water warms by 10.0 K when 0.0250 mol reacts. Use c = 4.18 J g−1 K−1. Find ΔH.","steps":["Q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 10.0 = 2090 J","The water gains heat, so the reaction loses 2090 J","ΔH = −2090/0.0250 = −83,600 J mol−1"],"answer":"ΔH = −83.6 kJ mol−1."},"mistake":"Q for water and ΔH for the reacting system have opposite signs.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Calorimetry calculations","labels":["m c ΔT","Q surroundings","−Q / n","kJ mol−1"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":54,"guidePage":48},"group":"Reactivity"}]},{"id":"R1.2","name":"Energy cycles in reactions","group":"Reactivity 1","sections":[{"id":"R1.2.1","title":"Average bond enthalpy calculations","hl":false,"summary":"Breaking bonds absorbs energy; making bonds releases it.","explain":["To estimate a reaction enthalpy, imagine first breaking all relevant reactant bonds and then making product bonds. Energy input is positive for bond breaking, while the energy released on formation is subtracted.","Use ΔH ≈ ΣE(bonds broken) − ΣE(bonds formed). Average bond enthalpies apply to gaseous bonds and average across different chemical environments, so estimates may differ from measured enthalpies involving particular molecules and states."],"steps":["Balance the equation and draw structures","Count broken and formed bonds with coefficients","Subtract total formed-bond energies from broken-bond energies"],"example":{"q":"Estimate ΔH for H2 + Cl2 → 2HCl using H–H 436, Cl–Cl 243 and H–Cl 431 kJ mol−1.","steps":["Bonds broken: 436 + 243 = 679 kJ mol−1","Bonds formed: 2 × 431 = 862 kJ mol−1","ΔH = 679 − 862"],"answer":"ΔH ≈ −183 kJ mol−1."},"mistake":"Bond breaking never releases energy by itself; bond formation is the releasing step.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"Average bond enthalpy calculations","labels":["Break: +679","Form: −862","Net: −183","Average gaseous values"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":55,"guidePage":49},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.2.2","title":"Hess’s law","hl":false,"summary":"Enthalpy change depends on initial and final states, not the route.","explain":["Hess’s law follows conservation of energy. If two routes connect the same initial and final states, their total enthalpy changes must agree; otherwise a cycle could create energy from nothing.","Manipulate known equations so their sum gives the target reaction. Reversing an equation changes the sign of ΔH; multiplying an equation multiplies ΔH by the same number. Species appearing on both sides cancel."],"steps":["Write the target equation","Reverse or scale known equations as necessary","Add equations and their adjusted enthalpies"],"example":{"q":"If A → B has ΔH = +40 and B → C has ΔH = −90 kJ mol−1, find A → C.","steps":["Add the two equations","Intermediate B cancels","Add enthalpies: +40 − 90"],"answer":"ΔH(A → C) = −50 kJ mol−1."},"mistake":"Do not reverse a reaction without reversing its enthalpy sign.","visual":{"kind":"cycle","title":"Hess’s law","labels":["A → B: +40","B → C: −90","Cancel intermediate","A → C: −50"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":55,"guidePage":49},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.2.3","title":"Standard formation and combustion enthalpies","hl":true,"summary":"Definitions fix the amount and physical states used in thermochemical equations.","explain":["Standard enthalpy of formation is the change when one mole of a substance forms from its elements in their standard states. The formation enthalpy of an element in its standard state is defined as zero.","Standard enthalpy of combustion refers to complete burning of one mole of a substance in oxygen under standard conditions and states. Carefully specify phases, since formation of liquid water differs in enthalpy from formation of water vapour."],"steps":["Identify whether formation or combustion is requested","Use exactly one mole of the defined substance","Balance with correct standard-state elements and products"],"example":{"q":"Write the formation equation for liquid water.","steps":["Use elements in their standard states: H2(g) and O2(g)","Form exactly one mole H2O(l)","Balance oxygen with coefficient 1/2"],"answer":"H2(g) + 1/2 O2(g) → H2O(l)."},"mistake":"Formation equations may use fractional coefficients to produce one mole of product.","visual":{"kind":"cycle","title":"Standard formation and combustion enthalpies","labels":["Elements: standard states","One mole product","Specified physical state","ΔHf°"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":55,"guidePage":49},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.2.4","title":"Formation and combustion data in cycles","hl":true,"summary":"Hess’s law leads to useful sum formulas with opposite subtraction orders.","explain":["From formation data, ΔHreaction = ΣνΔHf(products) − ΣνΔHf(reactants). Formation routes start from the same elemental reference, so subtracting reactant formation energies gives the change from reactants to products.","From combustion data, ΔHreaction = ΣνΔHc(reactants) − ΣνΔHc(products), provided both sides combust to the same final products. The order reverses because both known routes go downward toward common combustion products."],"steps":["Balance the target equation","Choose the correct formula and multiply by coefficients","Substitute signed values and retain units"],"example":{"q":"For CO + 1/2O2 → CO2, use ΔHf(CO) = −111 and ΔHf(CO2) = −394 kJ mol−1. Find ΔH.","steps":["Product formation sum = −394","Reactant sum = −111 + 0 for O2","Subtract: −394 − (−111)"],"answer":"ΔH = −283 kJ mol−1."},"mistake":"Do not swap the subtraction orders for formation data and combustion data.","visual":{"kind":"cycle","title":"Formation and combustion data in cycles","labels":["Formation: products − reactants","Combustion: reactants − products","Include coefficients","Keep signs"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":55,"guidePage":49},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.2.5","title":"Interpreting Born–Haber cycles","hl":true,"summary":"Ionic formation is related to atomization, ionization, electron affinity and lattice changes.","explain":["A Born–Haber cycle applies Hess’s law to ionic-solid formation through gaseous atoms and gaseous ions. Steps may include sublimation or atomization, bond dissociation, ionization energies, electron affinities and lattice enthalpy.","Check whether lattice enthalpy is defined for formation of a solid from gaseous ions or dissociation into gaseous ions: the signs are opposite. Divalent ions require the appropriate successive ionization energies and electron-affinity steps. The syllabus requires interpretation and calculation, not construction of a complete cycle from memory."],"steps":["Read each arrow and definition","Include stoichiometric multiples and successive steps","Equate total indirect route with formation enthalpy"],"example":{"q":"A cycle has formation ΔH = −400, atomization total +200, IE +500 and EA −350 kJ mol−1. Find lattice formation enthalpy.","steps":["Add known indirect steps: 200 + 500 − 350 = +350","Set −400 = +350 + lattice formation enthalpy","Rearrange: lattice = −400 − 350"],"answer":"Lattice formation enthalpy = −750 kJ mol−1."},"mistake":"Always check the stated lattice-enthalpy direction before assigning a sign.","visual":{"kind":"cycle","title":"Interpreting Born–Haber cycles","labels":["Atomize","Ionize / electron affinity","Gaseous ions","Lattice formation"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":55,"guidePage":49},"group":"Reactivity"}]},{"id":"R1.3","name":"Energy from fuels","group":"Reactivity 1","sections":[{"id":"R1.3.1","title":"Complete combustion equations","hl":false,"summary":"Combustion in sufficient oxygen commonly forms stable oxides.","explain":["Reactive metals and non-metals can react with oxygen to form oxides. Complete combustion of hydrocarbons and alcohols produces carbon dioxide and water when oxygen is sufficiently available.","Balance carbon first, hydrogen second and oxygen last. Alcohol fuels already contain oxygen, so count oxygen atoms present in the fuel before deciding the oxygen coefficient. Physical states may affect subsequent thermochemical interpretation."],"steps":["Write expected complete-combustion products","Balance C and H","Balance O last and use whole coefficients if convenient"],"example":{"q":"Balance complete combustion of ethanol, C2H5OH.","steps":["Two carbon atoms require 2CO2","Six H atoms require 3H2O","Products contain 7 O atoms; ethanol supplies 1, leaving 6 from 3O2"],"answer":"C2H5OH + 3O2 → 2CO2 + 3H2O."},"mistake":"Do not ignore the oxygen already contained in an alcohol.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Complete combustion equations","labels":["Fuel + O2","Carbon → CO2","Hydrogen → H2O","Balance oxygen last"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":56,"guidePage":50},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.3.2","title":"Incomplete combustion","hl":false,"summary":"Insufficient oxygen can produce carbon monoxide and solid carbon.","explain":["When oxygen supply is limited, carbon in an organic fuel may not oxidize fully to CO2. Products may include CO, carbon particles and mixtures of partially oxidized compounds, together with water.","Carbon monoxide is a serious toxic pollutant; soot particles can harm health and reduce combustion efficiency. Which incomplete-combustion equation is appropriate depends on the specified carbon-containing products; there is not just one universal equation."],"steps":["Read which products the question specifies","Balance C and H","Balance O and compare oxygen requirement with complete combustion"],"example":{"q":"Balance combustion of methane producing CO and water.","steps":["One carbon atom gives one CO","Four hydrogen atoms give 2H2O","Products need three O atoms per CH4; multiply all by 2"],"answer":"2CH4 + 3O2 → 2CO + 4H2O."},"mistake":"Do not assume incomplete combustion always produces only soot or only CO.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Incomplete combustion","labels":["Limited oxygen","CO formation","Carbon / soot","Less complete oxidation"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":56,"guidePage":50},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.3.3","title":"Comparing fossil fuels","hl":false,"summary":"Fuel evaluation includes energy, carbon emissions, pollution and resource limits.","explain":["Coal, crude-oil fuels and natural gas release chemical energy on combustion but are non-renewable on human timescales. Compare their energy per unit mass and the quantity of CO2 produced per unit energy, not only CO2 per mole.","Larger carbon-rich fuels can have greater tendency toward incomplete combustion under poor mixing. CO2 absorbs appropriate outgoing infrared radiation and contributes to the greenhouse effect. Environmental comparisons should also consider extraction, leakage, transport and other pollutants."],"steps":["Balance combustion and find CO2 moles","Convert to mass or per-energy basis as required","Compare with wider environmental and practical factors"],"example":{"q":"How much CO2 is produced by complete combustion of one mole of methane?","steps":["CH4 + 2O2 → CO2 + 2H2O","One mole CH4 produces one mole CO2","M(CO2) is approximately 44.01 g mol−1"],"answer":"Approximately 44.01 g CO2 per mole CH4."},"mistake":"Lower CO2 per mole of fuel is not automatically lower CO2 per kJ of useful energy.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"Comparing fossil fuels","labels":["Energy released","CO2 per energy","Incomplete combustion","Finite resources"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":56,"guidePage":50},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.3.4","title":"Biofuels and renewable carbon","hl":false,"summary":"Photosynthesis fixes carbon recently enough for biomass to replenish.","explain":["Photosynthesis converts carbon dioxide and water into organic material using light energy. A simplified equation is 6CO2 + 6H2O → C6H12O6 + 6O2. Biofuels derive from such recently fixed biological carbon rather than ancient fossil carbon.","Biofuels may be renewable if their sources can regrow sustainably, but they are not automatically carbon-neutral. Farming, fertilizer, processing and transport use energy, while land-use change and competition with food production can be important disadvantages."],"steps":["Explain the source and timescale of carbon fixation","Distinguish renewable resource from zero emissions","Evaluate life-cycle benefits and costs"],"example":{"q":"Why can a biofuel be renewable but still cause net greenhouse-gas emissions?","steps":["Its biomass can regrow and replace harvested material","Production and transport may consume fossil energy","Land-use change may release stored carbon"],"answer":"Renewability does not guarantee zero life-cycle emissions."},"mistake":"Never call a fuel carbon-neutral solely because a plant originally absorbed CO2.","visual":{"kind":"cycle","title":"Biofuels and renewable carbon","labels":["Photosynthesis","Recently fixed carbon","Regrowth potential","Life-cycle emissions"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":56,"guidePage":50},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.3.5","title":"Hydrogen and methanol fuel cells","hl":false,"summary":"Fuel cells continuously convert supplied chemical fuel into electrical energy.","explain":["A fuel cell separates oxidation and reduction at electrodes so electrons can travel through an external circuit. Fresh fuel and oxidant are supplied continuously; unlike a sealed primary cell, operation can continue while reactants are provided.","In acidic conditions, hydrogen oxidation is H2 → 2H+ + 2e−, and oxygen reduction is O2 + 4H+ + 4e− → 2H2O. Methanol oxidation can be written CH3OH + H2O → CO2 + 6H+ + 6e−. Half-equations depend on acidic or alkaline conditions, so follow the medium specified."],"steps":["Identify the supplied fuel and electrolyte conditions","Balance oxidation and oxygen-reduction half-equations","Equalize electrons and add to obtain the overall equation"],"example":{"q":"Combine acidic hydrogen fuel-cell half-equations.","steps":["Multiply H2 → 2H+ + 2e− by two","Add O2 + 4H+ + 4e− → 2H2O","Cancel electrons and H+"],"answer":"2H2 + O2 → 2H2O."},"mistake":"Fuel-cell electrodes are where electron-transfer reactions occur; electrons travel through the external circuit, not through the electrolyte.","visual":{"kind":"cell","title":"Hydrogen and methanol fuel cells","labels":["Fuel oxidation","Electrons through circuit","Oxygen reduction","Continuous supply"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":57,"guidePage":51},"group":"Reactivity"}]},{"id":"R1.4","name":"Entropy and spontaneity","group":"Reactivity 1","sections":[{"id":"R1.4.1","title":"Entropy and dispersal","hl":true,"summary":"Entropy measures the ways matter and energy can be distributed.","explain":["Entropy increases when energy and matter can be distributed among more possible arrangements. Under comparable conditions, a gas usually has greater molar entropy than a liquid, which in turn exceeds a solid. More gas particles often mean greater entropy, but detailed data provide the quantitative answer.","Calculate a standard entropy change using ΔS° = ΣνS°(products) − ΣνS°(reactants). Values are commonly in J K−1 mol−1; unlike formation enthalpy, an element’s standard molar entropy at ordinary temperature is generally not zero."],"steps":["Predict dispersal from phases and gas-particle counts","Multiply standard entropies by coefficients","Subtract reactant sum from product sum"],"example":{"q":"For A(g) → 2B(g), S°(A) = 200 and S°(B) = 130 J K−1 mol−1. Find ΔS°.","steps":["Product entropy sum = 2 × 130 = 260","Reactant sum = 200","ΔS° = 260 − 200"],"answer":"ΔS° = +60 J K−1 mol−1."},"mistake":"Entropy is not simply “messiness”; link it to distributions of energy and matter.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"Entropy and dispersal","labels":["More possible distributions","Phase matters","Gas particle counts","Products − reactants"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":57,"guidePage":51},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.4.2","title":"Gibbs energy calculations","hl":true,"summary":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS combines enthalpy and entropy effects.","explain":["Gibbs energy change relates reaction enthalpy and entropy at a specified absolute temperature. Under constant temperature and pressure, it provides a criterion for thermodynamic direction. The standard form is ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS°.","Match units before substituting. If ΔH is kJ mol−1 and ΔS is J K−1 mol−1, divide ΔS by 1000 before calculating TΔS. Use kelvin, never Celsius. The sign of the result is as important as the magnitude."],"steps":["Convert temperature to K","Put ΔH and TΔS in matching energy units","Substitute with signs and calculate"],"example":{"q":"At 298 K, ΔH° = −50.0 kJ mol−1 and ΔS° = −100 J K−1 mol−1. Find ΔG°.","steps":["Convert ΔS° to −0.100 kJ K−1 mol−1","TΔS° = 298 × (−0.100) = −29.8 kJ mol−1","ΔG° = −50.0 − (−29.8)"],"answer":"ΔG° = −20.2 kJ mol−1."},"mistake":"The minus sign in ΔH − TΔS must be applied even when ΔS is negative.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Gibbs energy calculations","labels":["ΔH term","T in kelvin","Convert ΔS units","ΔG = ΔH − TΔS"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":57,"guidePage":51},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.4.3","title":"Spontaneity and temperature thresholds","hl":true,"summary":"A negative Gibbs energy change favours forward thermodynamic change.","explain":["At constant temperature and pressure, ΔG < 0 indicates a thermodynamically spontaneous forward change, while ΔG > 0 favours the reverse and ΔG = 0 indicates equilibrium. Spontaneous does not mean fast: a large activation barrier can make a favourable reaction very slow.","At a threshold temperature, set ΔG = 0, so T = ΔH/ΔS with consistent units. If ΔH and ΔS are both positive, higher temperature favours spontaneity; if both are negative, lower temperature favours it. Opposite signs give temperature-independent tendencies within the assumption of constant values."],"steps":["Inspect ΔH and ΔS signs","Set ΔG = 0 to calculate threshold","Test above or below the threshold using the inequality"],"example":{"q":"For ΔH = +40.0 kJ mol−1 and ΔS = +100 J K−1 mol−1, above what temperature is forward change favoured?","steps":["Convert ΔS to +0.100 kJ K−1 mol−1","Set 0 = 40.0 − T(0.100), giving T = 400 K","For T > 400 K, TΔS exceeds ΔH"],"answer":"Forward change is favoured above 400 K."},"mistake":"Thermodynamic spontaneity does not guarantee an observable rapid reaction.","visual":{"kind":"trend","title":"Spontaneity and temperature thresholds","labels":["ΔG < 0: forward","ΔG = 0: equilibrium","Threshold T = ΔH/ΔS","Kinetics is separate"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":57,"guidePage":51},"group":"Reactivity"},{"id":"R1.4.4","title":"Gibbs energy and equilibrium","hl":true,"summary":"Reaction composition changes ΔG until equilibrium is reached.","explain":["Standard Gibbs energy ΔG° refers to standard states, but actual Gibbs energy depends on the mixture composition: ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln Q. As a forward-favoured reaction progresses, Q changes and ΔG approaches zero.","At equilibrium Q = K and ΔG = 0, giving ΔG° = −RT ln K. Thus K > 1 corresponds to negative ΔG°, while K < 1 corresponds to positive ΔG°. Use R = 8.31 J mol−1 K−1 and express ΔG° in joules if using this value."],"steps":["Distinguish actual ΔG from standard ΔG°","Use ΔG = 0 and Q = K at equilibrium","Calculate K or ΔG° with matching units"],"example":{"q":"At 298 K, K = 10.0. Estimate ΔG° using R = 8.31 J mol−1 K−1.","steps":["Use ΔG° = −RT ln K","Substitute −8.31 × 298 × ln(10.0)","This is approximately −5700 J mol−1"],"answer":"ΔG° ≈ −5.70 kJ mol−1."},"mistake":"At equilibrium actual ΔG is zero, but ΔG° need not be zero unless K = 1.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Gibbs energy and equilibrium","labels":["Composition Q changes","ΔG approaches zero","At equilibrium Q = K","ΔG° = −RT ln K"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":57,"guidePage":51},"group":"Reactivity"}]},{"id":"R2.1","name":"How much? The amount of chemical change","group":"Reactivity 2","sections":[{"id":"R2.1.1","title":"Balancing chemical equations","hl":false,"summary":"Balanced equations conserve atoms and show reacting mole ratios.","explain":["A balanced chemical equation is a bookkeeping statement: atoms are rearranged, but none are created or destroyed. The large coefficients in front of formulas count relative numbers of particles or moles. Subscripts inside a formula instead describe the chemical identity and must not be changed to balance an equation.","Include state symbols when the states are known: (s), (l), (g) and (aq). A solution labelled aqueous contains dissolved particles; it does not mean the solute itself is a pure liquid."],"steps":["Write correct formulas for every stated reactant and product.","Count atoms of each element on each side; change only coefficients.","Reduce to the smallest whole-number coefficients and add appropriate state symbols."],"example":{"q":"Balance the combustion equation C₂H₆(g) + O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + H₂O(l).","steps":["Balance C: C₂H₆ + O₂ → 2CO₂ + H₂O.","Balance H: C₂H₆ + O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O.","Seven O atoms are needed, so multiply all coefficients by two to avoid 3.5 O₂."],"answer":"2C₂H₆(g) + 7O₂(g) → 4CO₂(g) + 6H₂O(l)."},"mistake":"Never turn H₂O into H₂O₂ to balance oxygen: that changes the product.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Balancing chemical equations","labels":["2 C₂H₆","7 O₂","4 CO₂","6 H₂O","Atoms conserved"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":58,"guidePage":52},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.1.2","title":"Reacting masses, gas volumes and concentrations","hl":false,"summary":"Use a balanced equation to connect measurable quantities through moles.","explain":["Stoichiometry uses the coefficients of a balanced equation as a mole-to-mole conversion. The calculation usually follows a bridge: measured reactant quantity → reactant moles → product moles → required product quantity.","Convert a mass using n = m/M, a solution using n = CV with compatible volume units, and gases using an appropriate molar volume or PV = nRT. At the same temperature and pressure, gas volume ratios equal their mole ratios."],"steps":["Balance the equation and convert the known quantity into moles.","Multiply by the required coefficient divided by the known coefficient.","Convert the resulting moles into mass, concentration or gas volume as requested."],"example":{"q":"What mass of CO₂ forms from complete combustion of 0.500 mol CH₄? Use M(CO₂) = 44.01 g mol⁻¹.","steps":["CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O.","The CH₄:CO₂ mole ratio is 1:1, so n(CO₂) = 0.500 mol.","m = nM = 0.500 × 44.01 = 22.005 g."],"answer":"22.0 g CO₂ to three significant figures."},"mistake":"Coefficients give mole ratios, not generally mass ratios.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Reacting masses, gas volumes and concentrations","labels":["Measured quantity","Moles of known","Equation ratio","Moles required","Required quantity"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":58,"guidePage":52},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.1.3","title":"Limiting reactants and theoretical yield","hl":false,"summary":"The reactant that runs out first limits how much product can form.","explain":["If reactants are not supplied in the exact balanced ratio, one is consumed first. This is the limiting reactant; another may remain in excess. The theoretical yield is the greatest quantity of product allowed by the supplied amounts and the balanced equation.","A practical method is to divide each reactant amount in moles by its stoichiometric coefficient. The smallest result identifies the reactant that permits the fewest complete reaction units. Experimental yield is what is actually isolated, which may be lower because of incomplete reaction or losses."],"steps":["Find the moles of each reactant.","Divide each mole amount by its equation coefficient and identify the smallest value.","Use only the limiting reactant to calculate the theoretical amount of product."],"example":{"q":"For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, identify the limiting reagent when 2.0 mol N₂ and 3.0 mol H₂ are supplied.","steps":["N₂: 2.0/1 = 2.0 reaction units.","H₂: 3.0/3 = 1.0 reaction unit, the smaller value.","H₂ limits the reaction; 3.0 × 2/3 = 2.0 mol NH₃ forms theoretically."],"answer":"H₂ is limiting; theoretical yield is 2.0 mol NH₃."},"mistake":"The smaller mass is not necessarily the limiting reactant. Compare stoichiometrically adjusted mole amounts.","visual":{"kind":"ratio","title":"Limiting reactants and theoretical yield","labels":["N₂: 2.0 ÷ 1","H₂: 3.0 ÷ 3","Smallest ratio limits","2.0 mol NH₃"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":58,"guidePage":52},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.1.4","title":"Percentage yield","hl":false,"summary":"Percentage yield compares the amount isolated with the maximum predicted amount.","explain":["The percentage yield is experimental yield divided by theoretical yield, multiplied by 100%. The two yields must describe the same product in the same units. A real preparation may lose product during transfer, filtration or purification, or may not react completely.","A yield above 100% does not mean extra atoms were created. It suggests an impure or wet product, a measurement error, or an incorrect theoretical-yield calculation. Correctly identify the limiting reactant before calculating the theoretical yield."],"steps":["Calculate theoretical yield from the limiting reactant.","Ensure actual and theoretical quantities use identical units.","Calculate experimental/theoretical × 100 and interpret the result."],"example":{"q":"A preparation should produce 8.00 g of pure product but isolates 6.20 g. Calculate percentage yield.","steps":["Theoretical yield = 8.00 g.","Experimental yield = 6.20 g.","Yield = 6.20/8.00 × 100 = 77.5%."],"answer":"77.5%."},"mistake":"Do not divide theoretical by experimental yield; that reverses the definition.","visual":{"kind":"ratio","title":"Percentage yield","labels":["Experimental: 6.20 g","Theoretical: 8.00 g","× 100","77.5% yield"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":58,"guidePage":52},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.1.5","title":"Atom economy and green chemistry","hl":false,"summary":"Atom economy measures how much reactant mass can end up in the desired product.","explain":["Atom economy is a property of the balanced reaction, not a measurement of laboratory recovery. Calculate the molar mass contribution of the desired product, including its coefficient, divided by the sum of all reactant molar-mass contributions, multiplied by 100%.","A high atom economy means less of the starting material becomes unwanted products. It is only one measure of a useful process: yield, energy demand, hazardous reagents, solvent use and recycling also matter. A reaction can have excellent atom economy but poor experimental yield."],"steps":["Balance the equation and identify the desired product.","Multiply each molar mass by its coefficient.","Divide the desired-product mass contribution by total reactant contribution and multiply by 100."],"example":{"q":"Estimate atom economy for CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ when CaO is desired. Use masses 100.09 and 56.08 g mol⁻¹.","steps":["The equation is balanced with all coefficients equal to one.","Desired product contribution = 56.08 g per mole of reaction.","Atom economy = 56.08/100.09 × 100 ≈ 56.0%."],"answer":"Approximately 56.0%."},"mistake":"Do not substitute experimental product mass into atom economy; that belongs to percentage yield.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Atom economy and green chemistry","labels":["Reactant mass","Desired product","Unwanted CO₂","56.0% atom economy"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":58,"guidePage":52},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"}]},{"id":"R2.2","name":"How fast? The rate of chemical change","group":"Reactivity 2","sections":[{"id":"R2.2.1","title":"Measuring reaction rates","hl":false,"summary":"Rate is the change in a measured quantity per unit time; concentration is the defining quantity.","explain":["The rate of appearance of a product can be written as Δ[product]/Δt. For a disappearing reactant, −Δ[reactant]/Δt is positive. Average rate refers to a time interval; instantaneous rate at a particular time is the gradient of a tangent to the curve.","Concentration changes are often followed indirectly. A gas syringe gives gas volume, a balance can monitor gas mass loss, and colour or light transmission can track a changing solution. These raw rates may be reported in cm³ s⁻¹ or g s⁻¹ before conversion to concentration units."],"steps":["Identify the measured variable and its units.","For an average rate, divide the change by elapsed time.","For instantaneous rate, draw a tangent at the chosen time and calculate its gradient using widely separated points on that tangent."],"example":{"q":"A product concentration rises from 0.020 to 0.065 mol dm⁻³ in 15.0 s. Find the average formation rate.","steps":["Change in concentration = 0.065 − 0.020 = 0.045 mol dm⁻³.","Time interval = 15.0 s.","Rate = 0.045/15.0 = 0.0030 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹."],"answer":"3.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹."},"mistake":"A chord between two points gives an average rate, not the instantaneous rate at one of them.","visual":{"kind":"graph","title":"Measuring reaction rates","labels":["Concentration","Time","Average: chord","Instantaneous: tangent"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":58,"guidePage":52},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.2","title":"Collision theory","hl":false,"summary":"A reaction requires collisions with sufficient energy and suitable orientation.","explain":["Particles must encounter each other to react, but not every encounter succeeds. A collision needs enough energy to overcome the activation barrier and a geometry that allows the necessary bonds to be broken and formed.","Kelvin temperature is related to average kinetic energy. Heating increases the average particle speed, but its most important rate effect is often the greater fraction of particles with enough energy for successful collision. A statement about more collisions alone is incomplete."],"steps":["State that reacting particles must collide.","Explain the energy requirement relative to activation energy.","Explain that collision orientation must also permit the chemical rearrangement."],"example":{"q":"Why can two molecules collide without forming products?","steps":["Their collision may have less energy than the activation barrier.","Alternatively, they may have sufficient energy but approach in an unsuitable orientation.","Either failure means no successful reaction event."],"answer":"Sufficient energy and suitable orientation are both required."},"mistake":"Never say that every collision leads to reaction.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Collision theory","labels":["Collision","Energy ≥ Eₐ?","Correct orientation?","Successful reaction"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":59,"guidePage":53},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.3","title":"Factors controlling rate","hl":false,"summary":"Conditions change collision frequency or the proportion of successful collisions.","explain":["Increasing solution concentration or gas pressure puts more particles into a given volume, increasing collision frequency. Increasing the exposed surface area of a solid gives reacting particles access to more collision sites.","A temperature increase raises the fraction of particles above the activation energy. A catalyst offers a different pathway with a lower activation barrier. When investigating any one factor, hold the others constant so a rate change can be attributed to the variable being tested."],"steps":["Identify which condition has changed.","For concentration, pressure or surface area, explain the change in encounter frequency or available sites.","For temperature or catalyst, explicitly discuss the fraction of collisions that overcome the energy barrier."],"example":{"q":"Why does powdered CaCO₃ react faster with the same acid than the same mass of large chips?","steps":["Both samples have the same total chemical amount.","Powder has a greater exposed surface area.","More acid particles can collide with carbonate surfaces each second."],"answer":"Greater exposed area increases the rate of effective encounters."},"mistake":"More surface area changes reaction rate, not the final stoichiometric amount from the same limiting quantity.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Factors controlling rate","labels":["Higher concentration","Higher pressure","Greater surface area","Higher temperature","Catalyst"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":59,"guidePage":53},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.4","title":"Activation energy and Maxwell–Boltzmann curves","hl":false,"summary":"Only particles in the high-energy tail can cross the activation barrier.","explain":["The activation energy Eₐ is the minimum collision energy associated with a successful reaction pathway. A Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution shows the spread of particle energies at a given temperature, not a single energy shared by every particle.","At a higher temperature, the distribution becomes broader and its peak is lower and shifted toward higher energy. The total area stays the same for an unchanged number of particles. The area to the right of Eₐ increases, explaining the higher successful-collision probability."],"steps":["Label the horizontal axis kinetic energy and vertical axis number or fraction of particles.","Draw the higher-temperature curve with a lower, broader peak while preserving area.","Draw Eₐ on the energy axis and compare the areas beyond it."],"example":{"q":"Why can a small temperature increase greatly increase reaction rate?","steps":["The energy distribution broadens toward higher energies.","The fraction of particles above the unchanged activation barrier rises substantially.","More collisions therefore have sufficient energy to react."],"answer":"A larger high-energy fraction increases the frequency of successful collisions."},"mistake":"Heating does not lower Eₐ for the same pathway; it changes the energy distribution.","visual":{"kind":"distribution","title":"Activation energy and Maxwell–Boltzmann curves","labels":["Low T curve","High T curve","Activation energy Eₐ","Reacting high-energy tail"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":59,"guidePage":53},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.5","title":"Catalysts and energy profiles","hl":false,"summary":"Catalysts lower the activation barrier without changing reactant and product energy levels.","explain":["A catalyst changes the pathway by which a reaction proceeds and provides a lower activation energy. It is regenerated in the overall process. Biological catalysts are called enzymes. The guide does not require detailed homogeneous and heterogeneous catalyst mechanisms here.","On an energy profile, the reactant and product levels stay fixed while the barrier is lower. The catalyst does not change ΔH, the equilibrium constant or the equilibrium composition. It accelerates both forward and reverse processes so equilibrium is reached sooner."],"steps":["Draw identical reactant and product levels for catalysed and uncatalysed routes.","Make the catalysed barrier lower.","Explain that more particles can cross the lower threshold at the same temperature."],"example":{"q":"How does a catalyst change an exothermic energy profile?","steps":["Products remain lower in energy than reactants.","The alternative route has a lower peak relative to reactants.","The vertical difference between reactant and product energies, ΔH, is unchanged."],"answer":"Lower Eₐ; unchanged ΔH and equilibrium position."},"mistake":"A catalyst does not make a thermodynamically unfavourable reaction favourable.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"Catalysts and energy profiles","labels":["Reactants","Uncatalysed barrier","Lower catalysed barrier","Products","Same ΔH"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":59,"guidePage":53},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.6","title":"Multistep mechanisms and the rate-determining step","hl":true,"summary":"A possible mechanism must match both stoichiometric and kinetic evidence.","explain":["A reaction mechanism divides an overall transformation into elementary steps. An intermediate is formed in one step and consumed in a later one, so it cancels when steps are added. A catalyst is consumed and later regenerated instead.","The slow bottleneck is the rate-determining step. It need not be the first step. A proposed mechanism must sum to the observed overall equation and be consistent with the experimentally measured rate law; fitting one alone is not sufficient evidence."],"steps":["Add elementary steps and cancel species appearing on both sides.","Identify intermediates, regenerated catalysts and the stated slow step.","Compare the predicted kinetic dependence with observed experimental data."],"example":{"q":"In a mechanism A + B → I (fast), then I + C → P (slow), identify the intermediate and overall equation.","steps":["I is produced in step one and consumed in step two.","Add both steps and cancel I.","The second step is the stated slow bottleneck."],"answer":"I is the intermediate; overall A + B + C → P."},"mistake":"Do not assume the first step must determine rate or infer the full rate law from the overall equation.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Multistep mechanisms and the rate-determining step","labels":["Fast step","Intermediate I","Slow step","Product P"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":59,"guidePage":53},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.7","title":"Energy profiles for multistep reactions","hl":true,"summary":"Each elementary step contributes a barrier; intermediates occupy minima between peaks.","explain":["A multistep energy diagram contains more than one peak. The maxima represent transition states, fleeting high-energy arrangements along each step. The minima between them represent intermediates that can have a finite lifetime.","The activation barrier of a step is measured upward from that step’s starting minimum, not always from the original reactant line. When kinetic data identify a slow step, its barrier must be represented consistently; the highest point on the page is not by itself enough without considering its starting level."],"steps":["Count steps from the number of peaks.","Locate intermediate minima between peaks.","Measure each barrier from the preceding minimum and identify the rate-determining barrier using the kinetic information."],"example":{"q":"A two-step profile has an intermediate at −20 kJ mol⁻¹ and its following transition state at +60 kJ mol⁻¹. Find the second-step Eₐ.","steps":["The second step starts at the intermediate minimum.","Eₐ,₂ = energy of transition state − energy of intermediate.","60 − (−20) = 80 kJ mol⁻¹."],"answer":"80 kJ mol⁻¹."},"mistake":"An intermediate is a minimum, whereas a transition state is a maximum.","visual":{"kind":"energy","title":"Energy profiles for multistep reactions","labels":["Reactants","Transition state 1","Intermediate minimum","Transition state 2","Products"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":60,"guidePage":54},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.8","title":"Molecularity of elementary steps","hl":true,"summary":"Molecularity counts reacting particles in a single elementary event.","explain":["An elementary step is unimolecular if one reacting particle changes, bimolecular if two collide, and termolecular if three participate simultaneously. Molecularity describes a proposed step, not an entire multistep reaction.","The experimental order of an overall reaction is a different concept. It may resemble molecularity for an appropriate elementary step, but it must not be obtained by simply counting coefficients in the overall equation."],"steps":["Confirm that the equation describes one elementary step.","Count the reacting particles, including coefficients.","Assign unimolecular, bimolecular or termolecular."],"example":{"q":"Classify elementary steps I → P and A + 2B → Q.","steps":["I → P involves one reacting particle.","A + 2B → Q involves three reacting particles.","These are unimolecular and termolecular respectively."],"answer":"Unimolecular; termolecular."},"mistake":"Molecularity is not the sum of coefficients of an arbitrary overall reaction.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Molecularity of elementary steps","labels":["1 particle: unimolecular","2: bimolecular","3: termolecular"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":60,"guidePage":54},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.9","title":"Determining rate equations experimentally","hl":true,"summary":"Use controlled concentration changes to infer the dependence of rate on reactants.","explain":["A rate equation such as rate = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ is an experimentally established relationship. The exponents express how strongly the rate responds to each concentration. They cannot generally be read from a balanced overall equation.","To determine an exponent, compare experiments where only that concentration changes. For example, doubling [A] while keeping [B] constant and observing a fourfold increase in rate implies m = 2. Repeat for every variable."],"steps":["Find two runs differing in only one reactant concentration.","Calculate concentration and rate factors and identify the exponent.","Repeat for other reactants, then write the complete rate law."],"example":{"q":"Doubling [A] at constant [B] doubles rate; doubling [B] at constant [A] quadruples rate. Deduce the rate equation.","steps":["Rate factor 2 when [A] factor 2 gives first order in A.","Rate factor 4 when [B] factor 2 gives second order in B.","Combine the independent dependences."],"answer":"rate = k[A][B]²."},"mistake":"An unchanged rate after changing one reactant indicates zero order in that reactant under those conditions.","visual":{"kind":"table","title":"Determining rate equations experimentally","labels":["Change [A] only","Compare rate factors","Find m","Repeat for B","rate = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":60,"guidePage":54},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.10","title":"Reaction orders and graphical evidence","hl":true,"summary":"Orders are rate-law exponents; overall order is their sum.","explain":["For zero order in A, rate does not depend on [A]; for first order, rate is proportional to [A]; for second order, rate is proportional to [A]². The overall order is the sum of all exponents in the rate equation. Only integer orders are assessed in this guide.","Rate-versus-concentration plots are respectively horizontal, linear through the origin, and upward-curving for orders zero, one and two. Concentration-time curves also differ: a zero-order reactant falls linearly, while first-order decay shows a constant half-life and a curved decline. A simple second-order reactant decay also curves, with progressively longer half-lives as concentration falls."],"steps":["Read the experimental rate equation rather than the overall stoichiometry.","Identify each exponent and add them for overall order.","Match the rate–concentration graph to constant, proportional or square dependence."],"example":{"q":"For rate = k[A]²[B], state each order and the effect of tripling [A] at fixed [B].","steps":["Order in A is 2; order in B is 1.","Overall order is 2 + 1 = 3.","Tripling [A] multiplies rate by 3² = 9."],"answer":"Second order in A, first in B, third overall; rate becomes nine times larger."},"mistake":"A second-order dependence means rate changes by the square of the concentration factor, not by twice that factor.","visual":{"kind":"graph","title":"Reaction orders and graphical evidence","labels":["Zero: horizontal","First: straight line","Second: curved","Overall = sum of orders"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":60,"guidePage":54},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.11","title":"Rate constants and their units","hl":true,"summary":"The units of k ensure the rate equation has the correct overall units.","explain":["The rate constant k connects concentrations with the observed rate at a particular temperature. Rearranging the measured rate equation allows k to be calculated. Changing temperature generally changes k, while changing concentration at fixed temperature does not change the same reaction’s k.","If rate is mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹, first-order k has units s⁻¹, second-order k has dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ and zero-order k has mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. Derive units by dividing rate units by the concentration terms, rather than memorizing without checking."],"steps":["Rearrange k = rate / concentration factors.","Substitute concentration and rate values with units.","Cancel dimensions to find the units consistent with overall reaction order."],"example":{"q":"For rate = k[A][B], rate is 0.012 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ when [A] = 0.20 and [B] = 0.30 mol dm⁻³. Calculate k.","steps":["k = 0.012/(0.20 × 0.30).","k = 0.20 numerically.","Units = (mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹)/(mol² dm⁻⁶) = dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹."],"answer":"k = 0.20 dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹."},"mistake":"The units of k are not always s⁻¹; they depend on overall order.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Rate constants and their units","labels":["rate = k[A][B]","k = rate / [A][B]","Second order","dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":60,"guidePage":54},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.12","title":"The Arrhenius relationship","hl":true,"summary":"The temperature dependence of k allows activation energy to be calculated.","explain":["The Arrhenius equation is k = A e^(−Eₐ/RT). At higher T the negative exponent is less negative, so k generally increases. Use kelvin temperature and Eₐ in units compatible with R, normally J mol⁻¹.","Taking natural logarithms gives ln k = ln A − (Eₐ/R)(1/T). A plot of ln k against 1/T is linear with gradient −Eₐ/R and vertical intercept ln A. This separates activation energy from the pre-exponential factor."],"steps":["Use the linear form and label axes ln k and 1/T.","Find the gradient of the best-fit line.","Calculate Eₐ = −gradient × R and convert J mol⁻¹ to kJ mol⁻¹ if required."],"example":{"q":"An Arrhenius graph of ln k against 1/T has gradient −6000 K. Find Eₐ using R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.","steps":["Gradient = −Eₐ/R.","Eₐ = −(−6000) × 8.31 = 49,860 J mol⁻¹.","Convert by dividing by 1000."],"answer":"Eₐ = 49.9 kJ mol⁻¹."},"mistake":"The horizontal axis is 1/T, not T, and ln means natural logarithm.","visual":{"kind":"graph","title":"The Arrhenius relationship","labels":["ln k","1/T","Slope = −Eₐ/R","Intercept = ln A"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":60,"guidePage":54},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.2.13","title":"The Arrhenius factor","hl":true,"summary":"The pre-exponential factor reflects collision frequency and suitable orientation.","explain":["The Arrhenius factor A includes how frequently potentially reactive encounters occur and how likely their orientations are to be productive. It is not itself the fraction of collisions with sufficient energy; that temperature-sensitive fraction is represented by the exponential term.","From an Arrhenius straight line, A is found by exponentiating the intercept: A = e^(intercept). Its units are the same as the units of k because the exponential term is dimensionless."],"steps":["Find the y-intercept of an ln k versus 1/T plot.","Set ln A equal to that intercept.","Use the exponential function and give A the units of k."],"example":{"q":"A first-order reaction has an Arrhenius intercept of 20.0. Determine A.","steps":["The intercept equals ln A.","A = e²⁰ ≈ 4.85 × 10⁸.","A has the same units as first-order k, namely s⁻¹."],"answer":"A ≈ 4.85 × 10⁸ s⁻¹."},"mistake":"Do not use 10^intercept when the plotted logarithm is ln.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"The Arrhenius factor","labels":["Intercept = ln A","A = e^(intercept)","Frequency","Suitable orientation"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":60,"guidePage":54},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"}]},{"id":"R2.3","name":"How far? The extent of chemical change","group":"Reactivity 2","sections":[{"id":"R2.3.1","title":"Dynamic equilibrium","hl":false,"summary":"A closed system reaches equilibrium when forward and reverse rates are equal.","explain":["A reversible reaction proceeds in both directions. Starting with reactants, the forward process usually dominates initially; as products accumulate, the reverse process becomes more important. At dynamic equilibrium the rates become equal.","Equilibrium is dynamic because molecular events continue, even though macroscopic concentrations remain constant. A closed system prevents continuous loss or gain of material, allowing this balance to be maintained. The concentrations of products and reactants are not necessarily equal."],"steps":["Check that both forward and reverse reactions are possible.","State that the system is closed and rates are equal.","Distinguish constant concentrations from equal concentrations."],"example":{"q":"A sealed mixture has constant reactant and product concentrations. Does this mean reaction has stopped?","steps":["A constant composition can indicate dynamic equilibrium.","Forward and reverse reactions continue at the same rate.","Equal rates cause no net concentration change."],"answer":"No: molecular reactions continue with equal opposing rates."},"mistake":"Equilibrium means equal rates, not equal amounts.","visual":{"kind":"equilibrium","title":"Dynamic equilibrium","labels":["Closed system","Forward reaction →","← Reverse reaction","Equal rates","Constant concentrations"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":61,"guidePage":55},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.3.2","title":"Writing equilibrium-constant expressions","hl":false,"summary":"The equilibrium law relates equilibrium concentrations to the balanced equation.","explain":["For a homogeneous reaction aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD, the concentration expression is K = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ). The powers come from stoichiometric coefficients. Square brackets denote equilibrium molar concentrations.","The expression corresponds to the equation exactly as written. Reversing or scaling the equation changes the expression and the numerical value of its constant. In this topic the guide assesses homogeneous equilibrium expressions, with all participating species in the same phase."],"steps":["Balance the homogeneous equilibrium equation.","Put product concentrations in the numerator and reactants in the denominator.","Raise each concentration to its coefficient."],"example":{"q":"Write K for N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g).","steps":["Products go above the division line.","The coefficient 2 becomes the power on [NH₃].","Reactant coefficients become powers 1 and 3."],"answer":"K = [NH₃]² / ([N₂][H₂]³)."},"mistake":"Do not multiply concentration by its coefficient; use the coefficient as an exponent.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Writing equilibrium-constant expressions","labels":["Products numerator","Reactants denominator","Coefficients become powers","Equilibrium concentrations"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":61,"guidePage":55},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.3.3","title":"What the size of K tells you","hl":false,"summary":"K indicates the equilibrium tendency toward products or reactants at a stated temperature.","explain":["If K is much greater than one, the product concentration terms dominate the expression; if K is much less than one, reactant terms dominate. K near one means neither side overwhelmingly dominates in this concentration-product sense.","The value is temperature dependent. For the reverse reaction at the same temperature, K_reverse = 1/K_forward. A large K describes extent, not speed: a thermodynamically product-favoured reaction can still be kinetically slow."],"steps":["Read K relative to one.","Describe the favoured side without claiming the reaction is fast.","For the reverse equation, take the reciprocal at the same temperature."],"example":{"q":"A reaction has K = 2.0 × 10⁴ at a specified temperature. Find K for the reverse reaction.","steps":["The forward direction strongly favours product terms at equilibrium.","Reverse constant = 1/(2.0 × 10⁴).","Evaluate the reciprocal."],"answer":"K_reverse = 5.0 × 10⁻⁵."},"mistake":"A large equilibrium constant does not imply a high reaction rate.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"What the size of K tells you","labels":["K ≪ 1: reactant-favoured","K ≈ 1: neither dominates","K ≫ 1: product-favoured","Reverse K = 1/K"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":61,"guidePage":55},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.3.4","title":"Le Châtelier’s principle","hl":false,"summary":"An equilibrium system responds to a disturbance by opposing its effect.","explain":["Adding a reactant usually shifts a reversible reaction toward products; removing a product has a similar effect. Compressing a gaseous equilibrium favours the side with fewer gas moles, if the counts differ. These changes alter equilibrium composition but do not change K at fixed temperature.","Temperature is different: heating favours the endothermic direction and changes K. A catalyst affects how quickly equilibrium is reached but not the equilibrium composition, because both directions are accelerated. Apply the principle to physical equilibria too, such as a gas dissolving in water."],"steps":["Identify the disturbed variable: concentration, pressure or temperature.","For pressure, count gaseous coefficients; for temperature, identify the endothermic direction.","State the composition change separately from whether K changes."],"example":{"q":"For exothermic N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, predict the effects of compression and heating.","steps":["Compression favours the right: 2 gas moles rather than 4.","Heating favours the endothermic reverse reaction, decreasing the equilibrium ammonia amount.","Compression leaves K unchanged at fixed T; heating changes K and lowers it for this forward exothermic equation."],"answer":"Compression favours NH₃; heating favours reactants and decreases forward K."},"mistake":"Only a temperature change changes the equilibrium constant for a fixed equation.","visual":{"kind":"equilibrium","title":"Le Châtelier’s principle","labels":["Add reactant → products","Compress → fewer gas moles","Heat → endothermic direction","Catalyst: no shift"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":61,"guidePage":55},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.3.5","title":"Reaction quotient and direction","hl":true,"summary":"Compare the current reaction quotient Q with K to predict net change.","explain":["The reaction quotient Q uses the same concentration expression as K, but the concentrations may be measured at any instant, not only equilibrium. Comparing Q with the equilibrium value shows which side must increase.","If Q < K, the ratio of product terms is too small, so net forward reaction increases Q. If Q > K, net reverse reaction lowers Q. If Q = K, the system is at equilibrium at that temperature."],"steps":["Write the correct equilibrium expression.","Insert current concentrations to calculate Q.","Compare with K and predict forward, reverse or no net reaction."],"example":{"q":"For A ⇌ B, K = 4.0. At an instant [A] = 0.50 and [B] = 0.25 mol dm⁻³. Predict direction.","steps":["Q = [B]/[A] = 0.25/0.50 = 0.50.","Q = 0.50 is below K = 4.0.","Net A → B increases the product-to-reactant ratio."],"answer":"The net reaction proceeds forward toward B."},"mistake":"Do not use initial concentrations as if they are necessarily equilibrium values.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Reaction quotient and direction","labels":["Q < K: forward","Q = K: equilibrium","Q > K: reverse"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":61,"guidePage":55},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.3.6","title":"Calculating equilibrium composition","hl":true,"summary":"Use a concentration-change table with stoichiometric changes to solve equilibrium problems.","explain":["An initial-change-equilibrium table tracks how the concentrations change as a reversible reaction approaches equilibrium. Each change is proportional to the balanced coefficient. Substitute the equilibrium row into the K expression.","The guide does not expect quadratic equations here. For a very small K, a small dissociation may justify approximating the remaining reactant concentration by its initial value. Always check that the calculated change really is small relative to that initial concentration."],"steps":["Write initial concentrations and represent the reaction change by x.","Use coefficients to write every equilibrium concentration.","Substitute into K, use an allowed small-change approximation if appropriate, and check the result."],"example":{"q":"For A ⇌ B + C, initially [A] = 0.100 mol dm⁻³ and no products; K = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶. Estimate equilibrium [B].","steps":["At equilibrium [B] = [C] = x and [A] = 0.100 − x.","Assume x is small: K ≈ x²/0.100.","x = √(1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.100) = 3.16 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³, only about 0.32% of initial A."],"answer":"[B] ≈ 3.16 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³."},"mistake":"Use stoichiometry for every change; not every reaction produces equal concentrations of all products.","visual":{"kind":"table","title":"Calculating equilibrium composition","labels":["Initial","Change by coefficient × x","Equilibrium","Substitute into K","Check approximation"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":61,"guidePage":55},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"},{"id":"R2.3.7","title":"Gibbs energy and equilibrium constants","hl":true,"summary":"The relation ΔG° = −RT ln K connects thermodynamics with equilibrium.","explain":["The standard Gibbs energy change and equilibrium constant describe the same thermodynamic preference in different forms. A negative ΔG° corresponds to K > 1, while positive ΔG° corresponds to K < 1. If ΔG° = 0, K = 1.","In ΔG° = −RT ln K, use kelvin, natural logarithms and consistent joule units. Distinguish standard ΔG° from actual ΔG: at equilibrium actual ΔG is zero, but the standard value need not be zero unless K is one."],"steps":["Convert T to kelvin and ΔG° to J mol⁻¹ if needed.","Use ΔG° = −RT ln K or rearrange to ln K = −ΔG°/(RT).","Check that the sign of ΔG° agrees with whether K is above or below one."],"example":{"q":"Calculate ΔG° at 298 K when K = 100. Use R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.","steps":["ln 100 = 4.605.","ΔG° = −8.31 × 298 × 4.605 = −11,400 J mol⁻¹ approximately.","Divide by 1000."],"answer":"ΔG° ≈ −11.4 kJ mol⁻¹."},"mistake":"At equilibrium, ΔG = 0 does not mean ΔG° = 0 for every reaction.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Gibbs energy and equilibrium constants","labels":["ΔG° = −RT ln K","K > 1 → ΔG° < 0","K < 1 → ΔG° > 0","K = 1 → ΔG° = 0"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":61,"guidePage":55},"group":"Reactivity 2 · How much, how fast and how far?"}]},{"id":"R3.1","name":"Proton transfer reactions","group":"Reactivity 3","sections":[{"id":"R3.1.1","title":"Brønsted–Lowry acids and bases","hl":false,"summary":"An acid donates a proton; a base accepts a proton.","explain":["The Brønsted–Lowry model defines an acid or base by what it does in a particular reaction. An acid loses H⁺, while a base gains it. In water, H⁺ can also be represented as H₃O⁺ because a proton is associated with water molecules.","A base is a proton acceptor; an alkali is a base that dissolves in water and produces an alkaline solution. Therefore all alkalis are bases, but an insoluble basic solid need not be an alkali."],"steps":["Compare each reactant with the product containing the same atoms.","The species that loses one H⁺ acts as acid.","The species that gains H⁺ acts as base."],"example":{"q":"Identify acid and base in NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻.","steps":["NH₃ gains H⁺ to form NH₄⁺.","H₂O loses H⁺ to form OH⁻.","Therefore ammonia accepts and water donates the proton."],"answer":"NH₃ is the base; H₂O is the acid."},"mistake":"Do not identify acids only from whether their formula begins with H.","visual":{"kind":"proton","title":"Brønsted–Lowry acids and bases","labels":["Acid donates H⁺","Base accepts H⁺","NH₃ → NH₄⁺","H₂O → OH⁻"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":62,"guidePage":56},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.2","title":"Conjugate acid–base pairs","hl":false,"summary":"A conjugate pair differs by exactly one proton.","explain":["When an acid donates a proton, the remaining species is its conjugate base. When a base accepts a proton, it becomes its conjugate acid. Their formulas differ by one H atom and a charge of one unit.","In a complete proton-transfer reaction there are two conjugate acid–base pairs. Match species by the same underlying atoms, rather than pairing two reactants merely because they react together."],"steps":["Remove H⁺ to find an acid’s conjugate base.","Add H⁺ to find a base’s conjugate acid.","Check that both atom count and charge change correctly."],"example":{"q":"Find the conjugate acid and conjugate base of HCO₃⁻.","steps":["Adding H⁺ produces H₂CO₃ with zero charge.","Removing H⁺ produces CO₃²⁻.","Both differ from HCO₃⁻ by one proton."],"answer":"Conjugate acid H₂CO₃; conjugate base CO₃²⁻."},"mistake":"Removing H⁺ makes charge one unit more negative.","visual":{"kind":"proton","title":"Conjugate acid–base pairs","labels":["HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻","Acid / conjugate base","B + H⁺ ⇌ BH⁺","Base / conjugate acid"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":62,"guidePage":56},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.3","title":"Species that can donate or accept protons","hl":false,"summary":"An amphiprotic species can act as an acid or a base depending on its reaction partner.","explain":["Water and hydrogencarbonate can both donate and accept a proton. Their role cannot be fixed from the formula alone: it depends on the other reacting species.","To demonstrate amphiprotic behaviour, write one reaction where the species loses H⁺ and another where it gains H⁺. Charge and atom balance must remain correct in both equations."],"steps":["Find an available proton that could be donated.","Find a site capable of accepting a proton.","Write and balance one equation for each role."],"example":{"q":"Show the two proton-transfer roles of water.","steps":["With HCl, water accepts H⁺: HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻.","With NH₃, water donates H⁺: NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻.","Water therefore acts as base or acid depending on the partner."],"answer":"Water is amphiprotic."},"mistake":"Amphiprotic means proton donation and acceptance, not simply having both positive and negative ions.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Species that can donate or accept protons","labels":["H₂O accepts H⁺ → H₃O⁺","H₂O donates H⁺ → OH⁻","Role depends on partner"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":62,"guidePage":56},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.4","title":"The pH scale","hl":false,"summary":"pH = −log₁₀[H⁺] converts hydrogen-ion concentration into a logarithmic scale.","explain":["Every change of one pH unit represents a factor of ten in hydrogen-ion concentration. Lower pH means higher [H⁺]. Rearranging gives [H⁺] = 10^(−pH). Use the numerical concentration in mol dm⁻³ in these course calculations.","Universal indicator gives an approximate pH from its colour, while a calibrated pH probe provides a more precise numerical measurement. The scale is logarithmic, so equal concentration changes do not generally produce equal pH changes."],"steps":["Identify whether [H⁺] or pH is the given quantity.","Use −log₁₀[H⁺] for pH or 10^(−pH) for concentration.","Check that increasing [H⁺] decreases the calculated pH."],"example":{"q":"Calculate pH when [H⁺] = 2.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³.","steps":["pH = −log₁₀(2.0 × 10⁻³).","log₁₀(2.0 × 10⁻³) ≈ −2.699.","Change the sign."],"answer":"pH ≈ 2.70."},"mistake":"A solution at pH 2 has ten times, not twice, the [H⁺] of one at pH 3.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"The pH scale","labels":["[H⁺] increases ×10","pH decreases by 1","pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]","[H⁺] = 10^(−pH)"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":62,"guidePage":56},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.5","title":"The ionic product of water","hl":false,"summary":"Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] links hydrogen and hydroxide concentrations at a given temperature.","explain":["Water undergoes a small amount of self-ionization. At a fixed temperature the product [H⁺][OH⁻] is constant, so increasing one ion’s concentration decreases the other’s. At 298 K, K_w = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ for these concentration calculations.","A neutral solution has equal [H⁺] and [OH⁻]. An acidic solution has more H⁺ than OH⁻, while a basic solution has more OH⁻. Neutral does not always mean pH 7, because K_w changes with temperature; the pH 7 result applies at 298 K."],"steps":["Use the correct K_w for the specified temperature.","Rearrange to find the missing ion concentration.","Compare [H⁺] with [OH⁻] to classify the solution."],"example":{"q":"At 298 K, find [OH⁻] when [H⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³.","steps":["[OH⁻] = K_w/[H⁺].","[OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.0 × 10⁻⁵.","[OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³; H⁺ is greater than OH⁻."],"answer":"1.0 × 10⁻⁹ mol dm⁻³; the solution is acidic."},"mistake":"Do not assume the neutral pH remains exactly 7 at every temperature.","visual":{"kind":"equilibrium","title":"The ionic product of water","labels":["H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH⁻","Kw fixed at given T","Neutral: equal ions","298 K: Kw = 10⁻¹⁴"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":62,"guidePage":56},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.6","title":"Strong versus weak acids and bases","hl":false,"summary":"Strength describes extent of ionization; concentration describes how much is dissolved.","explain":["A strong acid or base ionizes essentially completely in the relevant aqueous reaction; a weak one establishes an equilibrium with substantial unionized material. Strong and weak are therefore different from concentrated and dilute.","At equal analytical concentration, a strong monoprotic acid generally gives higher [H⁺], lower pH and higher electrical conductivity than a weak acid. The equilibrium direction favours the weaker conjugate acid–base system. The guide lists HCl, HBr, HI, HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ as strong acids, and group 1 hydroxides as strong bases in solution."],"steps":["Identify whether the question concerns extent of ionization or amount per volume.","Use complete ionization for a strong monoprotic acid under appropriate dilute conditions.","For a weak acid, show a reversible equilibrium rather than assuming [H⁺] equals initial concentration."],"example":{"q":"Explain why 0.10 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid has a higher pH than 0.10 mol dm⁻³ HCl.","steps":["HCl is a strong acid and ionizes essentially fully.","Ethanoic acid is weak and only partly ionizes.","Its [H⁺] is lower at the same analytical concentration."],"answer":"Partial ionization gives the weak acid a higher pH."},"mistake":"A concentrated weak acid is not thereby a strong acid.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Strong versus weak acids and bases","labels":["Strong: extensive ionization","Weak: partial ionization","Concentrated: much per volume","Dilute: little per volume"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":63,"guidePage":57},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.7","title":"Neutralization and salt formation","hl":false,"summary":"Acids react with bases to form salts, with products depending on the type of base.","explain":["For an acid reacting with a hydroxide, the core neutralization is H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Metal oxides also consume acid to form a salt and water. Carbonates and hydrogencarbonates additionally release carbon dioxide.","Ammonia and amines are bases because their nitrogen lone pair accepts a proton, producing ammonium or substituted ammonium salts. To identify a salt’s parent acid and base, trace its anion to the acid and its cation to the base."],"steps":["Identify the acid anion and the base-derived cation.","Write the correct salt formula by charge balance.","Add water and, for carbonate/hydrogencarbonate, CO₂, then balance."],"example":{"q":"Write the reaction between hydrochloric acid and sodium carbonate.","steps":["Salt from Na⁺ and Cl⁻ is NaCl.","Carbonate with acid gives CO₂ and H₂O.","Balance requires two HCl for one Na₂CO₃."],"answer":"Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂."},"mistake":"An acid–carbonate reaction produces CO₂, not H₂.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Neutralization and salt formation","labels":["Acid + hydroxide → salt + water","Acid + carbonate → salt + water + CO₂","Ammonia + H⁺ → NH₄⁺"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":63,"guidePage":57},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.8","title":"Strong acid–strong base titration curves","hl":false,"summary":"The equivalence point occurs when acid and base have reacted in the stoichiometric ratio.","explain":["A titration curve plots pH against added titrant volume. Starting with a strong acid and adding strong base gives low initial pH, gradual rise, a steep jump near equivalence and a high-pH region after excess base accumulates.","For a monoprotic strong acid–strong base titration at 298 K, the equivalence point is approximately pH 7. It is the stoichiometric point, not necessarily the midpoint of the added volume scale. Use n = CV and the balanced ratio to determine unknown concentrations."],"steps":["Identify which solution is initially present and which is added.","Locate the steep inflection region and equivalence volume.","Apply the mole ratio at equivalence; explain the post-equivalence region using excess titrant."],"example":{"q":"25.0 cm³ of HCl is neutralized by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. Find [HCl].","steps":["n(NaOH) = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 0.00200 mol.","HCl:NaOH = 1:1, so n(HCl) = 0.00200 mol.","[HCl] = 0.00200/0.0250 = 0.0800 mol dm⁻³."],"answer":"0.0800 mol dm⁻³."},"mistake":"Equal volumes are not required at equivalence unless the reacting concentrations and ratio make them equal.","visual":{"kind":"titration","title":"Strong acid–strong base titration curves","labels":["Low initial pH","Steep pH rise","Equivalence: stoichiometric","Excess base region"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":63,"guidePage":57},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.9","title":"pOH and acid–base conversions","hl":true,"summary":"pOH = −log₁₀[OH⁻]; at 298 K pH + pOH = 14.00.","explain":["The pOH scale is the hydroxide analogue of pH. A high hydroxide concentration gives a small pOH. Taking logarithms of K_w = [H⁺][OH⁻] gives pH + pOH = pK_w.","At 298 K, pK_w = 14.00, but use the temperature-appropriate value in other conditions. A strong base may produce more than one hydroxide per formula unit, so count stoichiometry before converting to pOH."],"steps":["Find [OH⁻] or pOH from the given information.","Use pOH = −log₁₀[OH⁻] or [OH⁻] = 10^(−pOH).","Interconvert pH and pOH with pK_w at the stated temperature."],"example":{"q":"Find the pH of 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ NaOH at 298 K.","steps":["NaOH dissociates to give [OH⁻] = 0.0200 mol dm⁻³.","pOH = −log₁₀(0.0200) = 1.699.","pH = 14.00 − 1.699 = 12.301."],"answer":"pH ≈ 12.30."},"mistake":"Do not call −log[OH⁻] the pH; it is pOH.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"pOH and acid–base conversions","labels":["pOH = −log[OH⁻]","pH + pOH = pKw","At 298 K: 14.00"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":63,"guidePage":57},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.10","title":"Ka, Kb, pKa and pKb","hl":true,"summary":"Equilibrium constants quantify the strengths of weak acids and bases.","explain":["For HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, K_a = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA]. A larger K_a means more extensive acid ionization, hence a stronger acid. Similarly, a larger K_b means a stronger weak base.","pK_a = −log₁₀K_a and pK_b = −log₁₀K_b reverse the numerical trend: lower pK_a means stronger acid; lower pK_b means stronger base. These quantities describe the equilibrium tendency, not how much acid or base was initially added."],"steps":["Identify whether the data are K or pK values.","For K compare larger as stronger; for pK compare smaller as stronger.","Write the appropriate ionization equilibrium before calculating concentrations."],"example":{"q":"Acids A and B have pKa values 3.0 and 5.0. Which is stronger and by what factor do Ka values differ?","steps":["K_a(A) = 10⁻³ and K_a(B) = 10⁻⁵.","10⁻³/10⁻⁵ = 100.","The lower pK_a corresponds to the larger K_a."],"answer":"A is stronger; its Ka is 100 times larger."},"mistake":"A higher pKa means a weaker acid, not a stronger one.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Ka, Kb, pKa and pKb","labels":["Larger Ka → stronger acid","Smaller pKa → stronger acid","Ka = 10^(−pKa)"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":63,"guidePage":57},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.11","title":"Conjugate constants and weak-acid calculations","hl":true,"summary":"For a conjugate pair, KaKb = Kw; small ionization can simplify pH calculations.","explain":["Multiplying the acid-ionization constant of HA by the base constant of A⁻ cancels the conjugate concentrations, leaving K_w. A stronger acid therefore has a weaker conjugate base. At 298 K, pK_a + pK_b = 14.00 for that conjugate pair.","For a weak monoprotic acid with concentration c and small ionization, [H⁺] ≈ √(K_a c). The guide does not expect quadratic solutions, but you must recognize the approximation and check that calculated ionization is small relative to c. The corresponding weak-base result uses [OH⁻] ≈ √(K_b c)."],"steps":["Use K_aK_b = K_w only for a conjugate acid–base pair.","For a weak-acid pH, write the equilibrium and let ionized concentration be x.","If justified, approximate remaining acid as c and solve x²/c = K_a."],"example":{"q":"Estimate the pH of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ HA with Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵.","steps":["[H⁺] ≈ √(K_a c) = √(1.0 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³.","This is 1% of c, so the small-change approximation is reasonable.","pH = −log₁₀(10⁻³) = 3.00."],"answer":"Estimated pH = 3.00."},"mistake":"K_aK_b = K_w does not connect arbitrary unrelated acids and bases.","visual":{"kind":"equilibrium","title":"Conjugate constants and weak-acid calculations","labels":["HA / A⁻ conjugate pair","Ka × Kb = Kw","Small x: [HA] ≈ c","[H⁺] ≈ √Ka c"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":63,"guidePage":57},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.12","title":"Salt hydrolysis and pH","hl":true,"summary":"Some salt ions react with water, so a dissolved salt need not give neutral pH.","explain":["An ion derived from a weak base can act as an acid. For example NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺ makes solution acidic. An ion derived from a weak acid can act as a base: RCOO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ RCOOH + OH⁻ makes solution basic.","Carbonate accepts a proton from water to form hydrogencarbonate and OH⁻. Hydrogencarbonate can both donate and accept protons, so its net pH effect depends on the competing equilibria. Do not assume every salt is neutral; assess its ions and their parent acid/base strengths."],"steps":["Separate the dissolved salt into its ions.","Identify an ion capable of proton transfer with water.","Write the hydrolysis equilibrium and determine whether H₃O⁺ or OH⁻ is produced."],"example":{"q":"Predict whether aqueous sodium ethanoate is acidic, neutral or basic.","steps":["Na⁺ has negligible acid–base effect in this course context.","CH₃COO⁻ is the conjugate base of weak ethanoic acid.","CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻ generates hydroxide."],"answer":"Basic."},"mistake":"A salt made from acid and base is not automatically pH 7 after dissolution.","visual":{"kind":"proton","title":"Salt hydrolysis and pH","labels":["NH₄⁺ → H₃O⁺ production","RCOO⁻ → OH⁻ production","CO₃²⁻ accepts H⁺","HCO₃⁻ amphiprotic"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":64,"guidePage":58},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.13","title":"Weak/strong titration-curve combinations","hl":true,"summary":"Acid and base strengths determine initial pH, buffer regions and equivalence-point pH.","explain":["In a weak-acid–strong-base titration, a buffer region appears before equivalence and pH = pK_a at half-equivalence. The equivalence solution contains the weak acid’s conjugate base, so pH is above 7 at 298 K. A weak-base–strong-acid titration instead has an acidic equivalence solution and pOH = pK_b at half-equivalence.","Strong-acid–strong-base titrations have the steepest clear equivalence region. Weak-acid–weak-base titrations lack such a sharp jump; equivalence pH depends on their relative constants. Add small titrant increments near a rapid pH change when collecting experimental data."],"steps":["Identify both acid and base as strong or weak.","Use the salt at equivalence to predict acidic, neutral or basic pH.","Locate buffer and half-equivalence regions where applicable."],"example":{"q":"In a weak-acid–strong-base titration, half-equivalence pH is 4.80. What is pKa and is equivalence above or below pH 7 at 298 K?","steps":["At half-equivalence, [HA] and [A⁻] are equal.","Therefore pH = pK_a = 4.80.","At equivalence, conjugate base hydrolysis makes the solution basic."],"answer":"pKa = 4.80; equivalence pH is above 7."},"mistake":"Half-equivalence and equivalence are different points.","visual":{"kind":"titration","title":"Weak/strong titration-curve combinations","labels":["Initial weak-acid pH","Buffer region","Half-equivalence: pH = pKa","Equivalence: basic"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":64,"guidePage":58},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.14","title":"Acid–base indicators","hl":true,"summary":"Indicator colours depend on the ratio of two differently coloured conjugate forms.","explain":["An indicator can be represented as HInd ⇌ H⁺ + Ind⁻. The undissociated and deprotonated forms have different colours. Adding acid shifts the equilibrium toward HInd; adding base removes H⁺ and favours Ind⁻.","When the two forms have similar concentrations, a visible colour transition occurs near pH = pK_a of the indicator. Universal indicator is a mixture of several indicators, allowing approximate pH estimation across a wider range."],"steps":["Write HInd ⇌ H⁺ + Ind⁻.","Apply the equilibrium response to adding H⁺ or removing H⁺.","Associate the observed colour with the dominant indicator form."],"example":{"q":"An indicator is red as HInd and yellow as Ind⁻. Predict its colour after adding excess acid.","steps":["Excess acid increases [H⁺].","The equilibrium shifts left toward HInd.","The red form becomes dominant."],"answer":"Red."},"mistake":"The indicator changes colour over a range, not at one perfectly exact pH.","visual":{"kind":"equilibrium","title":"Acid–base indicators","labels":["HInd: colour 1","H⁺","Ind⁻: colour 2","Transition near pKa"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":64,"guidePage":58},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.15","title":"Choosing a titration indicator","hl":true,"summary":"The indicator transition range should fall inside the steep pH change near equivalence.","explain":["The equivalence point is fixed by stoichiometry: the correct reacting mole ratio has been added. The end point is the observed signal, such as an indicator colour change. A useful indicator makes these occur as close together as practical.","Choose an indicator from its stated transition range and the titration curve, rather than choosing one by habit. A weak-acid–strong-base titration requires a transition within its steep basic region; a weak-base–strong-acid titration needs one in the steep acidic region."],"steps":["Determine whether equivalence is acidic, neutral or basic.","Identify the steep pH interval around equivalence.","Select an indicator whose transition range lies within that interval."],"example":{"q":"A weak-acid–strong-base curve has a sharp jump from pH 7.5 to 10.0. Compare indicators changing at 3.1–4.4 and 8.2–10.0.","steps":["The acidic-range indicator changes long before equivalence.","The 8.2–10.0 indicator changes inside the steep jump.","Its end-point volume should therefore be close to the equivalence volume."],"answer":"Choose the indicator with range 8.2–10.0."},"mistake":"An end point is an observation; it is not the definition of equivalence.","visual":{"kind":"titration","title":"Choosing a titration indicator","labels":["Stoichiometric equivalence","Observed end point","Steep pH jump","Matching indicator range"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":64,"guidePage":58},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.16","title":"How buffers work","hl":true,"summary":"A buffer contains appreciable amounts of a weak acid/base and its conjugate partner.","explain":["An acidic buffer typically contains HA and A⁻. Added H⁺ is consumed by A⁻ to form HA; added OH⁻ is consumed by HA to form A⁻ and water. Both components are needed so the solution can respond to either addition.","A basic buffer can contain a weak base such as NH₃ and its conjugate acid NH₄⁺. Buffers resist small pH changes; they do not prevent every change and can be overwhelmed once a component is largely consumed. A strong acid alone lacks a substantial conjugate-base reservoir and is not a buffer."],"steps":["Identify the weak acid/base and its conjugate partner.","Write how one component consumes added H⁺.","Write how the other consumes added OH⁻ and note finite capacity."],"example":{"q":"Explain how an ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer responds to a little NaOH.","steps":["OH⁻ reacts with the weak acid component.","CH₃COOH + OH⁻ → CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O.","The ratio of acid to conjugate base changes only a little if both were abundant."],"answer":"Most added OH⁻ is consumed, limiting the pH rise."},"mistake":"A buffer does not keep pH perfectly constant and has finite capacity.","visual":{"kind":"proton","title":"How buffers work","labels":["HA consumes OH⁻","A⁻ consumes H⁺","Both components needed","Limited pH change"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":64,"guidePage":58},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.1.17","title":"Calculating buffer pH and dilution effects","hl":true,"summary":"Buffer pH depends on its conjugate ratio as well as its equilibrium constant.","explain":["Rearranging K_a = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA] gives [H⁺] = K_a[HA]/[A⁻]. Thus equal acid and conjugate-base concentrations give pH = pK_a. Increasing the base-to-acid ratio raises pH; adding acid lowers it.","Before an equilibrium calculation after adding strong acid or base, account for the stoichiometric neutralization of the buffer components. Simple dilution approximately preserves their ratio and hence pH, but lowers buffer capacity because less of each component remains per unit volume."],"steps":["Determine moles of HA and A⁻ after any added strong acid/base has reacted.","Use their concentration ratio in [H⁺] = K_a[HA]/[A⁻].","Calculate pH; for dilution distinguish almost unchanged ratio from reduced capacity."],"example":{"q":"A buffer has [HA] = 0.20 and [A⁻] = 0.10 mol dm⁻³ with Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵. Estimate pH.","steps":["[H⁺] = K_a[HA]/[A⁻].","[H⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.20/0.10 = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵.","pH = −log₁₀(2.0 × 10⁻⁵) ≈ 4.70."],"answer":"pH ≈ 4.70."},"mistake":"Use the post-neutralization buffer composition, not the original amounts, after adding strong acid or base.","visual":{"kind":"ratio","title":"Calculating buffer pH and dilution effects","labels":["Ka","[HA] / [A⁻]","Calculate [H⁺]","Dilution: similar pH, less capacity"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":64,"guidePage":58},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"}]},{"id":"R3.2","name":"Electron transfer reactions","group":"Reactivity 3","sections":[{"id":"R3.2.1","title":"Oxidation states and redox agents","hl":false,"summary":"Oxidation increases oxidation state; reduction decreases it.","explain":["Oxidation can be tracked as electron loss, an increase in oxidation number, oxygen gain or hydrogen loss. Reduction is the reverse: electron gain, a decrease in oxidation number, oxygen loss or hydrogen gain. The electron definition unifies reactions where no oxygen is involved. For assignments, an uncombined element has oxidation state 0 and a monatomic ion has its ion charge; hydrogen is usually +1 and oxygen usually −2, with exceptions handled from the formula and supplied context.","An oxidizing agent causes another species to be oxidized and is itself reduced. A reducing agent supplies electrons and is itself oxidized. Oxidation numbers are bookkeeping assignments; their sum equals the overall charge of a formula unit or ion."],"steps":["Assign known oxidation numbers and use the total charge to find unknown values.","Identify which element increases or decreases its oxidation number.","Name the oxidizing agent as the reactant reduced and the reducing agent as the reactant oxidized."],"example":{"q":"In Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, identify the oxidation, reduction and agents.","steps":["Zn changes from 0 to +2, so it loses two electrons and is oxidized.","Cu changes from +2 to 0, so Cu²⁺ gains electrons and is reduced.","The oxidized reactant is the reducing agent; the reduced reactant is the oxidizing agent."],"answer":"Zn is reducing agent; Cu²⁺ is oxidizing agent."},"mistake":"The oxidizing agent is reduced; the words describe its effect on the other reactant.","visual":{"kind":"electron","title":"Oxidation states and redox agents","labels":["Zn loses 2e⁻","Cu²⁺ gains 2e⁻","Oxidation number rises","Oxidation number falls"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":65,"guidePage":59},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.2","title":"Balancing redox half-equations","hl":false,"summary":"Separate electron loss and gain, then combine so transferred electrons cancel.","explain":["A half-equation explicitly shows electrons and one of the two redox processes. In acidic aqueous solution, balance atoms other than O and H first, oxygen with H₂O, hydrogen with H⁺, then charge with electrons.","Multiply half-equations to give equal electron numbers before adding. The combined equation must conserve both atoms and charge. In neutral solutions use species appropriate to the stated medium; do not insert unsupported reactants merely to make a numerical balance work."],"steps":["Balance the changing element, then O with water and H with H⁺ in acid.","Balance charge using electrons on the correct side.","Equalize electrons between half-equations, add, and cancel common species."],"example":{"q":"Balance MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ in acidic solution.","steps":["Balance oxygen by adding 4H₂O on the right.","Balance hydrogen by adding 8H⁺ on the left.","Left charge is +7 and right is +2, so add 5e⁻ to the left."],"answer":"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O."},"mistake":"Electrons are added to the side that makes charge balance, not the side with more atoms.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Balancing redox half-equations","labels":["Balance O with H₂O","Balance H with H⁺","Balance charge with e⁻","Cancel electrons in full equation"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":65,"guidePage":59},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.3","title":"Reactivity and displacement","hl":false,"summary":"Periodic position and displacement observations reveal relative oxidation or reduction tendencies.","explain":["An easily oxidized metal can transfer electrons to ions of a less easily oxidized metal, displacing the metal from solution. Down group 1, outer electrons are more shielded and more distant, so metal oxidation becomes easier.","Halogens show the opposite trend in reduction tendency: halogens higher in group 17 more readily gain electrons and can oxidize halide ions below them. In examination questions, metal-displacement evidence or supplied data should be used; a complete metal reactivity sequence need not be memorized for this outcome."],"steps":["Identify which element would lose electrons and which ion would gain them.","Use supplied displacement evidence or relevant group trend.","Write balanced electron-transfer and overall equations if reaction is predicted."],"example":{"q":"Explain Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂.","steps":["Chlorine lies above bromine in group 17 and is more readily reduced.","Cl₂ gains two electrons; two Br⁻ ions lose two electrons.","Chlorine therefore displaces bromine from bromide solution."],"answer":"Cl₂ is the oxidizing agent; Br⁻ is the reducing agent."},"mistake":"A halogen atom gaining an electron is reduced, even though its reactivity is being described.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Reactivity and displacement","labels":["More reactive metal → oxidation","Metal ion → reduction","Higher halogen displaces lower halide"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":65,"guidePage":59},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.4","title":"Reactive metals with dilute acids","hl":false,"summary":"Some metals reduce hydrogen ions to hydrogen gas while forming metal ions.","explain":["When a reactive metal meets an appropriate dilute acid such as hydrochloric or sulfuric acid, the metal can lose electrons and H⁺ can gain them. The products are a salt and hydrogen gas.","The metal’s common ion charge controls the equation ratio. This is a redox reaction, not simply neutralization by a metal hydroxide. The formation of gas can help monitor the reaction rate."],"steps":["Write metal → metal ion + electrons.","Use 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂ for the reduction.","Combine and include the acid’s spectator anion to write a molecular equation if asked."],"example":{"q":"Write the reaction of magnesium with dilute hydrochloric acid.","steps":["Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.","2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂.","Two chloride ions accompany Mg²⁺ in the salt."],"answer":"Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)."},"mistake":"Acid–metal reactions produce hydrogen; acid–carbonate reactions produce carbon dioxide.","visual":{"kind":"electron","title":"Reactive metals with dilute acids","labels":["Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂","Salt + hydrogen"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":65,"guidePage":59},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.5","title":"Anodes, cathodes and polarity","hl":false,"summary":"Oxidation is always at the anode; reduction is always at the cathode.","explain":["The electrode names are defined by the reactions, not by a universal sign. The anode is where electrons are released by oxidation; the cathode is where electrons are consumed by reduction.","In a spontaneous voltaic cell the anode is negative and cathode positive. In an electrolytic cell, the external supply forces non-spontaneous change, so the anode is connected to positive and cathode to negative. Keep the definitions fixed while changing the signs according to cell type."],"steps":["Identify which electrode reaction loses electrons: this is anode.","Identify electron gain: this is cathode.","Assign polarity only after identifying whether the cell is voltaic or electrolytic."],"example":{"q":"State the signs of the anode and cathode in an electrolytic cell.","steps":["The external supply removes electrons from the anode.","It supplies electrons to the cathode.","Thus oxidation is at positive and reduction at negative."],"answer":"Electrolytic anode +; cathode −."},"mistake":"“Anode is always negative” is false; oxidation is the permanent definition.","visual":{"kind":"cell","title":"Anodes, cathodes and polarity","labels":["Anode: oxidation always","Cathode: reduction always","Voltaic: anode −","Electrolytic: anode +"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":66,"guidePage":60},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.6","title":"Primary voltaic cells","hl":false,"summary":"A spontaneous redox reaction can drive electron flow through an external circuit.","explain":["A voltaic cell separates oxidation and reduction into connected half-cells. Electrons flow in the external wire from the anode to the cathode. Separating the half-reactions allows useful electrical work rather than direct local electron transfer.","A salt bridge completes the ionic circuit and prevents charge build-up. In a common metal/metal-ion cell, anions move toward the anode compartment where positive metal ions are produced, and cations move toward the cathode compartment where positive ions are consumed."],"steps":["Write and label the oxidation and reduction half-equations.","Draw the electron path from anode through wire to cathode.","Explain salt-bridge ion movement using charge balance, not electron transport through the bridge."],"example":{"q":"Describe the Zn/Cu cell electron flow and salt-bridge ion movement.","steps":["Zn oxidizes at the anode: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻.","Electrons travel through the wire to Cu²⁺ reduction at the copper cathode.","Anions migrate toward Zn; cations migrate toward Cu to balance charge."],"answer":"Electrons: Zn anode → Cu cathode; ions carry charge through the bridge."},"mistake":"Electrons do not travel through the salt bridge.","visual":{"kind":"cell","title":"Primary voltaic cells","labels":["Zn anode −","Wire electron flow →","Cu cathode +","Anions → anode","Cations → cathode"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":66,"guidePage":60},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.7","title":"Secondary cells and storage choices","hl":false,"summary":"Rechargeable cells use electrode reactions that can be driven in reverse.","explain":["A primary cell is used until its reactants are depleted and is not designed for practical recharging. A secondary cell can be recharged by an external electrical supply that drives the discharge reactions in reverse.","Compare systems by their purpose: secondary cells are reusable but need energy to recharge and degrade with cycles; primary cells can be convenient but create replacement waste; fuel cells operate while fuel and oxidant are continuously supplied and depend on fuel production and storage. Environmental impact includes the full energy and material supply chain."],"steps":["Identify the given discharge half-reactions.","Reverse both half-equations to represent charging.","Compare practical advantages and drawbacks without claiming any technology has no environmental cost."],"example":{"q":"A discharge half-reaction is M²⁺ + 2e⁻ → M. Write the corresponding charging reaction.","steps":["Charging reverses the chemical change at that electrode.","Move products and reactants to opposite sides.","Check that atoms and charge remain balanced."],"answer":"M → M²⁺ + 2e⁻."},"mistake":"A rechargeable battery is an energy store, not an unlimited energy source.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Secondary cells and storage choices","labels":["Primary: replace","Secondary: recharge","Fuel cell: continuous supply","Charging reverses reactions"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":66,"guidePage":60},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.8","title":"Electrolysis of molten salts","hl":false,"summary":"Electrical energy can force an otherwise non-spontaneous chemical transformation.","explain":["An electrolytic cell contains an electrolyte and electrodes connected to a DC supply. Electrons move in wires; ions move through the electrolyte. Cations travel toward the negative cathode, where they can gain electrons, while anions travel toward the positive anode, where they can lose electrons.","A molten binary salt is simpler than an aqueous solution because water is absent as a competing reagent. Predict products from the ions present and balance their electrode equations. Molten ionic materials conduct because ions are mobile, unlike the rigid solid."],"steps":["List the ions in the molten salt.","At the cathode, reduce the cation.","At the anode, oxidize the anion and combine electrode equations if asked."],"example":{"q":"Predict products from electrolysis of molten PbBr₂.","steps":["The melt contains Pb²⁺ and Br⁻.","Cathode: Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb.","Anode: 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻."],"answer":"Lead at cathode and bromine at anode."},"mistake":"Use aqueous competition rules only when water is actually present.","visual":{"kind":"cell","title":"Electrolysis of molten salts","labels":["DC power","Cathode: cation reduction","Anode: anion oxidation","Mobile ions in melt"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":66,"guidePage":60},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.9","title":"Oxidation of alcohols","hl":false,"summary":"Primary alcohols can form aldehydes then carboxylic acids; secondary alcohols form ketones.","explain":["A primary alcohol can undergo two oxidation stages: RCH₂OH → RCHO → RCOOH. A secondary alcohol R₂CHOH forms a ketone R₂C=O. Tertiary alcohols are not oxidized under comparable ordinary conditions because the carbon bearing OH has no attached hydrogen available for the same transformation.","Distillation can remove an aldehyde as it forms, limiting further oxidation. Reflux returns volatile material to the reaction mixture, allowing continued heating and more complete oxidation to a carboxylic acid. The guide does not require memorizing named oxidizing agents or detailed oxidation mechanisms here."],"steps":["Classify the alcohol by the carbon bearing OH.","Choose aldehyde/acid for primary or ketone for secondary.","For a primary alcohol, match distillation to aldehyde isolation and reflux to further oxidation."],"example":{"q":"Give the oxidation sequence for ethanol and the conditions that favour ethanoic acid.","steps":["Ethanol CH₃CH₂OH first forms ethanal CH₃CHO.","Ethanal can oxidize further to ethanoic acid CH₃COOH.","Heating under reflux retains material for continued oxidation."],"answer":"CH₃CH₂OH → CH₃CHO → CH₃COOH; reflux favours the acid."},"mistake":"“All alcohols oxidize to ketones” is incorrect.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Oxidation of alcohols","labels":["Primary alcohol","Aldehyde","Carboxylic acid","Secondary alcohol → ketone"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":66,"guidePage":60},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.10","title":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","hl":false,"summary":"Organic reduction can add hydrogen equivalents and lower carbon oxidation state.","explain":["Carboxylic acids can be reduced to primary alcohols through an aldehyde stage; ketones reduce to secondary alcohols. These changes can be represented with [H] as a shorthand for reducing equivalents while keeping the organic formulas clear.","Hydride ions supply hydrogen with an electron pair in organic reduction. You need to understand their role, but the guide does not assess specific named reducing agents or the detailed reduction mechanism for this outcome."],"steps":["Identify carboxylic acid, aldehyde or ketone functionality.","Replace the carbonyl functionality with the appropriate alcohol product.","Balance using hydrogen equivalents and water where required."],"example":{"q":"Predict the reduction product of propanone, CH₃COCH₃.","steps":["Propanone contains a ketone carbonyl.","Reduction adds hydrogen equivalents across the carbonyl group.","The carbonyl carbon gains H and its oxygen becomes OH."],"answer":"CH₃CH(OH)CH₃, propan-2-ol."},"mistake":"Reduction of a ketone produces a secondary alcohol, not a primary alcohol.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Reduction of carbonyl compounds","labels":["Carboxylic acid","Aldehyde intermediate","Primary alcohol","Ketone → secondary alcohol"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":66,"guidePage":60},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.11","title":"Hydrogenation of unsaturated compounds","hl":false,"summary":"Adding hydrogen reduces multiple bonds and decreases unsaturation.","explain":["Alkenes and alkynes contain carbon–carbon multiple bonds. Adding H₂ converts a π-bond component into new C–H σ bonds, reducing the degree of unsaturation. Full hydrogenation of an alkene gives an alkane; an alkyne can add two H₂ molecules overall.","This is reduction because the carbon atoms gain hydrogen. Product prediction requires preserving the carbon skeleton and adding the correct number of hydrogen atoms, not breaking the C–C framework."],"steps":["Identify the number and position of multiple bonds.","Add one H₂ per C=C to make a C–C single bond.","For complete C≡C saturation, add two H₂ molecules."],"example":{"q":"Write complete hydrogenation of ethyne.","steps":["Ethyne is HC≡CH.","The triple bond has two π components to hydrogenate.","Adding two H₂ produces CH₃CH₃."],"answer":"C₂H₂ + 2H₂ → C₂H₆."},"mistake":"One mole of H₂ is not enough to convert one mole of an alkyne completely to an alkane.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Hydrogenation of unsaturated compounds","labels":["C≡C + 2H₂","C–C saturated","Fewer π bonds","More C–H bonds"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":67,"guidePage":61},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.12","title":"Standard electrode potentials","hl":true,"summary":"Reduction potentials compare half-cells with the standard hydrogen electrode.","explain":["The standard hydrogen half-cell is assigned E° = 0 V by convention. Other standard electrode potentials are measured relative to it under standard conditions. The data booklet lists reduction potentials.","A more positive standard reduction potential indicates a greater thermodynamic tendency for that reduction compared with a less positive value. Reversing a half-equation reverses its potential sign, but multiplying the equation does not multiply E° because potential is an intensive quantity."],"steps":["Read listed equations in their reduction direction.","Compare potentials: more positive reduction is favoured.","Use the hydrogen electrode reference and the stated standard conditions in explanations."],"example":{"q":"Compare Cu²⁺/Cu at +0.34 V and Zn²⁺/Zn at −0.76 V. Which reduction is more favourable under standard conditions?","steps":["Both values are listed as reduction potentials.","+0.34 V is more positive than −0.76 V.","Cu²⁺ therefore has the stronger tendency to accept electrons."],"answer":"Cu²⁺ → Cu is the more favourable reduction."},"mistake":"Do not multiply an electrode potential when multiplying a half-equation to balance electrons.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Standard electrode potentials","labels":["Hydrogen reference: 0 V","More positive E°","Greater reduction tendency","E° not multiplied"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":67,"guidePage":61},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.13","title":"Calculating standard cell potential","hl":true,"summary":"A positive E°cell predicts a thermodynamically spontaneous forward reaction under standard conditions.","explain":["Using tabulated reduction potentials, E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode. The cathode is the reduction chosen for the proposed forward reaction, while the anode half-equation runs in reverse as oxidation.","A positive result corresponds to the spontaneous standard direction. A negative value means the proposed forward reaction is non-spontaneous under standard conditions and the reverse is favoured. This does not predict how fast a reaction occurs."],"steps":["Choose the reduction and oxidation half-equations for the stated direction.","Use both tabulated values as reduction potentials in E°cathode − E°anode.","Interpret the sign and check electron balance in the overall reaction."],"example":{"q":"Calculate E°cell for Zn/Cu from E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V.","steps":["Cu²⁺ is reduced at the cathode.","Zn is oxidized at the anode, but use its listed reduction potential in the subtraction.","E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76)."],"answer":"E°cell = +1.10 V; standard forward reaction is spontaneous."},"mistake":"Subtract the anode’s listed reduction potential; do not accidentally reverse its sign twice.","visual":{"kind":"cell","title":"Calculating standard cell potential","labels":["Cathode: +0.34 V","Anode reduction value: −0.76 V","Subtract","Cell: +1.10 V"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":67,"guidePage":61},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.14","title":"Cell potential and Gibbs energy","hl":true,"summary":"ΔG° = −nFE°cell relates electrical potential to thermodynamic driving force.","explain":["The number n is the moles of electrons transferred per mole of the balanced overall reaction. Faraday’s constant F gives charge per mole of electrons. Multiplying nF by cell potential gives energy, with the negative sign linking a positive cell potential to a negative standard Gibbs change.","Use E in volts and F in C mol⁻¹ to obtain J mol⁻¹. Changing the scaling of an overall reaction changes n and ΔG°, but not E°. This is why cell potential and reaction energy must not be treated as interchangeable numerical quantities."],"steps":["Balance the half-reactions and determine n.","Substitute n, F and E°cell into −nFE°cell.","Convert joules to kilojoules and check that a positive E gives negative ΔG°."],"example":{"q":"Find ΔG° for a cell with E° = 1.10 V transferring two electrons. Use F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹.","steps":["n = 2.","ΔG° = −2 × 96,500 × 1.10 = −212,300 J mol⁻¹.","Divide by 1000."],"answer":"ΔG° ≈ −212 kJ mol⁻¹."},"mistake":"n is the balanced electron-transfer count, not the sum of every coefficient in the equation.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Cell potential and Gibbs energy","labels":["ΔG° = −nFE°","n: electron count","Positive E°","Negative ΔG°"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":67,"guidePage":61},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.15","title":"Electrolysis of aqueous solutions","hl":true,"summary":"Water competes with dissolved ions for oxidation and reduction at electrodes.","explain":["Unlike a melt, an aqueous electrolyte contains water, which can also participate at each electrode. Product prediction therefore compares possible reductions at the cathode and possible oxidations at the anode, guided by supplied standard potentials and the specified conditions.","Concentration and electrode material can matter in the syllabus examples, notably aqueous sodium chloride and copper(II) sulfate. For aqueous NaCl, hydrogen is formed at the cathode; concentrated chloride can produce chlorine at an inert anode, while in sufficiently dilute chloride solution oxygen production from water becomes more important. In copper sulfate with inert electrodes, copper can plate at the cathode while water is oxidized at the anode; with a copper anode, the electrode itself can oxidize and dissolve."],"steps":["List both dissolved-ion and water electrode possibilities.","Use the relevant E° data and specified conditions to identify competing processes.","Check whether the anode is inert or can itself oxidize."],"example":{"q":"Compare the anode process in CuSO₄(aq) using an inert anode versus a copper anode.","steps":["With an inert anode, the electrode does not provide copper for oxidation.","Water can oxidize: 2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻.","With copper anode, Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ replenishes dissolved copper ions."],"answer":"Inert anode: oxygen formation; copper anode: copper dissolves."},"mistake":"Do not predict aqueous electrolysis exactly as if water were absent.","visual":{"kind":"cell","title":"Electrolysis of aqueous solutions","labels":["Water competes","Cathode reduction choices","Anode oxidation choices","Electrode material matters"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":67,"guidePage":61},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.2.16","title":"Electroplating","hl":true,"summary":"An object is coated by reducing metal ions onto its surface at the cathode.","explain":["Electroplating uses an electrolytic cell to deposit a thin metallic layer. The object to be coated is connected as the cathode so metal cations can gain electrons on its surface. The electrolyte contains ions of the coating metal.","An anode made from the coating metal can dissolve to replace ions consumed at the cathode. The electrode equations therefore connect the loss of anode material with the gain of metal on the object. The current supply drives the overall transfer."],"steps":["Make the object the negative cathode.","Choose an electrolyte containing the desired metal ions.","Write cathodic metal deposition and, if appropriate, anodic metal dissolution."],"example":{"q":"Write copper electrode equations for copper-plating an object using a copper anode.","steps":["At the object, Cu²⁺ gains two electrons.","At the copper anode, Cu loses two electrons.","The dissolved ions transfer copper material between electrodes."],"answer":"Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu; anode: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻."},"mistake":"The plated object is the cathode, not the anode.","visual":{"kind":"cell","title":"Electroplating","labels":["Coating metal anode","Metal-ion electrolyte","Object = cathode","Mⁿ⁺ + ne⁻ → M"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":67,"guidePage":61},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"}]},{"id":"R3.3","name":"Electron sharing reactions","group":"Reactivity 3","sections":[{"id":"R3.3.1","title":"Radicals and unpaired electrons","hl":false,"summary":"A radical contains an unpaired electron and is often highly reactive.","explain":["A radical is identified by an unpaired electron, shown as a dot beside its symbol or formula. Radical does not mean charged: a radical may be electrically neutral or may also have charge. The important feature is its electron pairing.","For example Cl• and CH₃• each have an unpaired electron available for reaction. Because pairing that electron can create a new covalent bond, radicals often react rapidly with suitable molecules or other radicals."],"steps":["Count or inspect the outer electrons.","Identify an unpaired electron rather than merely a formal charge.","Show one dot for that unpaired electron in the species notation."],"example":{"q":"Explain why Cl• is a radical but Cl⁻ is not ordinarily described as one.","steps":["Cl• has seven valence electrons including one unpaired electron.","Cl⁻ has a complete paired octet.","The dot, not a negative charge, identifies radical character."],"answer":"Cl• has an unpaired electron; Cl⁻ has paired valence electrons."},"mistake":"A dot represents an unpaired electron; a charge symbol is a different property.","visual":{"kind":"electron","title":"Radicals and unpaired electrons","labels":["Unpaired electron •","Radical ≠ necessarily ion","Cl•","CH₃•"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":68,"guidePage":62},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.3.2","title":"Homolytic fission and initiation","hl":false,"summary":"Homolysis splits a bond so each fragment keeps one bonding electron.","explain":["When a bond breaks homolytically, the shared electron pair is divided equally: one electron goes to each atom. UV light or sufficient heat can initiate this process for halogen molecules, generating reactive radicals.","The electron movement is represented by single-barbed fish-hook arrows, each showing one electron. This is the initiation stage of a radical chain reaction because it creates radicals from non-radical starting molecules."],"steps":["Identify the bond broken by UV light or heat.","Assign one bonding electron to each fragment.","Show two radical products and use fish-hook rather than full electron-pair arrows."],"example":{"q":"Write the initiation equation for methane chlorination.","steps":["The chlorine molecule contains a Cl–Cl bond.","UV promotes homolytic bond fission.","Each Cl atom retains one bonding electron."],"answer":"Cl₂ → 2Cl• under UV light."},"mistake":"Homolysis does not produce Cl⁺ and Cl⁻; those would be heterolytic products.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Homolytic fission and initiation","labels":["Cl–Cl","UV / heat","One e⁻ to each atom","2 Cl• radicals"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":68,"guidePage":62},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.3.3","title":"Radical substitution chains","hl":false,"summary":"Propagation regenerates radicals; termination removes them.","explain":["In alkane halogenation, a halogen radical can remove H from an alkane to produce a hydrogen halide and an alkyl radical. The alkyl radical reacts with a halogen molecule to make a halogenoalkane and regenerate a halogen radical. These are propagation steps.","A chain ends when two radicals combine without producing a new radical. Multiple substitution and different attack sites produce product mixtures. Alkanes are relatively unreactive without initiation because their C–C and C–H bonds are strong and their bonds are essentially non-polar."],"steps":["Write initiation to create radicals.","Write propagation steps that consume one radical and produce another.","Write termination by combining two radicals into a non-radical product."],"example":{"q":"Give two propagation steps and one termination step for methane chlorination.","steps":["Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + CH₃•.","CH₃• + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•.","A possible termination is Cl• + CH₃• → CH₃Cl."],"answer":"Propagation regenerates the chain carrier; termination removes radicals."},"mistake":"A propagation step must regenerate radical character; two radicals joining is termination.","visual":{"kind":"cycle","title":"Radical substitution chains","labels":["Cl• attacks CH₄","CH₃• formed","CH₃• attacks Cl₂","Cl• regenerated","Radicals combine to terminate"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":68,"guidePage":62},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"}]},{"id":"R3.4","name":"Electron-pair sharing reactions","group":"Reactivity 3","sections":[{"id":"R3.4.1","title":"Recognizing nucleophiles","hl":false,"summary":"A nucleophile forms a bond by donating an electron pair.","explain":["A nucleophile is an electron-pair donor in a bond-forming reaction. It needs available electron density, often a lone pair. It may be negatively charged, such as OH⁻, or neutral, such as NH₃ or H₂O.","The reaction partner accepting the pair is an electrophile. A full curly arrow begins at the donated electron pair and points to the site where the new bond forms; arrows show movement of electrons, not movement of atoms."],"steps":["Look for an available lone pair or other electron-rich region.","Identify the electron-poor atom it can attack.","Draw the arrow from the electron pair toward that atom."],"example":{"q":"Why can both OH⁻ and NH₃ act as nucleophiles?","steps":["O in OH⁻ has available lone pairs.","N in NH₃ also has a lone pair even though the molecule is neutral.","Each can donate both electrons of a new bond."],"answer":"Both are electron-pair donors."},"mistake":"Nucleophiles need not be negatively charged.","visual":{"kind":"electron","title":"Recognizing nucleophiles","labels":["Lone-pair donor","OH⁻","NH₃","Electron pair → electrophile"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":68,"guidePage":62},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.2","title":"Nucleophilic substitution","hl":false,"summary":"A nucleophile forms a new bond while a leaving group departs.","explain":["In nucleophilic substitution, one group replaces another at an electrophilic atom. A polar C–halogen bond makes carbon partially positive, allowing an electron-rich nucleophile to donate a pair toward it.","The leaving group takes the old bond’s electron pair as that bond breaks. At SL, explain and represent the pair movement and overall equation; the distinct detailed mechanisms of primary and tertiary halogenoalkanes are HL extensions."],"steps":["Locate the electrophilic atom attached to the leaving group.","Show the nucleophile donating a pair to form a new bond.","Show the old bond pair going to the leaving group and balance the overall equation."],"example":{"q":"Predict the substitution product of bromoethane with aqueous hydroxide.","steps":["OH⁻ donates an electron pair to the carbon bonded to Br.","The C–Br bond breaks with its pair going to Br.","OH replaces Br in the organic molecule."],"answer":"CH₃CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂OH + Br⁻."},"mistake":"The leaving group departs with the bonding electrons in this substitution model.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Nucleophilic substitution","labels":["Nucleophile","δ+ carbon","New C–Nu bond","Leaving group takes pair"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":68,"guidePage":62},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.3","title":"Heterolytic fission and curly arrows","hl":false,"summary":"Heterolysis gives both bonding electrons to one fragment, commonly forming ions.","explain":["In heterolytic bond fission, one atom receives the entire shared pair. This produces electron-rich and electron-poor fragments, often an anion and a cation. It contrasts with homolysis, which gives one electron to each fragment.","A full double-barbed curly arrow represents movement of an electron pair. For bond breaking, its tail begins at the bond and its head ends at the atom receiving that pair. Correct arrow placement is part of the chemical explanation."],"steps":["Identify which atom receives the bonding pair.","Draw a full curly arrow from the bond toward that atom.","Assign resulting formal charges and verify overall charge conservation."],"example":{"q":"Represent heterolytic breaking of a C–Br bond when Br receives both bonding electrons.","steps":["Both C–Br bonding electrons move toward Br.","The bromine fragment becomes Br⁻.","The carbon fragment becomes electron-deficient and positively charged if no simultaneous bond forms."],"answer":"R–Br → R⁺ + Br⁻ in the isolated heterolytic representation."},"mistake":"A full curly arrow moves two electrons; a fish-hook moves one.","visual":{"kind":"electron","title":"Heterolytic fission and curly arrows","labels":["Bond pair → one atom","Cation fragment","Anion fragment","Full curly arrow"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":69,"guidePage":63},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.4","title":"Recognizing electrophiles","hl":false,"summary":"An electrophile accepts an electron pair when forming a bond.","explain":["An electrophile is attracted to available electron density because it can accept an electron pair. It may be positively charged, such as H⁺, or neutral but electron-deficient or polarized, such as a molecule with a partially positive carbon.","Classify electrophiles by their electron-accepting role rather than requiring a plus sign in the formula. A full curly arrow points toward the electron-deficient site where the new bond is formed."],"steps":["Identify a positive, partially positive or electron-deficient centre.","Find the nucleophile that can supply a pair.","Point the electron-pair arrow toward the electrophilic atom."],"example":{"q":"Explain why the carbon bonded to Br in bromoethane is electrophilic.","steps":["Bromine attracts the C–Br bonding electrons more strongly.","The carbon has a partial positive charge.","It can be attacked by an electron-pair donor."],"answer":"The polarized C–Br bond makes its carbon electron-poor."},"mistake":"An electrophile can be a neutral species containing a partially positive site.","visual":{"kind":"electron","title":"Recognizing electrophiles","labels":["Electron-pair acceptor","Positive or δ+ centre","H⁺ example","Arrow points to acceptor"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":69,"guidePage":63},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.5","title":"Electrophilic addition to alkenes","hl":false,"summary":"The electron-rich double bond reacts with electrophiles to give addition products.","explain":["An alkene’s π electron density is accessible above and below the carbon framework, making the double bond susceptible to electrophilic attack. Addition replaces the π component with new σ bonds while the original carbon–carbon connection remains.","Alkenes add halogens, hydrogen halides and water under suitable conditions. Bromine decolourization is useful evidence for unsaturation: the bromine is consumed in forming an addition product. Detailed mechanisms are HL content rather than required at SL here."],"steps":["Locate the C=C bond.","Replace it with C–C and add the reagent fragments to the two carbons.","Preserve the carbon skeleton and check the final atom count."],"example":{"q":"Predict the reaction of ethene with bromine.","steps":["Ethene has a C=C double bond.","One Br attaches to each of the two carbons as the π bond is consumed.","The product is a vicinal dibromoalkane."],"answer":"CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br, 1,2-dibromoethane."},"mistake":"Addition does not replace an existing hydrogen as substitution does.","visual":{"kind":"equation","title":"Electrophilic addition to alkenes","labels":["C=C electron rich","Electrophilic attack","π bond consumed","Two new σ bonds"]},"source":{"hl":false,"pdfPage":69,"guidePage":63},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.6","title":"Lewis acid–base theory","hl":true,"summary":"A Lewis acid accepts an electron pair; a Lewis base donates one.","explain":["The Lewis model describes acid–base reactions using electron pairs rather than requiring proton transfer. A Lewis base has a pair available for donation; a Lewis acid has a site able to accept that pair.","Brønsted proton-transfer reactions can often also be interpreted in Lewis terms: the base donates a pair to H⁺. The Lewis definition also covers reactions with no transferred proton, such as NH₃ donating to electron-deficient BF₃."],"steps":["Find the species supplying the electron pair.","Identify the accepting centre.","Classify donor as Lewis base and acceptor as Lewis acid."],"example":{"q":"Classify NH₃ and BF₃ in formation of H₃N→BF₃.","steps":["Nitrogen in NH₃ has an available lone pair.","Boron in BF₃ can accept that pair.","The donor is base and acceptor acid."],"answer":"NH₃ is Lewis base; BF₃ is Lewis acid."},"mistake":"Lewis acid–base theory is not limited to species donating or accepting protons.","visual":{"kind":"electron","title":"Lewis acid–base theory","labels":["Lewis base donates","Lewis acid accepts","Lone pair → empty accepting site"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":69,"guidePage":63},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.7","title":"Coordinate bonds in Lewis reactions","hl":true,"summary":"Lewis acid–base bonding forms a shared pair originally supplied by the base.","explain":["When a Lewis base donates a pair to a Lewis acid, a coordination bond forms. It is a covalent bond whose two electrons originally came from one atom. After formation, its shared-pair description is like other covalent bonds.","A Lewis structure of the reactants should show the donor lone pair, while the product shows the new bond. Nucleophiles function as Lewis bases and electrophiles as Lewis acids because they have the same pair-donation and pair-acceptance roles."],"steps":["Draw the donor lone pair in the reactant Lewis structure.","Draw an arrow from donor to acceptor for bond formation.","Draw the bonded product, checking electron count and overall charge."],"example":{"q":"Explain formation of NH₄⁺ from NH₃ and H⁺ using Lewis terms.","steps":["NH₃ supplies the nitrogen lone pair.","H⁺ accepts the pair.","The new N–H bond forms and the overall product has +1 charge."],"answer":"NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺; the new bond has a shared pair from N."},"mistake":"The coordinate-bond arrow points from donor to acceptor, not from positive ion to donor.","visual":{"kind":"flow","title":"Coordinate bonds in Lewis reactions","labels":["NH₃ lone pair","H⁺ acceptor","N→H bond forms","NH₄⁺"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":69,"guidePage":63},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.8","title":"Ligands and complex-ion charges","hl":true,"summary":"Ligands donate electron pairs to a central transition-metal cation.","explain":["A ligand is an ion or molecule with a donor electron pair that can coordinate to a metal centre. Neutral ligands such as H₂O or NH₃ contribute zero formal charge to the overall complex, while anionic ligands such as Cl⁻ contribute their ionic charge.","The total charge on a complex is the metal oxidation state plus the sum of ligand charges. This makes it possible either to determine complex charge from its components or infer the metal oxidation state from a known complex charge."],"steps":["List the metal oxidation state and every ligand charge.","Multiply each ligand charge by its count.","Add charges and write the complex in brackets with its overall charge."],"example":{"q":"Find the charge on a complex containing Co³⁺ and six NH₃ ligands.","steps":["Metal contributes +3.","Each ammonia ligand is neutral, so six contribute 0.","Total charge is +3 + 0."],"answer":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺."},"mistake":"Neutral ligands do not cancel the positive charge merely because they donate electrons into bonds.","visual":{"kind":"complex","title":"Ligands and complex-ion charges","labels":["Metal cation","Ligand lone pairs","Coordinate bonds","Total charge = charge sum"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":70,"guidePage":64},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.9","title":"SN1 and SN2 mechanisms","hl":true,"summary":"Primary and tertiary halogenoalkanes follow contrasting substitution pathways in the syllabus models.","explain":["The SN2 model is a concerted one-step process: a nucleophile attacks from the back of the C–leaving-group bond as that bond breaks. It has one transition state and no carbocation intermediate. Backside attack gives inversion at a suitable chiral centre, and rate = k[halogenoalkane][nucleophile].","The SN1 model has a slow ionization step producing a carbocation and leaving group, followed by nucleophile attack. It has two steps and an intermediate, with rate = k[halogenoalkane]. Primary halogenoalkanes are treated by SN2, tertiary by SN1, and secondary substrates can follow either in the guide’s scope."],"steps":["Classify the carbon bonded to the leaving group as primary, secondary or tertiary.","For SN2 draw simultaneous attack and bond cleavage; for SN1 draw ionization then attack.","Relate steps to rate law, intermediate presence and stereochemical consequence."],"example":{"q":"Contrast hydroxide substitution of a primary bromoalkane with a tertiary bromoalkane.","steps":["Primary: one-step backside attack; both reactants participate in the rate-determining event.","Tertiary: slow C–Br ionization forms a carbocation, followed by OH⁻ attack.","Rate laws are bimolecular and unimolecular respectively."],"answer":"Primary SN2: k[RX][OH⁻]; tertiary SN1: k[RX]."},"mistake":"SN1 has a carbocation intermediate; SN2 has a transition state but no intermediate.","visual":{"kind":"mechanism","title":"SN1 and SN2 mechanisms","labels":["Primary: SN2 one step","Backside attack / inversion","Tertiary: SN1 two steps","Carbocation intermediate"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":70,"guidePage":64},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.10","title":"Leaving groups and substitution rates","hl":true,"summary":"A weaker carbon–halogen bond generally makes leaving-group departure easier.","explain":["For otherwise comparable halogenoalkanes, substitution commonly becomes faster from chloro- to bromo- to iodo- compounds. The C–I bond is weaker than C–Br, which is weaker than C–Cl, so cleavage is easier.","Explain the trend using the relevant bond strength and leaving-group ability, not only the polarity of the bond. The guide does not assess detailed solvent effects or mechanism-dependent solvent changes for this outcome."],"steps":["Keep the carbon skeleton and other conditions comparable.","Compare C–halogen bond strength.","Predict faster substitution when the leaving group can depart more readily."],"example":{"q":"Rank CH₃CH₂Cl, CH₃CH₂Br and CH₃CH₂I by expected substitution rate under comparable conditions.","steps":["C–Cl is strongest among these bonds.","C–I is weakest and cleaves most readily.","The bromo compound is intermediate."],"answer":"CH₃CH₂I > CH₃CH₂Br > CH₃CH₂Cl in rate."},"mistake":"Greater C–Cl bond polarity alone does not mean the chloro compound reacts fastest.","visual":{"kind":"comparison","title":"Leaving groups and substitution rates","labels":["C–Cl: stronger bond","C–Br: intermediate","C–I: weaker bond","Iodo compound usually faster"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":70,"guidePage":64},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.11","title":"Mechanisms of electrophilic addition","hl":true,"summary":"The alkene π pair initiates addition by attacking an electrophilic reagent.","explain":["For hydrogen halide addition, the π electron pair attacks H while the H–X bond pair goes to X. A carbocation forms; the halide then donates a pair to the carbocation. Acid-catalysed hydration similarly involves protonation, water attack and loss of a proton to give an alcohol.","Halogen addition begins as the electron-rich double bond polarizes the halogen molecule; electrophilic attack and halide capture produce a dihalogenoalkane. Show electron pairs moving from electron-rich bonds or lone pairs toward electron-poor centres. For detailed intermediate drawings, follow the treatment specified for the course rather than inventing unsupported charges."],"steps":["Start the first curly arrow at the π bond toward the electrophile.","Show breaking of the relevant reagent bond and formation of the intermediate.","Complete attack by the electron-pair donor and any required deprotonation."],"example":{"q":"Describe the pair movements in adding HBr to ethene.","steps":["The C=C π pair forms a bond to H; the H–Br pair moves to Br.","An ethyl carbocation and Br⁻ result in the stepwise representation.","A Br⁻ lone pair forms the second new bond at the cationic carbon."],"answer":"Ethene + HBr → bromoethane through electrophilic attack then bromide attack."},"mistake":"Curly arrows start at electrons, never at a bare positive charge.","visual":{"kind":"mechanism","title":"Mechanisms of electrophilic addition","labels":["π pair → H","H–Br pair → Br","Carbocation","Br⁻ pair → C⁺"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":70,"guidePage":64},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.12","title":"Unsymmetrical addition and carbocation stability","hl":true,"summary":"The more stable possible carbocation helps explain the major addition product.","explain":["An unsymmetrical alkene can form different carbocations when protonated. More alkyl substitution generally stabilizes a carbocation: tertiary is more stable than secondary, which is more stable than primary in the simple comparison used here.","The pathway through the more stable carbocation is generally favoured, producing a major product rather than necessarily an exclusive one. Predict both possible connectivities before deciding which dominates for hydrogen halide addition or acid-catalysed hydration."],"steps":["Draw the two possible proton-addition orientations.","Classify the resulting carbocations.","Choose the more stable intermediate and attach halide or water-derived OH at that carbon."],"example":{"q":"Predict the major product from propene with HBr.","steps":["H addition to the terminal carbon leaves a secondary carbocation on the central carbon.","The alternative leaves a less stable primary carbocation.","Br⁻ attacks the more favoured secondary carbocation."],"answer":"2-bromopropane is the major product."},"mistake":"Say major product, not the only possible product, unless the question establishes exclusivity.","visual":{"kind":"mechanism","title":"Unsymmetrical addition and carbocation stability","labels":["Unsymmetrical alkene","Possible carbocations","Secondary > primary","Major product via more stable cation"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":70,"guidePage":64},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"},{"id":"R3.4.13","title":"Electrophilic substitution of benzene","hl":true,"summary":"Benzene replaces hydrogen to regain its delocalized ring after electrophilic attack.","explain":["Benzene’s π electrons attack a charged electrophile E⁺ to make a C–E bond, temporarily disrupting the ring’s delocalized system and producing a positively charged intermediate. This costs aromatic stabilization.","A proton is then removed from the carbon bearing E; the C–H electron pair restores the delocalized π system. The overall change substitutes E for H rather than adding permanently across a ring bond. Formation of the electrophile itself is not assessed for this outcome."],"steps":["Draw a curly arrow from ring π electron density to E⁺.","Show the positively charged intermediate with E and H attached to the attacked carbon.","Show C–H bond electrons restoring the ring as H⁺ is lost."],"example":{"q":"Explain why benzene commonly undergoes substitution rather than simple addition with an electrophile.","steps":["Initial electrophilic attack temporarily breaks the fully delocalized ring arrangement.","Losing H⁺ allows the C–H bonding pair to restore delocalization.","Substitution recovers the stabilized π system, whereas simple addition would leave it disrupted."],"answer":"Substitution restores the delocalized aromatic ring."},"mistake":"Do not leave the final substitution product as a permanently localized carbocation.","visual":{"kind":"mechanism","title":"Electrophilic substitution of benzene","labels":["Ring attacks E⁺","Positive intermediate","H⁺ removed","Delocalization restored"]},"source":{"hl":true,"pdfPage":70,"guidePage":64},"group":"Reactivity 3 · Mechanisms of chemical change"}]}];
const QUESTIONS = [{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which description identifies a compound?","options":["A single type of atom","Different elements bonded in a fixed ratio","Two substances physically mixed in any ratio","A substance separated by simple filtration"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Compounds consist of chemically bonded elements in a fixed ratio."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q001"},{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which change of state is deposition?","options":["Solid → liquid","Liquid → gas","Gas → solid","Solid → gas"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Deposition describes gas changing directly to solid."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q002"},{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"A liquid mixture has two components with different boiling points. Which method is most appropriate?","options":["Filtration","Distillation","Magnetic separation","Decantation of insoluble solid"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Distillation separates miscible liquids according to their boiling behaviours."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q003"},{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which state symbol represents a substance dissolved in water?","options":["(s)","(l)","(g)","(aq)"],"correct":3,"scheme":["(aq) means aqueous: dissolved in water."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q004"},{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Distinguish an element, a compound and a mixture.","scheme":["Element cannot be chemically broken down into simpler substances [1].","Compound contains different elements chemically bonded in a fixed ratio [1].","Mixture contains substances not chemically bonded in no fixed ratio [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q005"},{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"State how a solid dissolved in a liquid could be recovered and justify the method.","scheme":["Identify evaporation or recrystallization as a relevant method [1].","Explain that solvent is removed or crystals form from the solution [1].","Recognize that the solute is recovered by a physical separation rather than chemical change [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q006"},{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":3,"text":"A heating curve rises, becomes flat, then rises again. Explain the temperature plateau using the kinetic molecular model.","scheme":["The sample is undergoing a change of state [1].","Energy is used to overcome/interact with attractions rather than increasing average kinetic energy [1].","Therefore the temperature remains constant during the change [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q007"},{"topic":"S1.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":2,"text":"Convert 25.0 °C to kelvin. Explain what kelvin temperature measures.","scheme":["298.15 K [1].","Kelvin temperature measures average particle kinetic energy [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q008"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"A neutral atom contains 13 protons and 14 neutrons. Its mass number is","options":["13","14","27","1"],"correct":2,"scheme":["A = protons + neutrons = 27."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q009"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"An ion has atomic number 17 and charge −1. How many electrons does it have?","options":["16","17","18","19"],"correct":2,"scheme":["An extra electron produces a −1 charge: 17 + 1 = 18."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q010"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Isotopes of an element have different numbers of","options":["Protons","Electrons in every possible ion","Neutrons","Nuclei"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Isotopes have the same number of protons and different neutron counts."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q011"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"In a mass spectrum of a singly charged element, peaks at m/z 10 and 11 have abundances 20% and 80%. What is Aᵣ?","options":["10.2","10.5","10.8","11.2"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Weighted mean = 10(0.20)+11(0.80)=10.8."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q012"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"A species has A = 39, Z = 19 and charge +1. Determine its protons, neutrons and electrons.","scheme":["19 protons [1].","20 neutrons [1].","18 electrons [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q013"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":3,"text":"An element has isotopes of masses 63 and 65, with abundances 69% and 31%. Calculate its relative atomic mass.","scheme":["Use weighted mean with correct mass and percentage terms [1].","Aᵣ = 63(0.69) + 65(0.31) [1].","Aᵣ = 63.62 [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q014"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Explain how a mass spectrum can be used to determine relative atomic mass.","scheme":["Identify isotopic mass from peak position [1].","Identify relative abundance from peak intensity [1].","Calculate weighted mean of isotopic masses [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q015"},{"topic":"S1.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":2,"text":"Explain why isotopes can differ in physical properties despite being atoms of the same element.","scheme":["They have different neutron numbers and masses [1].","Mass differences can affect physical behaviour [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q016"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What is the maximum number of electrons in the n = 3 main energy level?","options":["6","8","18","32"],"correct":2,"scheme":["2n² = 2×3² = 18."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q017"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which statement correctly describes an orbital?","options":["A fixed electron path","A region with high probability of finding an electron","An electron itself","The entire nucleus"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Orbitals represent probable electron locations."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q018"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which rule explains why electrons occupy degenerate orbitals singly before pairing?","options":["Aufbau","Hund","Pauli","Octet"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Hund’s rule favours singly occupied orbitals first."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q019"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which light has the greatest photon energy?","options":["Longest wavelength","Lowest frequency","Shortest wavelength","Lowest speed in vacuum"],"correct":2,"scheme":["E = hc/λ, so smaller wavelength corresponds to greater energy."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q020"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What is the general first-ionization-energy trend down a group?","options":["Increases due to greater nuclear charge only","Decreases due to increased shielding and radius","Always stays constant","Increases because electrons get closer to the nucleus"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Greater atomic radius and shielding generally make electron removal easier."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q021"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Large jump between the third and fourth successive ionization energies suggests how many valence electrons?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"scheme":["The first three electrons are removed from the outer level before a core electron."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q022"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Describe three principles used to construct electron configurations.","scheme":["Aufbau: lower energy orbitals fill before higher [1].","Hund: equivalent orbitals singly occupied before pairing [1].","Pauli: maximum two opposite-spin electrons per orbital [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q023"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":2,"text":"Explain how a hydrogen line spectrum supports the idea of quantized energy.","scheme":["The spectrum has discrete wavelengths/lines rather than a continuous spectrum [1].","These correspond to transitions between fixed electron energy levels [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q024"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"HL","difficulty":"Hard","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":3,"text":"A spectral convergence limit is observed at frequency 3.29 × 10¹⁵ Hz. Calculate energy of a photon using h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.","scheme":["Correct substitution E = hf [1].","6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.29 × 10¹⁵ [1].","2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J per photon [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q025"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"HL","difficulty":"Hard","paper":"Paper 2","marks":4,"text":"Explain the overall pattern and two discontinuities in first ionization energies across a period.","scheme":["Effective nuclear attraction generally increases across a period [1].","Hence first ionization energy generally increases [1].","An electron entering a higher-energy p sublevel can produce a decrease [1].","Pairing in an orbital produces additional repulsion and can produce another decrease [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q026"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":2,"text":"Write the condensed electron configuration of chlorine (Z = 17) and state the number of occupied p orbitals in its outer shell.","scheme":["[Ne] 3s² 3p⁵ [1].","Three outer 3p orbitals occupied [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q027"},{"topic":"S1.3","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Give the ground-state configurations of Cr (Z = 24) and Cu (Z = 29) and explain why these are exceptions to a simple filling pattern.","scheme":["Cr: [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ [1].","Cu: [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹ [1].","Relative stability associated with half-filled and filled 3d subshells [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q028"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What is the amount of 18.0 g of water (M = 18.0 g mol⁻¹)?","options":["0.50 mol","1.00 mol","2.00 mol","18.0 mol"],"correct":1,"scheme":["n = 18.0 / 18.0 = 1.00 mol."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q029"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What is the molecular formula of a compound with empirical formula CH₂ and molar mass 56.0 g mol⁻¹? (C = 12, H = 1)","options":["CH₂","C₂H₄","C₄H₈","C₆H₁₂"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Empirical formula mass 14; multiplier 56/14 = 4."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q030"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What volume of a 0.200 mol dm⁻³ solution contains 0.0500 mol of solute?","options":["0.0100 dm³","0.100 dm³","0.250 dm³","4.00 dm³"],"correct":2,"scheme":["V = n/C = 0.0500/0.200 = 0.250 dm³."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q031"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"At identical temperature and pressure, 20 cm³ gas A and 60 cm³ gas B have mole ratio","options":["1:1","1:2","1:3","3:1"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Avogadro’s law makes mole ratio equal gas volume ratio."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q032"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Calculate the molar mass of CaCO₃ using Ca = 40.08, C = 12.01, O = 16.00.","scheme":["Include one Ca, one C and three O atoms [1].","40.08 + 12.01 + 3(16.00) [1].","100.09 g mol⁻¹ [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q033"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":4,"text":"A compound consists of 40.0% C, 6.7% H and 53.3% O by mass. Determine its empirical formula. Use C = 12.0, H = 1.0, O = 16.0.","scheme":["Assume a 100 g sample [1].","Calculate moles: C ≈ 3.33; H = 6.7; O ≈ 3.33 [1].","Divide by the smallest amounts to obtain ≈1:2:1 [1].","CH₂O [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q034"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":3,"text":"Calculate the molar concentration of a solution containing 0.120 mol of dissolved solute in 400 cm³ of solution.","scheme":["Convert 400 cm³ to 0.400 dm³ [1].","Apply C = n/V = 0.120/0.400 [1].","0.300 mol dm⁻³ [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q035"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"A gas reaction is 2CO(g) + O₂(g) → 2CO₂(g). What volume of oxygen reacts with 80 cm³ CO at the same T and P?","scheme":["Mole ratio CO:O₂ = 2:1 [1].","Gas volume ratio equals mole ratio under equal T and P [1].","40 cm³ O₂ [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q036"},{"topic":"S1.4","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":2,"text":"State the difference between relative formula mass Mᵣ and molar mass M.","scheme":["Mᵣ is relative and has no units [1].","M is mass per mole with units g mol⁻¹ [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q037"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which is an assumption of an ideal gas?","options":["Attractions dominate at all distances","Particles occupy substantial volumes","Particle collisions are elastic","Particles do not move"],"correct":2,"scheme":["The ideal gas model assumes elastic collisions."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q038"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"When do real gases generally deviate most from ideal behaviour?","options":["High T, low P","Low T, high P","High T, high P","Low T, low P"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Low T increases importance of attractions, high P increases finite-volume effects."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q039"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"At fixed T and fixed amount, pressure doubles. How does ideal gas volume change?","options":["Doubles","Halves","Quadruples","Stays constant"],"correct":1,"scheme":["PV is constant for a fixed n and T."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q040"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which SI pressure unit should be used with R in J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ in PV = nRT?","options":["atm","bar","Pa","mmHg"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Use pascal and cubic metres with the SI gas constant."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q041"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Explain why an ideal gas model becomes less accurate at low temperatures and high pressures.","scheme":["Intermolecular attractions become more significant at low temperatures [1].","Molecular volume is no longer negligible at high pressure [1].","Both violate assumptions of the ideal-gas model [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q042"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":4,"text":"Calculate the pressure of 0.200 mol of ideal gas at 300 K occupying 0.00500 m³. Use R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.","scheme":["PV = nRT [1].","Rearrange P = nRT/V [1].","Substitute 0.200 × 8.31 × 300 / 0.00500 [1].","9.97 × 10⁴ Pa [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q043"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"A fixed amount of ideal gas has volume 2.00 dm³ at 300 K. At the same pressure, determine volume at 450 K.","scheme":["V/T constant at fixed pressure and n [1].","V₂ = 2.00 × 450/300 [1].","3.00 dm³ [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q044"},{"topic":"S1.5","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":2,"text":"State two assumptions of an ideal gas model.","scheme":["Negligible particle volume [1].","No intermolecular forces / elastic collisions [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q045"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What forms when a metal atom loses an electron?","options":["An anion","A cation","An isotope","A molecule"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Electron loss produces a positively charged ion."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q046"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What is the formula of magnesium phosphate? (Mg²⁺; PO₄³⁻)","options":["MgPO₄","Mg₂(PO₄)₃","Mg₃(PO₄)₂","Mg₃PO₄"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Three Mg²⁺ and two PO₄³⁻ ions give total zero charge."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q047"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which formula represents nitrate?","options":["NO₂⁻","NO₃⁻","NH₄⁺","CO₃²⁻"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Nitrate is NO₃⁻."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q048"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Why can molten sodium chloride conduct electricity?","options":["Mobile electrons in the solid lattice","Mobile ions in the liquid","Its molecules are non-polar","The nuclei can move through the solid"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Mobile ions carry charge in the molten state."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q049"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Explain ionic bonding and why ionic solids have relatively high melting points.","scheme":["Ionic bonding is electrostatic attraction between opposite ions [1].","Ionic compounds have a giant three-dimensional lattice [1].","Significant energy is needed to overcome strong attractions [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q050"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":4,"text":"Explain electrical conductivity of an ionic compound as solid, molten and aqueous solution.","scheme":["Solid contains ions fixed in a lattice [1].","So the solid does not conduct [1].","Molten and aqueous forms contain mobile ions [1].","Mobile ions transport electrical charge [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q051"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Predict how increasing ionic charge and decreasing ionic radius affect lattice enthalpy magnitude.","scheme":["Greater ion charge leads to stronger electrostatic attraction [1].","Smaller ion radius allows ions to approach more closely [1].","Both generally increase lattice enthalpy magnitude / ionic bonding strength [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q052"},{"topic":"S2.1","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":2,"text":"Deduce the empirical formula of aluminium sulfate using Al³⁺ and SO₄²⁻.","scheme":["Charges balance with two Al³⁺ and three SO₄²⁻ [1].","Al₂(SO₄)₃ [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q053"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"A triple covalent bond contains how many shared electron pairs?","options":["One","Two","Three","Six"],"correct":2,"scheme":["A triple bond consists of three shared electron pairs."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q054"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which molecular shape is predicted for a central atom with four bonding domains and no lone pairs?","options":["Bent","Trigonal planar","Tetrahedral","Linear"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Four domains adopt a tetrahedral electron arrangement and molecular shape."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q055"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which statement correctly describes molecular polarity?","options":["Any polar bond makes the molecule polar","Shape is irrelevant","Bond dipoles can cancel depending on geometry","Only molecular mass matters"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Net dipole depends on the sum of bond dipoles and their directions."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q056"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which arrangement has three electron domains around a central atom?","options":["Linear","Trigonal planar","Tetrahedral","Octahedral"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Three electron domains have trigonal planar electron-domain geometry."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q057"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which interaction is generally strongest for comparable molar masses?","options":["London dispersion","Dipole-induced dipole","Dipole–dipole","Hydrogen bonding"],"correct":3,"scheme":["The syllabus gives hydrogen bonding as strongest among these under comparable molar masses."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q058"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"A chromatography spot travels 3.0 cm and the solvent front travels 7.5 cm. What is Rf?","options":["0.25","0.40","2.50","4.50"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Rf = 3.0 / 7.5 = 0.40."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q059"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which carbon allotrope has delocalized electrons within planar layers?","options":["Diamond","Graphite","Silicon dioxide","Silicon"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Graphite has delocalized electrons within its carbon layers."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q060"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"How many π bonds are in a carbon–carbon triple bond?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":2,"scheme":["A triple bond contains one σ and two π bonds."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q061"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"What is the electron-domain geometry of five domains around a central atom?","options":["Trigonal planar","Trigonal bipyramidal","Octahedral","Tetrahedral"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Five electron domains correspond to trigonal bipyramidal geometry."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q062"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which hybridization corresponds to two electron domains?","options":["sp","sp²","sp³","dsp³"],"correct":0,"scheme":["Two domains correspond to sp hybridization."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q063"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"A neutral atom has five valence electrons, one non-bonding pair and three single bonds. What is its formal charge?","options":["−1","0","+1","+2"],"correct":1,"scheme":["FC = 5 − 2 − 3 = 0."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q064"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1A","marks":1,"text":"Which observation supports electron delocalization in benzene?","options":["Alternating distinct long and short C–C bonds","Equal C–C bond lengths around the ring","Six isolated single bonds","Absence of any π electrons"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Equal ring C–C lengths are consistent with delocalized bonding."],"kind":"mcq","id":"Q065"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Easy","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Define a covalent bond and explain the octet rule.","scheme":["Electrostatic attraction between shared electron pair and positive nuclei [1].","Octet rule concerns achieving eight electrons in the valence shell [1].","Recognize the rule has exceptions such as incomplete octets [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q066"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"State the electron-domain geometry and molecular geometry of NH₃, and explain the difference.","scheme":["Four domains give tetrahedral electron-domain geometry [1].","Three bonds and one lone pair give trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry [1].","Molecular geometry describes atom positions, not lone-pair positions [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q067"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Explain why CO₂ has polar bonds but no overall permanent dipole moment.","scheme":["C=O bonds are polar because the atoms differ in electronegativity [1].","CO₂ is linear and symmetrical [1].","Equal bond dipoles cancel [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q068"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Compare the bonding and conductivity of diamond and graphite.","scheme":["Diamond is a three-dimensional network of covalently bonded carbon atoms [1].","Graphite has extended sheets/layers with delocalized electrons [1].","Graphite conducts along layers; diamond normally does not due to absent mobile charge carriers [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q069"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":4,"text":"Explain the relative boiling temperatures of two similarly sized molecules, one capable of hydrogen bonding and one with only dispersion forces.","scheme":["Identify hydrogen bonding as an intermolecular interaction [1].","Identify London dispersion in both molecules [1].","Hydrogen bonding is generally stronger under comparable molar masses [1].","More energy is required to separate molecules, leading to higher boiling temperature [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q070"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":3,"text":"In a chromatography experiment a spot moves 2.4 cm and the solvent front moves 6.0 cm. Calculate Rf and interpret a low Rf.","scheme":["Rf = distance spot / distance solvent front [1].","Rf = 0.40 [1].","Lower Rf reflects relatively stronger stationary-phase attraction (under the given conditions) [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q071"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Distinguish London dispersion, dipole–dipole and hydrogen bonding interactions.","scheme":["London dispersion results from temporary/induced dipoles and occurs in all molecules [1].","Dipole–dipole interactions act between permanent dipoles [1].","Hydrogen bonding is an attractive interaction involving H covalently attached to a strongly electronegative atom and a neighbouring electronegative atom [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q072"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"SL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Explain why a molecule with a central atom bonded to two atoms may be bent instead of linear.","scheme":["Count electron domains around the central atom [1].","Non-bonding electron pairs repel bonding pairs [1].","Lone pairs affect the positions of bonds and reduce their bond angle relative to the ideal arrangement [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q073"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Explain resonance in benzene using bonding and structural evidence.","scheme":["More than one localized double-bond arrangement can be drawn [1].","Actual π electrons are delocalized around the ring [1].","Equal C–C bond lengths support delocalization [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q074"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Determine the formal charge on oxygen in a Lewis structure with two lone pairs and a double bond.","scheme":["O has six valence electrons [1].","Four non-bonding electrons and four bonding electrons (half allocated) [1].","FC = 6 − 4 − 2 = 0 [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q075"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":4,"text":"Explain sigma and pi bonds in ethene, C₂H₄, and give the total number of each.","scheme":["A sigma bond involves head-on overlap [1].","A pi bond involves lateral p-orbital overlap [1].","Ethene contains five σ bonds (four C–H and one C–C) [1].","Ethene contains one π bond [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q076"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Hard","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Compare the hybridization and molecular geometry around each carbon in ethene and ethyne.","scheme":["Both carbon atoms in ethene are sp², trigonal planar [1].","Both carbon atoms in ethyne are sp, linear [1].","Relate differences to three versus two electron domains [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q077"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Hard","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"Determine the electron-domain geometry for a central atom with six domains, including two lone pairs, and explain why its molecular geometry differs.","scheme":["Six domains yield octahedral electron-domain geometry [1].","Molecular geometry depends on the positions of bonded atoms only [1].","Two lone pairs occupy domains but are excluded from the molecular-shape description [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q078"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Hard","paper":"Paper 1B","marks":3,"text":"An ion has two valid Lewis contributors with a double bond in alternative positions. Explain the implication for its bonding.","scheme":["Identify resonance contributors [1].","Identify delocalized electrons rather than a fixed single localized double bond [1].","Explain that atom positions stay unchanged while electron arrangement differs [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q079"},{"topic":"S2.2","level":"HL","difficulty":"Medium","paper":"Paper 2","marks":3,"text":"A nitrogen atom has five valence electrons, zero lone pairs and four single bonds in a Lewis structure. Determine its formal charge.","scheme":["FC = valence − nonbonding − ½ bonding electrons [1].","5 − 0 − 4 [1].","FC = +1 [1]."],"kind":"written","id":"Q080"},{"topic":"S2.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"Why does solid copper conduct electricity?","options":["Copper atoms move throughout the lattice","Delocalized electrons move through the metal","Copper ions leave the lattice as a current","Covalent molecules carry charge"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Metallic bonding contains mobile delocalized electrons that transport charge through the solid."],"id":"Q081"},{"topic":"S2.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Which comparison best explains the higher melting temperature of magnesium than sodium using a simple metallic-bonding model?","options":["Mg has more shells than Na","Mg contributes more delocalized electrons and has a greater cation charge","Na has stronger directional bonds","Mg consists of separate molecules"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Mg contributes two delocalized electrons per atom and forms more highly charged, relatively small metal cations, strengthening attraction to the electron sea."],"id":"Q082"},{"topic":"S2.4","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"Which structural change occurs when ethene forms poly(ethene)?","options":["The C=C double bond opens and new C–C links join monomers","Carbon atoms are removed as carbon dioxide","Water is released from every monomer","The monomer becomes an ionic lattice"],"correct":0,"scheme":["Addition polymerization uses the carbon–carbon double bond to connect monomer units; no small by-product is required."],"id":"Q083"},{"topic":"S2.4","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A diol reacts with a dicarboxylic acid to make a condensation polymer. Which linkage is formed?","options":["Amide, –CONH–","Ester, –COO–","Carbon–carbon triple bond","Ionic bond"],"correct":1,"scheme":["An alcohol group and carboxyl group react to make an ester linkage with elimination of a small molecule such as water."],"id":"Q084"},{"topic":"S3.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Which statement explains the decrease in atomic radius across period 3?","options":["An extra principal shell is added at every element","Nuclear charge increases while shielding by inner shells changes little","Electrons are removed from every atom","Protons are lost across the period"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Electrons are added to the same principal shell while increasing proton number strengthens attraction; the outer electron cloud contracts."],"id":"Q085"},{"topic":"S3.1","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A transition-metal complex appears blue in white light. What can be deduced?","options":["It emits only blue light through radioactive decay","It absorbs the complementary region of visible light","Its d orbitals must all be completely filled","Its colour is unrelated to electronic transitions"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Visible light absorbed can promote an electron between split d-orbital energies. Transmitted/reflected light has a complementary observed colour."],"id":"Q086"},{"topic":"S3.2","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"Which compound is a ketone?","options":["CH₃CH₂OH","CH₃CHO","CH₃COCH₃","CH₃COOH"],"correct":2,"scheme":["CH₃COCH₃ contains a carbonyl bonded to carbon groups on both sides; this is a ketone functional group."],"id":"Q087"},{"topic":"S3.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"An organic compound shows two proton NMR signals with integration ratio 3:2. Which structure is consistent?","options":["CH₃CH₂Br","CH₃OCH₃","CH₃CH₂CH₂Br","CH₃COCH₃"],"correct":0,"scheme":["CH₃CH₂Br has a three-H methyl environment and a two-H methylene environment. The other options have either one or three proton environments."],"id":"Q088"},{"topic":"R1.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"In an exothermic reaction carried out in an insulated cup, what is usually observed?","options":["The solution temperature increases","The solution temperature decreases","The reaction enthalpy is positive","Products have greater enthalpy than reactants"],"correct":0,"scheme":["An exothermic system transfers heat to its surroundings; the solution commonly warms and the reaction has negative ΔH."],"id":"Q089"},{"topic":"R1.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A solution of mass 100.0 g warms by 5.0 K. Using c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, what heat has the solution absorbed?","options":["20.9 J","209 J","2.09 kJ","20.9 kJ"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Q = mcΔT = 100.0 × 4.18 × 5.0 = 2090 J = 2.09 kJ."],"id":"Q090"},{"topic":"R1.2","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Bond enthalpies are H–H = 436, Cl–Cl = 243 and H–Cl = 431 kJ mol⁻¹. Estimate ΔH for H₂(g) + Cl₂(g) → 2HCl(g).","options":["−183 kJ mol⁻¹","+183 kJ mol⁻¹","−679 kJ mol⁻¹","+862 kJ mol⁻¹"],"correct":0,"scheme":["ΔH ≈ bonds broken − bonds formed = (436 + 243) − 2(431) = −183 kJ mol⁻¹."],"id":"Q091"},{"topic":"R1.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Which species has a standard enthalpy of formation of zero by definition at 298 K?","options":["O(g)","O₂(g) in its standard state","O₃(g)","H₂O(l)"],"correct":1,"scheme":["The standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its standard state is zero; oxygen’s standard state at 298 K is O₂(g)."],"id":"Q092"},{"topic":"R1.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"Which product is associated with incomplete combustion of a hydrocarbon and is toxic?","options":["Carbon monoxide","Only water","Sodium chloride","Hydrogen peroxide"],"correct":0,"scheme":["An insufficient oxygen supply can produce carbon monoxide, which is toxic; complete combustion would instead produce carbon dioxide and water."],"id":"Q093"},{"topic":"R1.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Which statement describes a hydrogen fuel cell during operation?","options":["It converts chemical energy directly into electrical energy","It stores all energy only as an increase in temperature","It always requires electrical energy to force its overall discharge reaction","Its fuel is regenerated indefinitely without input"],"correct":0,"scheme":["A fuel cell combines a continuously supplied fuel and oxidant in electrochemical reactions that convert chemical energy directly to electrical energy."],"id":"Q094"},{"topic":"R1.4","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"For a reaction with ΔH° = +40 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS° = +100 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, at what temperatures is ΔG° negative, assuming both quantities are temperature independent?","options":["Below 40 K","Below 400 K","Above 400 K","At all temperatures"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Convert ΔS° to 0.100 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. ΔG° = 40 − 0.100T is negative for T > 400 K."],"id":"Q095"},{"topic":"R1.4","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"If K > 1 at a specified temperature, what is the sign of ΔG° for the reaction as written?","options":["Positive","Negative","Exactly zero for every K","Cannot be related to K"],"correct":1,"scheme":["ΔG° = −RT ln K. When K > 1, ln K is positive and ΔG° is negative."],"id":"Q096"},{"topic":"R2.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"For 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g), 3.0 mol H₂ and 1.0 mol O₂ are mixed. What is the maximum amount of H₂O?","options":["1.0 mol","2.0 mol","3.0 mol","4.0 mol"],"correct":1,"scheme":["O₂ is limiting: 1.0 mol O₂ consumes 2.0 mol H₂ and makes 2.0 mol H₂O; 1.0 mol H₂ remains."],"id":"Q097"},{"topic":"R2.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"An experiment produces 7.2 g of product from a theoretical yield of 9.0 g. What is the percentage yield?","options":["20%","72%","80%","125%"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Percentage yield = actual/theoretical × 100 = 7.2/9.0 × 100 = 80%."],"id":"Q098"},{"topic":"R2.2","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"How does a catalyst increase reaction rate?","options":["It raises the temperature of every reaction","It provides a pathway with lower activation energy","It increases the enthalpy difference between products and reactants","It shifts every equilibrium to the products"],"correct":1,"scheme":["A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy so a greater fraction of collisions can lead to reaction; ΔH and equilibrium position are unchanged."],"id":"Q099"},{"topic":"R2.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"For rate = k[A]²[B], what happens to the rate when [A] doubles and [B] halves at constant temperature?","options":["It halves","It stays the same","It doubles","It increases eightfold"],"correct":2,"scheme":["The factor is 2² × 1/2 = 2. The rate doubles, while k remains constant at unchanged temperature."],"id":"Q100"},{"topic":"R2.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"What is true at dynamic chemical equilibrium in a closed system?","options":["Reactants and products always have equal concentrations","Both forward and reverse reactions stop","The forward and reverse reaction rates are equal","Every equilibrium constant equals one"],"correct":2,"scheme":["At dynamic equilibrium both reactions continue at equal rates; concentrations are constant but not necessarily equal."],"id":"Q101"},{"topic":"R2.3","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"For a reaction at fixed temperature, Q = 0.20 and K = 4.0. Which direction is favoured as equilibrium is approached?","options":["Forward, making more products","Reverse, making more reactants","No net change because Q is non-zero","Neither; Q cannot be compared with K"],"correct":0,"scheme":["Q < K, so net forward reaction increases the product-to-reactant ratio until Q reaches K."],"id":"Q102"},{"topic":"R3.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"What is the pH of a solution with [H⁺] = 2.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³?","options":["2.0","2.70","3.30","11.30"],"correct":1,"scheme":["pH = −log₁₀(2.0 × 10⁻³) = 2.70 to two decimal places."],"id":"Q103"},{"topic":"R3.1","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A buffer contains a weak acid HA and its conjugate base A⁻ at equal concentrations. What is the relationship between pH and pKₐ?","options":["pH = 0","pH = 7 under all conditions","pH = pKₐ","pH = 2pKₐ"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Kₐ = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA]. When [A⁻] = [HA], [H⁺] = Kₐ, so pH = pKₐ."],"id":"Q104"},{"topic":"R3.2","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"In a voltaic cell, where does oxidation occur?","options":["At the positive cathode","At the negative anode","At both electrodes simultaneously by definition","Only in the salt bridge"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Oxidation occurs at the anode. In a voltaic cell the anode supplies electrons and is the negative electrode."],"id":"Q105"},{"topic":"R3.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Standard reduction potentials are E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V. What is E°cell for the spontaneous zinc–copper cell?","options":["−1.10 V","−0.42 V","+0.42 V","+1.10 V"],"correct":3,"scheme":["Cu²⁺ is reduced at the cathode and Zn is oxidized at the anode. E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V."],"id":"Q106"},{"topic":"R3.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"Which process produces chlorine radicals from Cl₂ under UV light?","options":["Heterolytic fission with both electrons on one atom","Homolytic fission with one electron on each atom","Proton transfer","Condensation polymerization"],"correct":1,"scheme":["Homolytic fission gives one bonding electron to each chlorine atom: Cl₂ → 2Cl•."],"id":"Q107"},{"topic":"R3.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Which equation is a termination step in methane chlorination?","options":["Cl₂ → 2Cl•","Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + CH₃•","CH₃• + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•","CH₃• + Cl• → CH₃Cl"],"correct":3,"scheme":["Termination combines two radicals to form a non-radical product without regenerating another radical."],"id":"Q108"},{"topic":"R3.4","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Easy","text":"Which statement defines a nucleophile?","options":["It donates a pair of electrons to form a bond","It must always have a positive charge","It accepts a proton in every possible reaction","It contains no lone pairs or π electrons"],"correct":0,"scheme":["A nucleophile donates both electrons of a newly formed bond. It can be neutral or negatively charged."],"id":"Q109"},{"topic":"R3.4","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1A","kind":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Which rate equation is consistent with a one-step SN2 reaction between a primary halogenoalkane RBr and OH⁻?","options":["rate = k[RBr]","rate = k[OH⁻] only","rate = k[RBr][OH⁻]","rate = k[RBr]²[OH⁻]"],"correct":2,"scheme":["Both the halogenoalkane and nucleophile participate in the concerted bimolecular substitution step; the rate is first order in each."],"id":"Q110"},{"topic":"S2.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Explain why a pure metal can be both malleable and electrically conducting.","scheme":["Metal cations are surrounded by delocalized electrons in a metallic lattice [1].","The electrons are mobile and carry electrical charge [1].","Layers can change position while non-directional attraction between cations and electrons is maintained, allowing deformation [1]."],"id":"Q111"},{"topic":"S2.4","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Draw or describe the repeating unit of the addition polymer formed from CH₂=CHCl. Explain why its formation can have 100% atom economy.","scheme":["Open the C=C double bond to give the backbone repeating unit –CH₂–CHCl– [1].","Show the repeating unit in brackets with bonds continuing through the brackets and subscript n [1].","All monomer atoms are incorporated in the polymer; no small-molecule by-product is required [1]."],"id":"Q112"},{"topic":"S2.4","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A polyester is made from HO–CH₂–CH₂–OH and HOOC–CH₂–COOH. Describe its repeat linkage and explain the small molecule released.","scheme":["The alcohol groups of the diol react with carboxyl groups of the dicarboxylic acid [1].","An ester linkage –C(=O)–O– forms between monomer residues [1].","A valid repeat unit is –O–CH₂–CH₂–O–C(=O)–CH₂–C(=O)– with continuous backbone bonds [1].","Water is eliminated when an –OH and H combine during formation of each ester linkage [1]."],"id":"Q113"},{"topic":"S3.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Predict and explain the reaction when chlorine is added to aqueous potassium bromide. Include an ionic equation.","scheme":["Chlorine oxidizes bromide ions to bromine because chlorine is the stronger oxidizing halogen [1].","Cl₂(aq) + 2Br⁻(aq) → 2Cl⁻(aq) + Br₂(aq) [1].","An orange/brown colour from bromine develops; Br⁻ loses electrons while chlorine gains electrons [1]."],"id":"Q114"},{"topic":"S3.2","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Give the IUPAC name of CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ and explain why it is classified as a secondary alcohol.","scheme":["The longest chain contains four carbons: butane parent [1].","The hydroxyl group receives the lowest possible locant, giving butan-2-ol [1].","The carbon bearing –OH is bonded to two other carbon atoms, so the alcohol is secondary [1]."],"id":"Q115"},{"topic":"S3.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1B","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Hard","text":"A compound has molecular formula C₂H₄O₂. Its IR spectrum has a broad absorption spanning approximately 2500–3300 cm⁻¹ and a strong carbonyl absorption. Its ¹H NMR contains a 3H singlet and a 1H downfield signal. Deduce a structure and justify it.","scheme":["The formula and carbonyl absorption support a carbonyl-containing molecule [1].","The very broad O–H absorption is consistent with a carboxylic acid group [1].","A 3H singlet is consistent with CH₃ next to a carbonyl carbon with no adjacent hydrogen [1].","CH₃COOH (ethanoic acid) satisfies the molecular formula and gives a separate acidic O–H proton [1]."],"id":"Q116"},{"topic":"R1.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1B","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A reaction warms 150.0 g of solution from 20.0 °C to 27.0 °C. A total of 0.0500 mol of limiting reactant reacts. Use c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ and ignore calorimeter heat capacity and heat loss. Calculate the molar reaction enthalpy.","scheme":["ΔT = 27.0 − 20.0 = 7.0 K [1].","Qsolution = mcΔT = 150.0 × 4.18 × 7.0 = 4389 J [1].","Heat released by the reaction is −4.389 kJ [1].","ΔH = −4.389/0.0500 = −87.8 kJ mol⁻¹ (approximately −88 kJ mol⁻¹ to two significant figures) [1]."],"id":"Q117"},{"topic":"R1.2","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Given A → B has ΔH = +45 kJ mol⁻¹ and B → C has ΔH = −120 kJ mol⁻¹, determine ΔH for C → A. Explain the principle used.","scheme":["For A → C, add the two steps: +45 − 120 = −75 kJ mol⁻¹ [1].","Reverse the process: C → A has ΔH = +75 kJ mol⁻¹ [1].","Hess’s law: enthalpy change depends only on initial and final states, not the pathway [1]."],"id":"Q118"},{"topic":"R1.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Hard","text":"For CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), calculate ΔH° using ΔHf°(CH₄) = −74.8, ΔHf°(CO₂) = −393.5 and ΔHf°(H₂O(l)) = −285.8 kJ mol⁻¹.","scheme":["Use ΔH° = ΣνΔHf°(products) − ΣνΔHf°(reactants) [1].","ΔHf° for O₂(g), the standard-state element, is zero [1].","Substitute: [−393.5 + 2(−285.8)] − [−74.8 + 2(0)] [1].","ΔH° = −890.3 kJ mol⁻¹ [1]."],"id":"Q119"},{"topic":"R1.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Write balanced equations for complete combustion of methane and incomplete combustion forming carbon monoxide. Explain why limited oxygen poses an additional health risk.","scheme":["Complete combustion: CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O [1].","Incomplete combustion to CO: 2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O [1].","Carbon monoxide is toxic; incomplete combustion can therefore create a serious additional health hazard [1]."],"id":"Q120"},{"topic":"R1.4","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A reaction has ΔH° = −50.0 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS° = −120 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Calculate ΔG° at 298 K and determine the temperature range for thermodynamic spontaneity under standard conditions, assuming ΔH° and ΔS° are constant.","scheme":["Convert ΔS° to −0.120 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ [1].","ΔG° = ΔH° − TΔS° = −50.0 − 298(−0.120) = −14.24 kJ mol⁻¹ [1].","Set ΔG° = 0: T = ΔH°/ΔS° = (−50.0)/(−0.120) ≈ 417 K [1].","ΔG° < 0 for T < 417 K; spontaneity does not imply a rapid reaction [1]."],"id":"Q121"},{"topic":"R2.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Calcium carbonate decomposes: CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g). Using molar masses CaCO₃ = 100.09 and CaO = 56.08 g mol⁻¹, calculate the theoretical mass of CaO from 10.00 g CaCO₃ and the percentage yield if 4.80 g CaO is recovered.","scheme":["n(CaCO₃) = 10.00/100.09 ≈ 0.09991 mol [1].","The 1:1 ratio gives 0.09991 mol CaO [1].","Theoretical mass = 0.09991 × 56.08 ≈ 5.603 g [1].","Percentage yield = 4.80/5.603 × 100 ≈ 85.7% [1]."],"id":"Q122"},{"topic":"R2.2","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1B","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"A tangent to a gas-volume/time curve passes through (10 s, 20 cm³) and (30 s, 50 cm³). Estimate the instantaneous rate represented by this tangent and explain why it is not necessarily the average rate for the whole reaction.","scheme":["Calculate tangent gradient ΔV/Δt = (50 − 20)/(30 − 10) [1].","Rate = 1.5 cm³ s⁻¹ [1].","A tangent estimates the rate at one chosen time; a whole-reaction average uses the overall change and elapsed time [1]."],"id":"Q123"},{"topic":"R2.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 1B","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Hard","text":"At constant temperature, doubling [A] with [B] fixed multiplies the rate by four; doubling [B] with [A] fixed has no effect. When [A] = 0.200 mol dm⁻³, the rate is 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. Deduce the rate equation and calculate k with units.","scheme":["The reaction is second order in A because doubling [A] gives a fourfold rate [1].","It is zero order in B, so rate = k[A]² [1].","k = (8.0 × 10⁻⁴)/(0.200)² = 2.0 × 10⁻² [1].","Units of k are dm³ mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ [1]."],"id":"Q124"},{"topic":"R2.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), the forward reaction is exothermic. Predict the effects of increasing pressure, increasing temperature and adding a catalyst on the equilibrium amount of NH₃. State which change alters K.","scheme":["Increasing pressure at constant temperature favours the side with fewer gas particles, so increases the equilibrium amount of NH₃ [1].","Increasing temperature favours the endothermic reverse direction, so decreases NH₃ [1].","A catalyst changes neither equilibrium composition nor K; it accelerates attainment of equilibrium [1].","Only temperature changes K here; increasing T decreases K for the exothermic forward reaction [1]."],"id":"Q125"},{"topic":"R3.1","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 1B","kind":"written","marks":3,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"25.00 cm³ of hydrochloric acid is neutralized by 20.00 cm³ of 0.1500 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. Calculate the HCl concentration.","scheme":["HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O gives a 1:1 mole ratio [1].","n(NaOH) = 0.1500 × 0.02000 = 0.003000 mol = n(HCl) [1].","[HCl] = 0.003000/0.02500 = 0.1200 mol dm⁻³ [1]."],"id":"Q126"},{"topic":"R3.1","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Hard","text":"A buffer contains 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid and 0.150 mol dm⁻³ ethanoate ions. Given Kₐ = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵, estimate its pH and explain what happens to added small amounts of H⁺.","scheme":["Kₐ = [H⁺][CH₃COO⁻]/[CH₃COOH] [1].","[H⁺] ≈ (1.8 × 10⁻⁵)(0.100/0.150) = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ [1].","pH ≈ 4.92 [1].","Ethanoate removes added H⁺: CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃COOH, limiting the pH change [1]."],"id":"Q127"},{"topic":"R3.2","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Hard","text":"A spontaneous voltaic cell has E°cell = +1.10 V and transfers 2 mol of electrons per mole of reaction. Calculate ΔG° using F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹ and explain the sign.","scheme":["Use ΔG° = −nFE°cell [1].","Substitute −2 × 96,500 × 1.10 = −212,300 J mol⁻¹ [1].","ΔG° = −212 kJ mol⁻¹ to three significant figures [1].","A negative standard Gibbs energy change is consistent with thermodynamic spontaneity under standard conditions [1]."],"id":"Q128"},{"topic":"R3.3","level":"SL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Medium","text":"Write the initiation step and two propagation steps in the UV-initiated chlorination of methane, then give one termination step.","scheme":["Initiation: Cl₂ → 2Cl• under UV light [1].","Propagation: Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + CH₃• [1].","Propagation: CH₃• + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl• [1].","A valid termination such as Cl• + CH₃• → CH₃Cl or 2Cl• → Cl₂ or 2CH₃• → C₂H₆ [1]."],"id":"Q129"},{"topic":"R3.4","level":"HL","paper":"Paper 2","kind":"written","marks":4,"difficulty":"Hard","text":"Predict the major product when propene reacts with HBr and explain the carbocation argument. Assume ordinary electrophilic addition conditions.","scheme":["The major product is 2-bromopropane, CH₃CHBrCH₃ [1].","The π bond initially reacts with H⁺, and protonation can give two possible carbocations [1].","A secondary carbocation, CH₃CH⁺CH₃, is more stable than the alternative primary carbocation [1].","Br⁻ attacks the secondary carbocation, producing the major product [1]."],"id":"Q130"}];
